o—toß.C., New ton-le-Wil- -I,ows ; L. K. Shaw's Refuge. Strangeways, Manchester ; death to our gracious lord and king., George the i salford Good. Samaritan Society, Girl's Evening Home, T W 'Third—God bless and protect him I retched man, Slford ;* Wads aed Strays (Seaforth and Bode Rouse) 1 p, ; how could you do it ?' I ndlebury Hospital, Pendleburv, Swinton, Wigan In- "I never did, your honour, 0 !—I never did i tirmary,children's ward; Wigan (St. Thomas,St. James's, threaten his Majesty in any way !" st. Patrick, Sr. Andrew's, and St. Catherine's), St. "Ale !—Beware of falsehood 1 Did you not dis- Helens Providence Hospital, St. John's Tue Brook, tinctly, and even angrily, cry out; 'Hang the Wigan Deaf and Dumb Asylum. Padgate, Penketh and aing f'" I Sankey. Prescot, • Liverpool Reformatory Ship Akbar, "Aye, your honour—l did that, I confess; but Working Ladies' Guild, London, Warrington Wesleyans, it was not good King George, at all, that I cried and Doe nall Green. out against--but the king of clubs !" I — "The king of clubs 1" repeated the justice, in amaze. "Miserable caitiff! would you add insult to treason? Is it thus you speak of your lawful Sovereign?" "Indeed, your honour, you mistake me entirely," pleaded the man, wringing his hands. "'Let me tell you how it was: It was yester-e'en, and Tom Bowman and mesel, wi' two more, were playin' a ame of whist, and we were both sides in the seine- poles—he was nine, and I was nine; and the struggle came for the odd trick, don't you see ?—I held the ace and queen o' trumps in me own hand —which was clubs,—and had captured a trick with the ace, an' then led the queen, when Torn Bow- man, the rascal! clapped down the king, and took the trick and won the game! and then, yer honour I cried out, 'Hang the king I ' " The justice tried to smile, but his dignity would not allow him. However, he put away the awful ` frown, and said, with a majestic wave of the hand: " Ah, if that was all, thou mayst depart, for now, in peace; bet, beware ! Let it not happen again !" " By the blessin' o' the saints, I never will," returned the grateful tailor, with an honest burst of enthusiasm. "Aye, an' I'll never e'en say, hang to the knave, lest I should offend your wor- ship I" And the man had departed before the ponderous judge could fully consider the spirit and intent of this last speech.</p>
        <p>TIT- BITS.</p>
        <p>WHY is trouble always brewing! Why no% baking, butchering, washing, or doing something else besides this everlasting brewing? "This room is very close," remarked a guest to. the head waiter of a New York Broadway restaurant; "can't I have a little fresh air ? " The Well-drilled automaton raised his voice to a high pitch. "One air!" he yelled, after a pause adding, "Let it ht.. fresh I" Ir we were to choose the most appropriate symbol of the fleeting, the evanescent, the perish- able, the decaying, the here-to-day-and-gone-to- morrow, perhaps it would be a pair of boy's boots. A SCOTTISH etergyinan (le., Li contention in a house, and asked,' Who is the head here?" The man said : " Sit down a little; we're just trying to settle that point." Mots: Do you believe there is such a thing as a haunted house Wicks: "Oh yes Indeed; but it depends a great deal upon how good-looking the gin i is who lives in it." A TRAVELLER, recently describing a tropical shower, wrote to a friend in the following words: "The rain-drops were extremely large, varying in size from a shilli”g to eighteenpence. "</p>
