        <head>WRONG SUBJECT.</head>
        <p>THE WRONG SUBJECT.</p>
        <p>"ILANG-T-HE--lalkl- -6,--171</p>
        <p>AT THE PHILHAICSIONIC.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, I think the round waist with a belt will be much worn during the au t umn and winter, and the seamless variety of it is especially suitable for the loosely woven woollens. For stout persons these . waists are made almost like a very short basque, and the belt is 03 the outside, giving a slightly longer-waisted effect then when the belt follows the waist line. Fashion, however, no longer demands so long a waist as En t been sought after for some time past, and the short-waisted person need no more be tempted to disfigure herself by making a waist.line where there is none.</p>
        <p>When the weather.refuses to Allem us to con our autumn clothing, and we y tarn after some new attire, the easiest way of satisfyiug that desire is by the purchase of a new hat or bonnet. No matter what the temperature, we doff our straw and take to felt, and, crowned with novelty, how can anyone feel demode / A very pretty bonnet is ef wallflower velvet, covered with is steel net edged with balls It has a large bow at the back of blach satin, with black feather tuft with osprey in the centre. ' •As the colder weather approaches, capes and capelets appear as part of the bodices, but in the bodices thermelves there is little new. Flat Victoria berthas, generally of velvet, are worn on high bodices, where they occupy the same position as they do on the low bodice—that is to outline the bust. Combinations of the new thin "muslin" velvets will be lovely, bu,, expensive; a dress of this kind will embody three shades of brown—for instance, very light "taboo," golden brown, and fur of a darker brown tone. 13Aeques will be much used ; even on very smart evening dresses, and the short double flounce put on with the waistbelt is now elongated into two deep points in front, or " peplum " fashion with the points appearing at each side. This style of hasque is accordeon or knife-pleated, or in a thin material it, can be gathered. A tasteful visiting costume is in pale pink taffeta, adorned with front, long sleeves, and band round the skirt in plaid ribbon, checked pink and black. The short puffed sleeves are edged with a frill of embroidered net in keeping with the fan. shaped pleatings, which fall on each side of the bodice from folded shoulder straps. Belt striped horizontally with narrow black dribbon ; velvet band round the throat to matcff. A deep lace flounce boarders the skirt, and is exchanged with three cross bands of taffeta. Capote in yellow paillason straw, in the centre of which a blackbird spreads its wings and tail, glittering with jet ; on ach side are tufts of block end cherry coloured wee. intermingled with black feathers.</p>
        <p>I went to bear that music, in order, if it might be, to rid my mind of an absorbing, irritating, personal annoyance, which I knew it was unworthy to ponder over, having graver and nobler anxieties. but which I could not for the 'life of me, dis- miss from my thoughts for more than a few mom- ents. The ignoble worry—for it deserved no other name—perpetually reappeared, more exasperating and More persistent after each enforced banishment from any mind, and as the French say of le naturel— every time I chased it away, il revenait au galop. It chanced that the piece was a quintette by Mendels- sotm and one which was new to me. It opened with a long:drawn sotto voce sigh for the violoncello, instantly responded to by a rapid, irritable, indignant little phrase from the first violin, which plainly exclaimed " God bless my soul, how is it possible you don't under. stand !" This roused the other four instruments, all of whom endeavoured to soothe and explain,and matters appeared to be getting less agitating when the viola—who, to speak the truth, had merely been repeating, without much earnestness and in a sort of mechanical way, the explanations of the others—suddenly caught up the first violin's point of view, and exclaimed : 0 God bless my soul, how is it possible you don't understand !" This pro- duced a general protest : "But we do understand ! Don't you hear what we say? Why don't you listen !" etc., etc. ; to which the first violin added : Why that's precisely what I say ! " God bless my soul, hew is it possible YOU don't understand !" The viola was somewhat confused at this, and declared that the violin had no occasion to be angry, for that, after all, she was supporting his views of the case ; and the two continued for a short time agreeing very amic- ably together, mumuring "just so," " exactly," " ef • course," in quite friendly fashion, when the second violin burst out indignantly in his turn, scornfully remarking that it was all very well to go on flattering onn another in that way (" They always do !" put in the double-bass), but the real fact of the matter was, that not a single one of them really understood. " Not understand r shouted the first violin and viola together, " why we understood from the very first that " And so did I," geld the double-bass, "I saw at the beginning that-- ." But here the second violin grew quite desperate, and fairly shrieked out " God bless my soul, how is it possible you don't understand !" To describe the wry with which all turned upon the second violin at this, would be impossible. The violon- cello tried hard to interpose, and even declared, with some asperity, that matters need never have gone so far if, instead ot interrupting him at the very first word he , uttered.they had only heard what he had to say; he even made what seemed to be an attempt to say it, sufficiently loud to be heard above the clamour of the others, but in vain ; and his voice sank at last into a monotonous, grumbling protest, which he kept up until the other four, who with ever-increasing violence, continued asking each other, all at once • " God bless my soul, how is it possible you do not understand!" suddenly came to an abrupt close, evidently from sheer exhaustion and want of breath.</p>
        <p>A charming walking costume is made from one of the new woollen materials, and trimmed with braid. The trimming on the shirt consists of a broad braid, edged top and bottom with a narrow one; jacket is trimmed with narrow braiding, and cults, sash; and vest are of surah. Ermine still plays its part on capes made in the Russian circular shape, of red velvet. A three- quarter length sealskin coat is tight-fitting, with natural blue fox outlining the yoke and forming the collar and cuffs; this blue fox is a compare.. tively new combination with sealskin, and It may be written down as very effective. Sealskin forms a full, short cape, which only reaches to the waist, with skunk edging the shoulder collar, while the cape itself is lined throughout with very handsome brocade. A capital driving coat is made in blue, tight-fitting, with large turned-down collar of blue fox, blue fox being in high favour. Blouses still continue fashionable. The new form is made rather full in front, with a band and all•round basque intended to be worn outside, nob inside the dress, the trimmidg consisting of hori- zontal bands of white or black lace insertion lined with a colour. There are generally three such rows on the front of each blouse. Some are embroidered in black or white, and a good theatre bodice which comes under the head of blouse can be bought elaborately trimmed with cream lace, which covers the shoulders in frills, or radiates from the neck in rows of insertion, or peeps in between the puffings of the sleeve. An evening toilette, suitable for young girls, is made in accordeon-pleated nun's veiling, crape, or cashmere. It is made in the Empire style, and has a full silk sleeve.</p>
        <p>Moiri, antique is evidently to be the fashionable material for trimmin • gowns and coats. It can be seen on Pearly every model. The handsomest coat I have seen this season was of black moire antique, with a pelerine of black velvet bordered with black tips. Plain, shot, striped, and figured moir6 silks will be worn for evening gowns. Toe season's sup- ply of shot silks are more delicate in colouring this season. One of the loveliest of the moir6 brocades had a shot pale blue and gold ground colour covered with n Hautil trellis-work pattern in gold. The same design is prourable in ever so many other combinations. Another example of moire antique showed a festooned pattern of flowers. A third, with a pink tnd green shot ground, had the pretty ribbon ffct which you see in the Louis brocades, and small black spots. Yours lovingly, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Districts.</p>
        <p>Mdme. Patti has a parrot which, according: to the " South Wales Post," swears in Portuguese from morn- ing till night. Miss Susanna Beecer, , of Coniston, one of the most in- timate of Mr. Ruskin's small circle of friends there, died a few days ago. She was 87, and had spent nearly all her life at Coniston, in works of charity _among the dales-</p>
        <p>A moment of silence ensued, and the violoncello then repeated his first sigh, more softly and still more sadly that before, and as none of the others had energy left to quarrel with him, remained mournfully master of the situation.</p>
