        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1893. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>An heiress directory has been published by a St. Louis journalist, giving the names, ages, and incomes of the girls of the place, together with such descriptions of their appearance as would be likely to interest gentlemen on the look-out for wives. Three brave women in New York—Mrs. Celia Raynor, Mrs. Renie Southard, and Miss Jennie Rhodes—have been presented with gold medals from the Maritime Association for heroism exhibited in aiding in the rescue of the shipwrecked crew of the barque Martha I'. Tucker. . . . .</p>
        <p>Although Lady Florence Dixie states that she was offered the sum of £135,000 to establish a morning half- penny newspaper for women, and that she declined the offer on account of the conditions laid down, I under- stand that she is about to start a paper on her own account.—" Pearson's Search Light." Mrs. Gladstone is a typical English old lady,with a fine kindly round face, and a slight but suitable embonpoint Her one ideal is her husband. She lives for him, thinks of him, and watches his every movement. To hear her make a speech at a bazaar or women's meeting is a treat. She is fluent, speaks slowly, and articulates well. From an article in the" Times " on the hunting season it seems that there are two ladies who are " masters " of harriers. One is H.R.H. the Comtesse de Paris, who hunts the country round Stowe; the other is Mrs. Cheape, who hunts the Bentley Harriers in Essex. Lady Veen Hastings has given up her" mastership " of a pack in Ireland—perhaps because she is herself about to get a" master "in the person of young Mr. Campbell of Stonefield—and her brother, the Earl of Huntingdon,has taken her place. " Think of me," says Miss Willard to her American temperance friends, " as living quietly, 20 miles from London, in Lady Henry's cottage home, which she pre- fers to any of her residences, where, with Annie Gordon and my loyal helpers, I am able to do a large amount of correspondence and composition ; so that I need be by no means counted out from among the active forces of our movement. I have every reason to believe from what Sir Benjamin Ward Richardson and those in council with him told me, that by next spring I shall be able to go back to my beloved native country ready to work." A society young woman on the ether side of the big pond has devised a novel entertainment, which is shortly to be made public. It is to be a " reception for people who can't sleep at night." Among her friends, she says, are a great many people who are troubled with insomnia and who contess that they spend many frightful waking hours walking the floor, looking out of the window, rocking in easy-chairs, trying to read or write, and in other useless and tiresome occupations. When her plans are fully matured this original young woman intends, on at least two nights in every week, to be "at home " to those distressed female friends from midnight until morning.</p>
