      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>About Shoes.</head>
        <p>About Shoes.</p>
        <p>Everyone likes to see a nice-fitting shoe, but not every one knows what constitutes a nice fit. An ill-futing shoe is much more bkely to produce corns and bunions and other diseases to which the human foot is heir than a tight-fitting one. One which is too loose, allowing the foot to slip about, is as bad as can be, but a shoe which is loose enough in the foot for comfort, if it he properly fitted at the ankle, may not be productive of discomfort; and this, a professional shoe-fitter tells me, is a point which should not be overlooked. When the shoe is first buttoned, the three or four but- tons which come at the centre or smaller part of the ankle should be tight enough to he almost uncomfortable In a day or two they will have worn themselves loose enough for comfort and still hold the shoe a snug fit. For this reason it is wisdom not to have the buttons permanently fastened to the she as they may need to be tightened at this place from time to time as the shoe stretches.</p>
        <p>If the shoe is held as tightly as it should be at the ankle the foot cannot press into the toe of the shoe and produce corns and enlarged joints on the toes, and it cannot slide from the heel, and cause the shoe to break across the back seam above the stiffening. Another point which should be carefully looked after is the hol- low under the foot. If the shoe is not cut hollow enough to fit your foot, there will he wrinkles from the instep to the sole towards the back and front. If it is cut too hollow the shoe will draw uncomfortably over the foot. A high heeled shoe is productive of corns, throwing the I foot too far forward into the shoe. The appearance of a man's shoe (and likewise a woman's) is said to be an indication of his character. If a man's shoe be run down at the heel his business may be, and a careful per- son never neglects to have the heels of his shoes polished as carefully as the toes. A tan shoe if it becomes scratched may be redressed with the patent dressing sold for that purpose, but it requires more careful wearing than a black shoe. It is an easy matter to remove unsightly marks by rubbing with a little vaseline. A banana is said to be effective if used in the same way. Tan shoes when old are much more unsightly than black ones. Low shois will in time, if worn regularly, weaken the ankles, but are munh mere comfortable for hot weather than high ones. White canvas and kid shoes may be cleaned with Fuller's earth.</p>
        <p>Soups. There is a number of methods of making soup stocks, and no two will give exactly the same results. One of the simplest and most satisfactory is that of clear stock or bouillon. By this the best flavour of the meat is ob- tained, for none passes off in steam, as when the meat is boiled rapidly. The second -mode is in boiling the stock a great deal to reduce it. This gives a very rich soup with a marked difference in the flavour from that made with clear meat kept in water at the boiling point. The third way leaves a mixed stock, which will not be clear unless whites of eggs be used. In following the first method we buy clear beef speci- ally for the stock, and know from the beginning just how much stock there will be when the work is com- pleted. By the second metlisd we are not sure, because more or less than we estimate may boil away. The third stock being made from bones and pieces of meat left from roasts and from the trimmings of raw meats, will always be changeable in colour, quantity and quality. This is, however, a very important stock, and it should always be kept on hand. No household, evea where only a moderate amount of meat is used, should be without a stock-pot. It can be kept on the back of the range or stove while cooking is going on. Two or three times a week it should be put on with the trim- mings and bsnes left from cooked and uncooked meats. This practice will give a supply of stick at all times, which will be of the greatest value in making sauces, side dishes, and soups. Meat, if only slightly tainted, will spoil a stock • therefore, or at care must be taken that every particle is perfectly sweet. Vegetables make a stock sour very quickly, so if you wish to keep a stock do not use them. Many rules advise putting vegetables into the stockpot with the meat and water and cooking from the very beginning. When this is done they absorb the fine flavour of the meat, and give the soup a rank taste. They should cook not more than an hour—the last hour—in the stock. A white stock is made with veal or poultry. The water ia which a leg of mutton or fowl has been boiled makes a good stock for soups and gravies. A soup stock most be cooled quickly or it will not keep well. In winter any kind of stock ought to keep good a week. That boiled down to a jelly will last the longest. In toe warm months three days will be the average time stock will keep.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>smoothed back the long, lovely dark -durls that strayed over the pillow, and caressed the girt's little burning bands. "Why, any one can see she has not bees the same since Master Rutledge announced that he was going abroad. But, then, men are proverbially blind.' An hour later Uldene had grown so much worse that a doctor was summoned in all haste. Wee ~ 4 it chance, or the strange oomplication of fate most cruet?—the physician called in was young Doctor 4 Keith, the. same physician who played such prominent part in one of our previous ehapters. ' Dr. Keith looked gravely at the beautiful patient he had been called to attend. ~ "She is sliffering from nervous prostration, and an incense mental shock," he said. "If it turns to bran fever between this and midnight, saving , her will be something less than a miracle." This was the startling word Mrs. Pierce carried' down to Rutledge Chester in the library, and which caused him to send the following telegram flashing southward over the wires to Mark 4 Sefton:, "Can you come on at once? Uldene is very ill. In Mrs. Chester's hurried and unexpected depar- ture, she had quite forgotten to mention her dead- nation, aud, therefore, at this critical hour she could not be recalled. The matter was all the more alarming when the young doctor called Rutledge Chester to Uldene's bedside an hour later, asking that the doctors be called in for the purpose of consultation. "I have done all in my power for her," he said, compa,sionately. "I should like to have the opinions of other physicians as a last resort." Three skilful, prominent physicians were sum- moned without delay. Each promptly concurred in the opinion of young Dr. Keith. There was an acute mental trouble praying upon the heart of the lovely, hapless patient. Her strange symptoms baffled and puzzled t. ern, skilful as they were. She was sinking—dying before their eyes • human skill seemed of little avail ; all their efforts to arrest the scythe of the dread destroyer, Death, seemed futile. It was their opiniee that when the tido drifted out at midnight the t.fe of beautiful Uldene would drift out with it. The doctors, together with Rutledge Chester and Mrs. Pierce, the housekeeper, sat by the couch, awaiting in the solemn hush the pitiful end. Outside the door the servants knelt, weeping and wailing. The ding girl, who had brought sun- shine and joy to the quiet old mansion, had been dearly beloved by one and all. Now they were kneeling, praying outside the door awaiting with averted face for the breath of life to leave its beautiful tenement of clay. Mark Sefton would 'ome—come late. When he arrived all would be over with dark-eyed Uldene. A little before midnight she had opened her great, dark, wistful eyes, and a smile of unutter- able joy lit up her face as they rested upon Rut- ledge Chester. "Is it true that I am dying ?" the whispered. faintly. "Tell me, is it true? Do not deceive me. • I—l know—l feel that I am." How could they look into her face and answer her falsely, "Tell me, Rutledge, is it true?" she whispered. He corrtrolled himself with a mighty effort, and bent over her, taking her poor, fluttering little hand In his. "I—l fear it is so, Uldene," he said, huskily. "Is there any message you would like to send tz) any one—any wish you would like fulfilled ?" She looked up into his eyes with a great light breaking over her lovely face. "If one great wish in my heart could be fulfilled, I—l could die happier," she faltered, in pitiful, quivering peps. "If there is anything I can do for you, rest assured that you have only to ask and I will gran it, if it be in my power, Uldene," he answered, huskily, little dreaming what that great wish i:n her heart was. "I cannot tell you, Rutledge. I will whisper it to Mrs. Pierce, and she will tell you," she mur- mured. "Remember, Rutledge, I would never ask it I were not going to die—so soon—yes, eo soon." "You shall tell Mrs. Pierce," he answered, i gently. 1 And yet no thought came to him what it was she CHAPTER XII. I wished in this her dying hour. THE FULTILMENT OF A TERRIBLE CURSE. , The doctor and Rutledge withdrew to the other ' end. of the apartment, and the dying girl was left The secret he had discovered in so strange a with the sobbing housekeeper quite alone. manner puzzled and troubled Rutledge Chester not "I wish—but, oh, it could not be," sighed a little. Uldene, faintly—"l wish—that I—could be Nut" He was amazed that anyone should love him so much—that it was of such vital importance to any 1 ledge Chester's bride. I loved him so in life w one whether he lived or died. His heart was 411 my heart, with all my soul. I should like to be- touched ; he was greatly perplexed, long to him on earth, and wait for him inside the gates of heaven. I should like his hands to clasP If his heart had not been given to another it me as my life drifts out. I should like his voice te, might have turned then to Uldene. He felt so be the last sad music I should hear on earth; sorry for her. He was a thorough gentleman, and the words—ah, if I could but hear him say; ' ur the question which agitated him was, What should he do ? dene—my Uldete—my bride l' I-e-oh, then I could die happy—so happy. He admired Uidene exceedingly. She was the - 4! Would it be hard for him to grant me most beautiful girl he had ever seen; but admira- tion was one sentiment, love quite another. dying wish, do you think ?" she whispered, stil! 1 fainter. "Remember, it would be for a few brig He came to the conclusion that the best course moments—only a few fleeting moments—while I. to pursue would be to go away at once. It would hover between this world and the great unknown be kinder to Uldene than to stay, knowing her 1 world I am going to. Tell him quickly, Mrs. pitiful secret. Pierce. Tell him how I love him. 1 love him si° In time she would learn to forget him. She was ! well, that if, after I am dead, he came nel-' only a romping, merry, madcap of a school girl— I knelt upon my tomb, parted the long grass, and' a spoiled, indulged child. With absence she would ' whispered my name, I should hear-his voice in rnY be sure to forget this little romance, he assured of grave. Remember, he should never have known himself. my love if I were not dying." When he made known his determination at the Sobbing like a child, the old housekeeper called breakfast table, two days later, Mrs. Chester I Rutledge Chester aside, and imparted to him the was greatly disturbed, and Uldene grew pale as startling mission upon which the dying girl had death. tent her. He was going—going away on the beginning of That was the bitterest hour of Rutledge Chester's the following week. The words sounded like a , life. He started back with a low, hoarse cry. Ile death-ke ell to hapless Uldene. What should she ' fought one of the strangest battles with his own do with her life after he went away? The world I thoughts that mortal mau was over called upon to would be BO lonely; the brightness of her life would ' face. be obscured in pitiful gloom. She arose and left •• Remember, master, it is but for a few brief the table, with a forced smile on her lips, and a moments that the poor child would bear Yr° Steady step; but when the door of the breakfast- name," urged the housekeeper. "She would net room was closed between Rutledge Chester and last the hour out that would see her your bride. herself, the wildest sob that was ever heard broke She is so young—a thoughtless child—and she from her lips, and she groped her way through the loves you so." marble corridor like one stricken blind,, lie was touched to the depths of his soul; the_ On the staircase she met Mrs. Chester's maid struggle in his heart was ended. He turned to the hurrying 4 ;ward the breakfast-room with a tele- old housekeeper with a white, grave face. gram ; but lae matter did not interest her—nothing cc I will grant the poor child's dying wish," b.c3i interested her save that which concerned Rutledge said, hoarsely. "She shall be my bride ! 0° Chester. grant that it may make her last moment's An hour later Mrs. Chester came into her boudoir happier." in a flutter of excitement. "I am called away hurriedly by e telegram I A minister was hastily summoned, and in the hour of pridnirdlt while t,he wild winds moan have just received, Uldene," she said. "I shall ' .. ' be back Thursday at latest. Do you think you and sobbed Our Bide, and the whirling snowflake° without wrapped the earth in a cold, white, frorell will mind remaining here a day or so in charge of r shroud the t,Lal marriage—that man could neveao the servants, dear?" she asked, anxiously. break ' and Heaven would not annul'' "Rutledge will he here, you know, when ho is not ' • solemnised. at his club-room." "I shall not mind," answered the girl. In the solemn hour of death Uldene lay bill' If Mrs. Cheattr had not been so flurried and upon her pillow— Rutledge Chester's brie: excited she would have noticed how white Uldene's While hovering on the brink of eternity she had ouoo lovely face was, and how hopeless and forlorn was braved the tatal warning that hung like a , to over her beastiful, hapless head—that sole her usually gay, sprightly manner. An hour later, the senator and his wife took warning : "She ntut Weer love, for she mita 7t6vet their hurried departure. Mrs. Chester's last words, nyci'llke as she took her place beside her husband in the family coach, were : "1 may have a startling surprise for you when I return; still I must not be too sanguine." Then the coach door closed with a bang, and an instant later whirled out of sight. "You will be very lonely without mother the few days she will be gone," said Rutledge, thoughtfully, as he turned to Uldene. "I should suggest that you invite some of your girl friends here to pass the time with you." "No," replied Uldene. " I—l could not endure their chatter and their laughter," she muttered below her breath. "It would drive me mad." Uldene seemed to forget that she was standing on the cold marble steps, with only the crimson silk scarf wrapped loosely around her dark, curly head; but Rutledge remembered. "The carriage is out of sight," he said, taking her cold little hand to lead her back to the library. "Come into the house, Uldene." The magic touch of his hand seemed to unnerve her. Quick as a flash, she snatched it from his grasp and sprang into the vestibule, but ere she had taken another step forward she suddenly swayed to and fro like a leaf in the tempest-tossed wind, and would have fallen to the floor, if Rut- ledge had not sprung forward and quickly caught her in his arms. " Efldene I " he cried in alarm. She did not answer. She had sunk back in his arms in a dead faint. Rutledge bore the slender form quickly to the library, and rang the bell with such a resounding peals for the housekeeper that motherly Mrs. Pierce was "startled clean out of her wits," as she afterwards expressed it. In a few words Rutledge explained what had happened. "See that she is removed to her room at once, and give hist. your kindly attention," he said, thoughtfully and gravely. "I wonder that young master is so blind that he cannot see the girl is pining away for love of him," thaughLiiia._ Darea,Gouaaalaztvicls, Is Asli_i</p>
        <p>lighted up his dark, grave, kingly face, as he bent over his love. "If this was acting, what could the reality be 1" they asked themselves. But as they gazed upon the face of the girl he held clasped in the arms, their wonder grew. Her long, dark curling hair fell around her lovely face like a black veil. Was it fancy, or did the wondrous face of Uldene Sefton really whiten under their gaze, and her lips grow ashen pale. Was it love or terror that shone in the dark, upraised eyes? The scene held the vast audience spell-bound, and frightened them as they gazed, until the silken curtain shut out the scene from their sight. And at that instant quite a scene was transpiring back of the curtain. In stepping back from the narrow platform which had served as a stage, one of the planks beneath the Brusoels rug gave way, and Rutledge was precipitated to the floor below—a distance of ten feet or more—his head striking one of the pillars that supported the groined roof as he fell. A wild cry burst from Uldene's white lips. In an instant she was kneeling beside the bleeding, unconscious form. She raised his head in her white arms, attempting to staunch the blood that flowed from a wound on his temple, with sobs and bitter cries pitiful to bear. "Leave ma with him until the doctor and his mother come," she whispered, motioning them all away. Silently they quitted the apartment, closing the door softly after them; and the gay strains of the dance music that struck up in the grand ball-room stifled the sound of her wailing cries. "Rutledge ! my love I oh, my love ! if you die, Heaven must let me die, too !" she sobbed, wildly, covering the white unconscious face, the closed eyes, and the matted hair with pas- sionate kisses in her terrible grief. "What would life be to me without you ?" she moaned. "Oh, Rutledge, my love, you will never know how well I have loved you. I would give my life for yours. I would meet death to save you one pang." She clasped the unconscious form still closer in her white arms, caressing him with piteous agony, and murmuring broken uords of love over him. _ _</p>
        <p>Unconscious, did I say? It was not quite that. For a few moments the force of the fall stunned and bewildered Rutledge Chester, but the action of the cold water with which Uldene bathed his face brought back his dazed senses at once. He felt the clasp of the clinging arms about him. The yassionato, wailing, broken words of love that were sobbed out over him fell like a shock upon his startled ears. Sheer amazement and conster- nation chained his dazed senses. Should he open his eyes and falter: "Forgive me, Uldene; I have unconsciously discovered your love for me?" It would be worse than death to the girl's proud nature; the shock, the cruel em- barrassment would prostrate her. Yet it Wa.:3 equally embarrassing to him to receive the assur- rances of her wild, idolatrous love, with closed eyes, she believing him to be unconscious. Rutledge Chester was a gentleman and a man of honour in the strictest sense of the word; yet , between duty and delicacy he scarcely knew which way to turn. Fortunately the doctor's quick footsteps relieved him in his strangely trying dilemma. The usual restoratives were applied; then he dared opened his eyes. Uldene stood beside him with a white scared face. "Oh, doctor, tell me—is he badly hurt or not? I—l cannot bear suspense," he heard her say, piteously. "Badly hurt? Oh, no, my dear," replied the doctor, cheerfully. "I am glad to say Mr. Chester has had a miraculouse escape. He has had a bad fall—a slight scalp wound—and was stunned, that's all. I'll venture to say he will be all right in a day or so." Rutledge Chester looked up into her face, his own flushing painfully. His heart smote him with a strange pity. The great, dark, velvety eyes that fell so quickly under his gaze were wet with tears.</p>
