      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held on Thursday at noon, at the Town flail. There were present :—The Nlayor (Alderman Oreenall),the Deputy-Mayer (Alderman Francon:lb). Aldermen : G. Blackhurst, C. J. Holmes, J. Platt, T. H. Sutton, J. E. Wright. Councillors :J. Ashton, S. J. Beaman, A. Bennett, W. Bolton, J. Cantlell, R. T. Fairclougb, J. Fairclougb, T. Flood, J. Garner, J. S. Green, L. Greening, J. Depherd, J. Hill, T.Hutch- inson, H. J. Longton, J. Muir, G. Plumptor-Q, Ij Roberts, W. J. Shaw, J. Shillcock, W. P. Skelton, J. Smethurst, W. Tinsley, and the following officers : J. L. Whittle (town clerk). J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer), T. Longdin (borough surveyor), and J. H. Gernall (medical officer).</p>
        <p>THE RECENT ELECTIONS. The Mayor announced that the following gentlemen were elected on the let November as members of the Council :—Town Hall Ward, Mr. J. Shilleock ; White- cross, Mr. H. Roberts ; Bewsey, Mr. J. Shaw Green ; Orford, Mr. W. P. Skelton ; St. John's, Mr. W. J. Shaw; Fairfield, Mr. H. J. Longton ; Howley, Mr. A. Bennett; Latchford, Mr. J. Ashton. Mr. J. Hepherd was re- turned unopposed for St. Austin's Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ELECTION OF MAYOR.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF MAYOR.</p>
        <p>_ The MAYOR announced that the next business was the election of Mayor for the ensuing year. Alderman Flomies said that it fell to his lot twelve months ago to propose the re-election of the Mayor. He did so again with great pleasure, and on behalf of the Council expressed their appreciation of the services of the Mayor, coupled with those of his lady,the Mayoress. (Applause.) Alderman Sue Tom seconded the proposal. He spoke in eulogistic terms of the Mayor's past services, and was glad that in that cennection Mrs. Greenell's name had been mentioned. Alderman FRANCOMB and Councillor H. ROBERTS briefly supported the mction, which was carried unani- mously. The MAYOR in replying said :—Before making any other remarks, I have to thank you for bringing my wife's name in when speaking of me. She has been a great help-meet to me, and lam gratified at your men- tioning it. For the third time I have to thank you for the great honour you have conferred upon me again in electing me Mayor of my native town. I thank you gentlemen, for the cordiality with which you have this day re-elected me, and for the kind expressions you have thought fit to use in connection with my services for the past two years. I appreciate your kindness, and thank you for your confidence. I cannot accept the office without at the same time feeling the heavy responsibili- ties which I see foreshadowed in the coming year. Dur- ine the present year the town has been unfortunately afflicted with the terrible epidemic of smallpox, which, in addition to the suffering caused, cast a heavy cloud over trade, and a burden on the ratepayers. (Hear, hear.) At present the outlook is even still more gloomy. We are face to face now with a most lamentable coal strike, which entirely paralyses all trade in our country, and of which we shall feel the effects for months, if not years. (Hear, hear.) It is terrible to think of the suffering in our midst, of the starving men, women, and children, who through no fault of their own are now in this severe weather without coal, and absolutely depen- dent on our distress funds for their daily bread and the hare necessities of life. I can only trust that before long better counsels will prevail, and a settlement he arrived at. (Hear, hear.) I thank my townspeople and friends around for their hearty response to my appeal for help, and regret being obliged to appeal still further, hut do so confidently, knoeving that the people of Warrington will never allow deserving people to starve in their midst, but will assist to the best of their ability as far as their incomes will allow, those less fortunate than themselves. I trust the coming year will in every way be a brighter and more prosperous one for our town. (Applause.) The year on which we are now entering will be a busy one. At the present time we have matters of momentous importance to settle with the Manchester Ship Canal Company, the settlement of which must not be delayed, but which we have every reason to believe will be amicably arranged. (Hear, hear.) It is officially announced that the Ship, Canal will be opened on the let of December, and Warrington wilEe one of the first towns to welcome such an import- ant event. We have our rights and interests to protect, and intend doing so, not acting in a hostile spirit, but assisting the company as far as possible, but considering at the same time that the welfare of our traders and town in general is to us of paramount importance. (Hear, hear.) There are mane other public matters torcine themselves upon the attention of the Council, and remembering that Warrington now numbers nearly 56.000 inhabitants, it is not to be wondered that the wants of the town grow in proportion to its size. There has been no lack of enterprize during the last few years, namely, the purchase of the Water- works, the erection of the new Baths, and the improve- ment of your public streets, but still much remains to do to keep pace with the times, and some of the most important being the improvement of the bridewellwhich year after year is condemned by the Government Inspector as being totally inadequate ; the enlargement of your Town Hell, which is most necessary,the present arrangement being most inconvenient for the amount of business transacted under its roof, and also the still further widening of our streets. In the bill which it is proposed to introduce into Parliament next year, I can see a very busy time for the Council, and I have no doubt I shall receive, as I have done in the past, the cordial assistance of the members of the Council, upon which in a great measure, I shall have to rely. Again let me thank von, in conclusion, for the great honour you have conferred upon me, and believe me that the honour and welfare of the borough are to me of the first consideration. (Loud applause.) The election of an alderman for Latchford was next proceeded with. A report of the proceedings in connec- tion with which will be found in another column. APPOINTMENT OF COMMITTEES. The following committees were next appointed . WATCu.—The Mayor ; Aldermen Blackhurst, Fran- comb, Greening, Harrison Platt, Sutton ; Councillors Cannel", Greening, Heaton:Tinsley. FINANCE AND EsTATE.—Aldermen Blacktturst, Holmes, Greenall ; Councillors Bennett, James Fairclough, Shaw, Green, flepherd, Monks, Longton, Tinsley. STREET IMPROVEMENT AND LIGHTING .—Aldermen Blackhurst, Harrison, Platt, Sutton, Wright ; Coun- cillors Bolton, Bonnell, Hepherd, Muir, Plumpton. HEALTH.--Aldermen Blackhurst, Sutton,Wreght,Coun- cillors Beaman, Bonnell, Flood, Greening, Roberts, Shaw Wilkinson. SANITARY IVomTs.—Councillors Beaman, Bonnet) ,Flood Greening, Monks, Roberts,Shillcock, Skelton,Smethnrst, Wilkinson. PAVING AND SEWERAGE—AIdermen Wright, Ashton, Councillors Beaman, Jas. Fairclough, R. T. Fairclough, Flood, Heaton, Hutchinson, Muir, Smethurst. ScHoor. ATTENDANCE.—AIderman Blackhurst, Coun- cillors Beaman, Cannel!, Flood, Garner, Shaw-Green; Heaton, Plumpton, Shaw, Shillcock, Smethurst, Wilkin- son. MUSEUM, LIBRAIiY, ARTS, AND TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION. —Alderman Greenall (Mayor), Alderman Sutton, Coun- cillors Bennett, Flood, Shaw-Green, Greening, Monks, Muir, Plumpton, Roberts, and the following nen-mem- bers of the Council, viz. :—Messrs. Robert Davies, Wm. Owen, Robert Pierpoint, M.P., J. E. Worsley, John Peers, F. Monks, J. F. Dutton. CEMETERY.—AIdermen Harrison, Ashton, Councillors Bonnell, Flood, Heaton, Hill, Hutchinson, Muir, Shill- cock, Wilkinson. TOWN RALL,PARIC, AND BATHS.— The Mayor, Aldermen Francomb, Harrison, Sutton,Wright, Councillors Bolton, Cannell, Heaton, Hill, Hutchinson, Muir. MARKETS.—Aldermen Greenall, Blackhurst, Ashton, Cnncillors .J. Fairclough, Garner, Shaw-Green, Hep- herd, Hill, Hutchinson, Tinsley. Gas—Aldermen Holmes, Wright, Councillors R. T. Fairclough, James Fairclough, Greening, Heaton, Hepherd, Monks, Skelton, Tinsley. RlVERS.—Aldermen Francomb, Sutton, Councillors Bennett, Bolton, IL T. Fairclough, Greening, Heaton, Hutchinsem Monks, Roberts. FIRE ENGlNE.—Alderman Ashton, Councillors Cannel!, Heaton, Hill, Hutchinson, Muir, Plumpton, Shaw, Shillcock, Smethurst. WATER.—Aldermen Blackhurst, Francomb, Wright, Councillors Beaman, Bolton,Garner, Shaw Green, Green- ing, Hepherd, Roberts.</p>
