        <p>" Meltham is ringing with it! One of the most marvellous things that ever came within my kn ow- ledge." These words were spoken by a prominent member cf the Meltham Local Board to a representative of the " Huddersfield Weekly News" the other day. I They made our reporter open his eyes, for it is not often this gentleman vouchsafes any belief in the marvellous ; and the next words astonished hi:n even more. " Yes; the girl was practically dad; she had no use whatever in her legs, and for I believe six years, had been as help- less as a child. Prior to her illness she had worked in the mihlof Messrsionas Brook and Brothers,and land every one who knew her fully believed, when we heard three years ago that the doctors had given her up, that she was doomed: imagine, then, our astonishment when we learned, about a month ago, that she was not only in good health and spirits, but actually able to walk." This statement aroused our representative's interest, and he asked the Local Board member if he would give him the particulars of the case. The latter said that the girl's name was Emma Haigh : that she lived at Green's-end, Meltham, and that for six years she had been a helpless cripple, but suddenly the news went rpund that a new specific—Dr. Williams' Pink Pills— had effected a cure, and that the girl could walk. Our representative, therefore, visited Meltham and investig- ated the matter, having full authority to probe ttie so- called miracle, and ascertain if there were any truth in , the reports.</p>
        <p>Melthain is a pretty little hamlet or village situate about five miles from Huddersfield. Our representative had no difficulty in finding Mrs. Haigh's ; and the first person he asked as to her whereabouts said, " Do you mean her that had a daughter that couldn't walk P' On his being answered in the affirmative, he continued. •' Come on,lad, and I'll show thee t'house,' and as he went along he told our representative sufficient of the history of the case to give an unsolicited corroboration to what he bad been told by the ?fore-mentioned Local Board member. On knocking, the door was opened by a stout matronly-looking woman, with Yorkshire stamped on every feature, and truth shining brightly in her eyes. Come in, sir," she said, and accepting the invitation, he stepped into the home of a working man, with car- peted floor, at down on an easy long settle, and found himself vis-ci.vis with a comely bright-eyed brunette of about 25 summers. Her face bore traces of severe affliction, and her hands, which were busily occupied with some work, were held in rather a peculiar way. " Well, and how are you to day !" said our represen- tative. "Oh ! I'm very well, indeed," replied Miss Haigh ; and then she gave the facts of her case. Some seven or eight years ago she was down with rheumatism and for five or six weeks she was unable to go to her work. When she had been at work about a fortnight her ankles and legs began to pain her, and she was com- pelled to leave her work and finally again take to her bed. The doctor attended her, and tried all the remedies he could think of, but without avail, and the poor girl endured terrible pain in her drawn-up limbs. On the advice of the doctor, her mother took the girl to the Huddersfield Infirmary, first as an out-patient, and finally as an in-patient, but to their sorrow and dismay she was discharged from that institution as an incurable. Subsequently Miss Haigh was sent to the Convalescent Home at Bridlington ; but on arrival was informed they could not take her in, as they only took people who could walk. " Home once more, probably to die !" as the girl said pathetically ; and she was put into bed, from which she did not rise for six long and weary years —years of anguish and suffering which only those who have suffered the pains which follow in the train of rheumatism have any conception of. Her parents tried everything they could get, and she swallowed gallons of one kind of medicine and another, but all to no purpose. One day her brother came in in a very excited way and said that he believed he had found something which would cure her; and as he spoke he produced a copy of the "Weekly News," and pointed out an article headed, " Narrow Escape of a Holmfirth Girl." The brother read the article aloud, and, as he went on, Miss Haigh said she found that the case was a one to hers, and hope rose high, in her breast that once more, by the medium of the specific mentioned therein (Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People), she might be able to walk out and breathe the fresh air of heaven. The mother, nearly as excited as her daughter almost flew to the chemist and asked for some of the pills. Before the first box was finished, Miss Haigh said she felt a change coming over her, and by the time she had taken four boxes, she could it up in bed and dress her own hair. Two more boxes, and she was able to get up, dress, and walk about the floor with the aid of crutches ; " and now, look here," said Miss Haigh, and she getup from her and walked sprightly across the floor. After resuming her seat, she said, in answer to an inquiry, that between the first box and the second, she ' was a fortnight without taking the pills, as they had to wait until they come from Manchester, ana she felt the want of them very much in the interval; but now they had done her so much good that when the other week she had to wait a few days without them, she felt no harm ceme from it. " I don't intend," she said," to he without again; and I shall always feel a great deal of gratitude to Dr. Williams for the good he has done me. They saved me from a living death, and have given back to me strength to walk about and enjoy life." " Then you really and truly believe, Mrs. Haigh, that it was these pills that have given back to your daughter that power in her legs which she had been deprived of for six years ?" " Most certainly I do," was the dame's reply. " I shall always feel I owe Dr. Williams a debt which I can never repay, for what he has done for my daughter." In further conversation, Mrs. Haigh said that when her daughter had taken the third or fourth box, she (Mrs. Haigh) was standing looking out of the window, when she felt a touch on her shoulder, and turning quickly, she saw standing in the centre of the floor her daughter, who had never walked for so many years. " My heart jumped into my mouth," said the moth er, with glistening eyes, "and can you wonder when I saw her as she is now, and know that it is through the instru- mentality of Dr. William's Pink Pills that she is able to walk that I feel so happy and grateful to him ?" Bidding Mrs. Haigh good day, our representative took his leave. He interviewed several neighbours, and one and all said it was nothing short of a miracle. He also saw a Mrs. Ling, who had been suffering for years and years from languor and weakness. She said she could do nothing first thing in the morning, but when she had taken one box, on the recommendation of Mrs. Haigh, she felt her strength come back as if by magic, and added, pointing to a little cot, in which lay two fine twins," I could never have lived when they were born if it had not been for Dr. William's Pink Pills." The courteous manager of the Meltham Co-operative Society stated to our representative that it was through his instrumentality the Pills were introduced into the village. The case of Miss Haigh was quite a genuine one, and he himself felt quite convinced that there was no better medicine going. Everybody is taking them now, he said, and where he once used to order a couple of boxes, lie now had to order grosses. "And you really think these pills cured Miss Haigh ? " "Ido ; for I knew the girl well, and it is nothing short of the mar- vellous." To sum up, our representative left Meltham that even- ing fully convinced of the truth and genuineness of the cure, and also with a firm belief that Dr. Williams' Pills are unique in their action upon disease. The beauty of these Pills, as Mr. Haigh said, was that they were not a purgative, and thereby weakening, but they strengthened, and at the same time got rid of all superfluous matter. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are recognised as one af the greatest modern medicines, a perfect blood builder and nerve restorer, curing such diseases as Rheumatism, Neuralgia, Partial Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, St. Vitus' Dance, Nervous Headache, Nervous Prostration and :the tired feeling resulting thereform, diseases depending upon Humours in the Blood, such as Scrofula, Chronic Erysipelas, Aic. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are a specific for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, tthile in the ease of men they effect a</p>
