        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inex- pensive as compared with other remedies er medical treatment.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CIII7RCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. On Sunday evening the Rev. J. Warnock, M.A., 8.D., preached a special sermon in St. John's Presbyterian Church, Winwick-street, taking for his subject, "Aptitudes worth thinking about, or what our inferiors teach us."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. Drummond, 8.A., preached an in- teresting sermoa at the Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel on Sunday evening, on "Doubting and believing." His remarks were listened to with great interest by a large congregation. THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There was a capital attendance at tbe P. S. A. meeting in Wycliffe Church, on Sunday afternoon. The chair was occupied by Mr. A. Corney, who delivered a short address, taking as his subject "The Battle of Life— choice of weapons." Mr. Thomas Johnson, superintend- ent of a Manchester ragged school, was the speaker for the afternoon, and he based his remarks on the text, "If yo love me, love one another." The address was a very interesting one and was listened to with much at- tention. During the service two solos were well rendered by Mr.W.H.Wallington, and Mr. Watt. Mr.W.H.Payton, accompanied on the organ. The president, Rev.J. Yonge announced that the membership of the brotherhood had now reached 1008. Mr. C. T. Owen read the lesson.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN HOME MISSION WORK. An illustrated lecture was given in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Bold-street, on Monday evening, by the Rev. Marmaduke Riggall, formerly Wesleyan minister at War- ! rington. The lecturer dealt with the work of the Devon and Dorset Mission in connection with the Wesleyan denomination, of which Mr.Riggall is the superintendent. Mr. W. Silcock occupied the chair, and briefly introduced the lecturer. At the commencement of his lecture, Mr. Riggall showed his audience a map of the district, covered by the mission, whichis comidered the widest in Great Britain. The area covered by this mission is ovel 500 square miles, and comprises for the most part rurat parishes, tho chief towns being Budleigh Salterton Honiton, and Chard, many of the inhabitants being en- gaged in lace manufacture, and agricultural pursuits. The lecturer described the many difficulties experienced in obtaining land on which to build missions, owing to the intolerance and bigotry of country squires and par- sons. To show the rapid progresi made since the mis- sion was started, Mr. Riggall explained that the mis- sion stations had increased by ten, they now numbering 128, and there were now 59 church members against 263 before the mission was inaugurated. Many of the I missions were shown on the screen, some of them pre- senting a dilapidated condition, and other, places of meeting being private houses. The lecturer announced that the missions were visited by the present president of the Wesleyan Conference—Rev. IL J. Pope—and as he was not satisfied with their present condition, he de- termined to set on foot a movement for the erection of ten new Wesleyan chapels for the Devon and Dorset Mission, and a considerable amount of money had been raised towards that object. The pastor of the Wesleyan Chapel at Chard, Mr. Fish gave a short account of his work at Chard, and a picture of the church was thrown on the screen. It was sandwiched between a butcher's shop on the one side, and a public house on the other, and a tin whistle club at the latter place, Mr. Fish re- marked never forgot to play during service time. The church itself was in need of repair, and it is pro- posed to build a new one. The people attending the chapels were very poor, and the lecturer remarked that a woman making Honiton lace, by working 12 hours a day could earn ninepence, and the farm labourers earned about 12s. per week. A collection was taken in aid of the fund for building the new chapels. At the close of the lecture, the Rev. G. D. Mason offered prayer, and an interesting and instructive meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Tuesday night. Mr. J. Pritchard presided, and there were present Messrs. T. Handley. S. Taylor, and G. Moss (clerk).</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee reported that 69 parents had been summoned before them and 65 ap- peared. 32 parents were ordered to be summoned be- fore the justices. The report of the Attendance Officers for the past month showed an average attendance of 3,075 as against 3,117 in the corresponding month of last year, being a decrease of 42. The percentage of attendance was 83•3, same as the corresponding month last year.—Passed on the motion of Mr. Handley.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The School. Management Committee recommended that the salary of Miss Kilburn, head teacher of Green- way-road Girl's School,be advanced to £llO per annum, that the salary of Miss Robb, head teacher at Mill Brow Infant School be advanced to £B5 per annum. That Miss Robb be appointed assistant teacher at Greenway- road Girl's Evening School, at a remuneration of 3s. 6d. per evening. The Clerk reported that the Evening Schools had opened well, there being on the Rolls 100 males and 86 females. The work was progressing satis- factorily.—On the motion of the Chairman the minutes were confirmed. SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the School Accommodation Committee had been held. Present : Mr.J.Pritchard (chairman) the Rev. Canon Wood, Messrs. T. Handley, and E. Itnison. The Clerk presented statistics of attendance during the month of September last, showing that the number of girls in the schools was 80 more than the certified accommodation. There were also about 20 girls absent from school owing to not being able to gain admission. This taken together showed a want of accommodation for 100 girls. On the other hand It had been ascertained that there were in the schools about 40 girls belonging to workmen employed on the Ship Canal, and these would probably leave the town on the completion of the works. 'I hese facts were fully considered by the Com- mittee who recommended that the Board's architect be consulted with respect to extending Greenway-road schools so as to provide increased accommodation for girls.—Mr. Handley moved the confirmation of the minutes. He thought they ought at the same time to give consideration for boys and infants. There was much building going on at Higher Runcorn and there would be a great many infants from those houses. Where were they to be sent to? According to the returns there was only room for five infants in Newton School; in Mill Brow there was only room for two and they could not expect infants to travel into the centre of the town even it there was accommodation there. There was accom- modation for 35 infants at the National school, and 56 infants at Trinity school, but they could not expect children from Higher Runcorn to travel either to Church at., or Irwell-lane. He therefore thought the Board ought to take into serious consideration the making of provision for infants at Greenway-road school.—Thel Chairman suggested that this matter should be post- poned and the Board take a more extended view.—Mr. Handley thought they had better proceed as recom- mended at present, as there was so small a Board that evening, but he deemed it advisable to call attention to the want of accommodation for infants. They could discuss the matter with their architect.—Mr. Taylor said the need of more accommodation was constantly before the School Attendance Committee. They were met in every direction by parents saying they could not get their children into school.—This remark applied not only to girls but to boys and infants. In the face of such statements by parents the School Attendance Committee felt in a most awkward position. If they had accommodation in their own schools they could deal with them. In his opinion it was no use getting their architect there to deal with accommodation for a num- ber of girls, so long as there was a need of accommoda- tion all round. They had better face the question broadly and fully ; deal with the whole of it and not part of it. The Board would have to do it.—Dr.Gastaldi in a letter apologising for his inability to be present through ill- ness, expressed the opinion :that neither :the increase of salaries nor the proposed extension of the girls' school were opportune at the present time, especially as the trade in the locality and the completion of the Manches- ter Ship Canal were not such as would warrant an in- crease in the rates.—The Chairman said it would be better to deal with the question as a whole than in piecemeal fashion,but lie suggested that the whole ques- tion might be raised before the architect.—Mr. Handley Said there were now 38 boys in attendance at Green- way-road school in excess of attendance.—Tne minutes were passed, on the understanding that the members could raise the whole question before the architect.</p>
        <p>The Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH GLORR Is unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to starchad linen, ,tc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. —, Own- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss?" From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear 011..- 1 am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so.called Starch Glazes and Polishes,"but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every, house. Sold at id., 3d., and 6d., ,by most starch sellers. If not in stock will prooure it. T. CRlnaLur. Blackburn, P:oprietor.--ildbt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>AN ABORTIVE CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The joint conference of coalowners' and miners' repre- sentatives was resumed on Saturday at the Westminster Palace Hotel, London, after eacla section of the disput- ants had, as usual, for some time deliberated separately. The joint conference lasted little more than an hour and a half, and had a disappointing result, no settlement being arrived at. No arrangement was made for any resumption of the conference. From reports and state- ments it appears that the Coalowners' Association pro- posed the establishment of a Board of Conciliation to endeavour to bring about a settlement of the present dispute,and to adjust any future differences. They also proposed that work should be resumed at 15 per cent. reduction, such sum to be banked pending the decision of the Conciliation Board, and to bo finally apportioned as between employers and workmen in accordance with the terms of settlement. The men's delegates, on the other hand, proposed that they should resume work at the old rate of wages until the let April, 1894 ; that the minimum be 30 per cent, above the wages rate of 1888 ; and that a Board of Conciliation be formed to determine the rate on and from April of next year. Mr. Pickard ultimately announced that the delegates could not accept the proposed terms, but would submit them to the men, and thereupon the conference dissolved. RE-OPENING OF THE SHIPLEY (DERBYSHIRE)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PITS.</head>
        <p>PITS.</p>
        <p>The following notices were on Monday posted at the pit heads of these collieries, which employ between 1,600 and 1,700 men :—ln consequence of the bad state of the soft coal workings caused by gob fires, it is necessary to recommence work in that seam. The soft coal pits will therefore be open on Thursday next at the old rate of wages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MASS MEETING AT ST. HELENS. THE MASTERS' PROPOSALS REJECTED. THE STRUGGLE TO GO ON.</head>
        <p>MASS MEETING AT ST. HELENS. THE MASTERS' PROPOSALS REJECTED. THE STRUGGLE TO GO ON.</p>
        <p>Three thousand men assembled at the Peoplft's Palace, St. Helens, on Monday morninc,°, to hear Mr. Thomas Glover, J.P., report upon the London Confer- ence. Mr. John Jackson, a collier, was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Glover, J.P., first referred to the statements of Mr. B. B. Glover, a local colliery proprietor, who on an election platform charged the miners' agent with divulg- ing in public a private conversation and also asking what Mr. Glover had done with the money that had passed through his fingers during the past five or six years. The speaker said that the conversation was not private, as it was a public question concerning his workmen,and there was no understanding that it would be private. As to the money that had been paid, it went through the hands of a treasurer, and a fort- nightly balance sheet was prepared. The great hulk of the money, the lion's share, went in resisting the pri- vate reductions made by such masters as Mr. B. B. Glover. The Federation had paid £l,OOO per week to- wards men on strike in various parts of the country. Mr. B. B. Glover had also stated that 71d. out of every is. paid went in the salaries of Federation officials. That had been placed in the hands of a solicitor, who had advised them that as it was a general question the leaders had not a case against Mr. B. B. Glover. But he (the miners' agent) could say that no salaries were paid to the Federation officials. Mr.B. B. Glover might think he had some latitude on an election platform, but he ought to be careful and not slander men who were trying to do the best they could for both men and mas- ters. (Applause.) The masters (Mr. Glover said) had made their minds up to starve the workingmen into sub- mission, as it was only by starvation they would be able Ito gain their ends. A more degrading and humiliating position the masters were never placed in—to gain their ends they would starve thousands of workingmen who had made them thousands of pounds. There was t mas- ter at the conference, Mr. Bidder, who was in a false position. He had pits at work at the old rate and was connected with pits that were standing out. And that was the very position of Mr. B. B. Gl)ver, St. Helens. He had his pits at St.lielens stopped and his men starv- ing, and he was connected with pits in Staffordshire which were at work and selling coal at 20s. per ton. If ever there was a false position under heaven that was a false position. He was starving the men in the place where he resided, and where he boasted he was born, and making money in another place. (A Voice : "Take a note of that.") There were employers at the confer- ence who were willing to allow their men to go to work, and if they could got every colliery wherever possible to work, their position would be greatly improved. If they could do this, and the Wigan Coal and Iron Com- pany, with Mr. Hewlitt, could be boycotted, he would boycott that firm. They might talk about that being a conspiracy, but they had got to do their best to pro- tent their interests and their wages. (Cheers.) If one firm would undersell other employers and bring down prices, and then seek to lower wages,the men must stop the wheels of progress of such a firm until they quoted higher rates, rates upon which they could pay interest upon capital arid pay a proper wage to their workmen. (Applause.) The lauthorities had brought mounted police into the town I and fifty other constables from Liverpool to protect them, and they had had nothing to do. (A voice: " Except drinking") except living in luxury. But they (the ratepayers) had got to pay for it—£4o a day. The Mayor had said he was prepared to pay the money if the Council would not (A voice : " Let him pay "). If he (Mr. Glover) had been in the Council he would have got up and asked the Council to let Mr. Sinclair pay for them. The Mayor and the chairman of the Watch Com- mittee were not so ready to pay £4O a day to help the starving wives and families. God only knew the men did not want the strike to go on in its present condition but he did not think it was anything to boast about that those who had made their money out of the working men of the tewn would not render them any assistance. It was for the meeting to decide whether they would accept the master's proposals of 15 per cent, reduction subject to arbitration. The masters at the London con- ference offered 15 per cent, reduction and a conciliation board, and Mr. Hewlitt, of the Wigan Coal and Iron Company, who lost his temper, said they (the men) had not modified their proposals one jot, and that the men would have to go in at a reduction. The miners' leaders without even the authority of the men, had offerod to reduce their minimum wage from 30 per cent to 30 per I cent. if the prices in April nest were as low as they were in July last when the strike commenced. Mr. Hewlitt said they would have to go on fighting, and they might depend upon it they would have to go in at a reduction, and he got in such a temper that everybody became agitated, and an adjournment took place. He was perfectly satisfied that the chief object of the mas- ters who met at LOndon was expressed by Mr. Hewlitt "We have never been beaten, and we don't intend to be beaten this time." Mr. George Dixon, a check weigliman, regarded the action of the employers as a cruel, inhuman disappoint- ment, and considered the calling of the meeting on their part a piece of fraud on the public. He did not blame the men's representatives for conceding what they had in order, if possible, to bring about a settlement. He further asserted that if all the colliery books were , opened up it would be found that the men had not received 20 per cent. out of what was represented as a 40 per cent. increase. The resolution was then put to the meeting, and with one dissentient it was decided to reject the offer of the masters, and to continue the struggle. The meeting which had been very quiet, then termin- ated. A large crowd of people were unable lto gain admission to the Palace.</p>
        <p>AN OFFER BY ST. HELENS COALOWNERS. Messrs. Richaril Evans and Co., the largest coal- owners in the St. Helens district, on Monday afternoon I gave netice to their 4,000 men that they might resume work at the old rate, but 15 per cent will be te- tained by the Company until a general settlement was effected. If it is decided that the men shall work at the old rate permanently this sum will be returned to] them, but any reduction agreed upon will be kept by the Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>GLOOMY FOREBODING AT ST. TIELENS. COLLINS GREEN REFUSE TO RENEW THEIR OFFER.</head>
        <p>GLOOMY FOREBODING AT ST. TIELENS. COLLINS GREEN REFUSE TO RENEW THEIR OFFER.</p>
        <p>A feeling seems to be gaining ground in St. Helens-1 that this, the greatest and most prolonged coal strike on, record, will extend into new year. The Collins Green and Bold Collieries Company, St. Helens, have decided not to renew the offer made last week for men to resume work at 7s. per day for colliers, and 6s. per day for drawers. The company have not yet considered the question of reopening the pits on the pro- posals made at the joint conference, viz., at the old rate of wages, but 15 per cent, retained pending ameneral settlement. The manager - (Mr. Jackson) does not think there is any likelihood of an early resumption of work. A HAYDOCK MINISTER'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Riddette, of Flaydock, on Wedneaday issued an appeal. He says :—" For the love of God and ' for the sake of our common humanity do send me a little help for the starving woman and children of this township. The entire population, 7,000 is entirely dependant upon the coal industry, and the stoppage of the mines means absolute starvation for some families, and terrible destitution to all. In God's name do send something quickly." The circular gives a number of cases of families suffering terrible destitution. The rev. gentleman adds that he has carefully analysed a large number of wages' sheets for that district and finds that the net normal war.te for an average miner in Ilaydock is 4s. 6d. a day. For a long time past the masters had not provided work for more than five days a week. He :Ada that miners are free from the responsibility of the continuance of the strike. THE HOUSE OF COMMONS SUBSCRIPTION. TORIES WILL NOT SUBSCRIBE. The movement in favour of raising a House of Commons subscription for the wives and children of the men on strike is says the London correspondent of the "Manchester Guardian," in a bad way, and appears likely to fall through. The Conservatives refuse to have asaything to do with it, on the ground that from their point of view to subscribe to such a fund would bo to prolong the strike. The idea at the beginning was to make the subscription purely a non-party affair, aid as</p>
        <p>his is found to be impossible the project will probably be allowed to drop. • MR. SAM WOODS AND THE DISTRESS AT 1 WIGAN.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. Hebblethwaite Primitive Methodist minister, Wigan, has received the following note from Mr. S. Woods, M.P. : House of Commons, Nov. 7. 1893.</p>
        <p>Dear Si r,—l have your very kind letter here this morn- ing, and I shall do what I can in order to assist you in your noble and self-denying work, and to get what can be got to feed our noble men and their families in their ' defensive resistance of this unholy and wicked combina- tion of callous hearted commercial experts who are using all the powers of earth and hell to prop up their con- temptible dignity. Their responsibility for this reckless policy of starvation will not be lifted from their shoulders by smooth words and finely-rounded manifestoes. They will find that they will have to reckon with an injured nation, abused privileges, and an ever watchful and over-ruling Providence. Many thanks.—Yours tru'y. S. WOODS.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. Hebblethwaite. MR. WOODS, M.P., AND THE ATTITUDE OF THE MEN. Mr. Sam Woods, M.P., has addressed the following Jotter to Mr. P. D. Grimke-Drayton, manager of the Hay- dock collieries r--" I duly received your letter, which I beg to ackno*ledge. lam not aware, nor had I the re- motest idea of the case to which you refer. Had I known of any specific cases of intended injury to pro- perty or life I should not have hesitated one solitary moment in giving you the information. I may again repeat my sincere regret that at your colliery, either with or without official sanction, there should be offered so many insidious stratagems and inducements to irri- tate the men locked out, which are not adopted at other I collieries in the same condition ; and that I naturally feared the men under excitement would do desperate things. But from what I see in the Press you are not willing to close your pits until an honourable settlement of the wages question is come to, as other employers have done. I can, therefore, only express my deep re- gret.—Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>SAM. WOODS.</head>
        <p>SAM. WOODS.</p>
        <p>T. D. Grimke-Drayton, Esq. SURFACE DIGGINGS AT ITAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>A brisk trade has been done at the Flay dock surface coal diggings, and it is estimated that hundreds of tons of coal have been removed. The men have, however, been stopped.oa Monday four men who again attempted to sink a shaft were taken to the flaydock Police Office, but were allowed to go after their names had been taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PRECAUTIONS AT MESSRS. PILKINOTON'S.</head>
        <p>PRECAUTIONS AT MESSRS. PILKINOTON'S.</p>
        <p>*Since the incendiary fire at Messrs. Pllkington's strawyard, St. Helens, additional precautions have been taken by the firm to ensure the safety of other portions of their works. The extensive timber yard of the firm is nightly guarded by St. Helens and Liverpool con- stables, and a new steam pump has been fixed in the yard by the firm. The Liverpool foot police are en- gaged every night in guarding collieries and works.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTS ON MESSRS. RICHARD EVANS AND CO.'S WORKMEN.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon',;:fi-ie—tn—a-n. named Peter James was returning to his home at Edge-green, after working in the No. 1 Pit of the Golborne Collieries, belonging to Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Limited, when he was attacked by three men unknown,and brutally assaulted. When the attack was made James was crossing a field near Ashton-road, about a quarter of a mile from his house. He was struck and kicked about the body, and was subsequently left unconscious. He regained con- sciousness a short time after, and was able to crawl home. When he reached home he again lapsed in an un- conscious state. About seven o'clock Dr. Dobbs, of Golborne, arrived, and attended to the injuries. James's face was very much disfigured. His eyes were blackened 1 and his lips swollen. During the night he continued in a semi-conscious state. His condition was slightly im- proved on Tuesday morning. James had been suffering from neuralgia for five weeks, and had only returned to his work on Monday morning. On Saturday night, another fireman at the Golborne pit, named Joseph Hewitt, was assaulted, and later in the evening, at half-past eleven, an attack was made on his house, and several of the front windows smashed, but no one was injured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>*OMAN'S MEETING,</head>
        <p>A *OMAN'S MEETING,</p>
        <p>A " mass meeting of women " was held in St. James's Ball, London, on Tuesday evening, under the presidency of Mrs. Sidney Webb, and resolutions were passed expressing sympathy with the women and children in the mining districts. A sum of £255 was collected in the ball as the commencement of a relief fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MR. WOODS, M.P., AND THE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>MR. WOODS, M.P., AND THE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woods, M.P., states that he does not prop‘se to move the adjournment of the house on Friday, as he had intended. The Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation meets at Wigan on Monday, to receive the reports of meetings which have been held in reference to the recent joint meeting at Westminster. New develop- ments are expected, which may tend to a settlement of the dispute, and Mr. Woods feels that if he were to move tbe adjournment on Friday something might be said to aggravate the present position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SUTTON HEATII AND LEA. GREEN</head>
        <p>THE SUTTON HEATII AND LEA. GREEN</p>
        <p>_ COLLIERIES. A RUMOURED COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>It was stated in St. Helens on Thursday that the Sutton Heath and Lea Green Colliery Company, one of the largest firms in the district had offered 7s. per day for colliers and 6s. per day for drawers. On inquiry at the offices on Thursday afternoon it was stated that the officials of the company had left for the day, and neither confirmation nor contradiction of the rumour could be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CONFESSION OF MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CONFESSION OF MURDER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>STRANGE_ CONDUCT_ OF A _ST. HELENS MAN.</p>
        <p>- At the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, be- fore Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Messrs. William Pierpoint, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright, a man giving the name of Henry Littler was charged on his oven confession with drowning a man unknown at Shrewsbury, on February last. The man, Constable (Mr. S. Hunt) said that the prisoner walked into the police station on Saturday night and told Inspector Spinks that he wished to make a statement as there was something lying heavily upon his mind. In the presence of Inspector Spinks and Sergeant Chantler, he made the following statement ; " Police Station, Warrington, Nov. 4th, 1893 : Henry Littler, aged 36 years, skin cleaner, saith ; I wish to give myself up for having drowned a man (an oldish chap) in the canal near the Shrewsbury Workhouse, one Mon- day afternoon about nine months ago (about February last). We were going along the canal embankment, and we had a few hasty words, and he (the deceased man) called me a rotten b— and struck me in the face, and ' I threw him into the canal and walked away towards 1 the Workhouse, but I was too soon to get admitted, and I remained about for a few hours until the Workhouse opened for casuals. I slept that night in the casual ward there, and the body of the deceased man was taken out of the canal and taken into the Workbousethe same evening. I left the Workhouse the following morning. I have been indifferent towns working since, but I have not had any peace of mind since the above occurrence took place, and lam wishful to have the the matter inquired into. lam a native of St. Helens. (Signed). Henry Littler." Mr. Hunt said be had written to the Chief Constable of Shrewsbury, and had received the following letters in reply ;—" Chief Constable's Office, Shrewsbury. Re Henry Littler—Sir, I beg to acknowledge the receipt of your letter on the 4th inst., and the statement of the above respecting he having drowned a man about Feb. last. An old man was found drowned in the river Severn, near the Workhouse, about that time. The place where it occurred is outside the borough, therefore I have banded your letter and his statement over to the county police.-1 am, sir, your obedient servant, Henry Blackwell, Chief Constable." The following letter was received from the county police:—" County Constabulary Office, Shrewsbury, sth November, 1893. Re Henry Littler—Sir, lam directed by the Chief Constable to acknowledge the receipt of of your letter of the 4th inst. to the Shrewsbury borough , police, and to inform you that on the the 23rd July last the body of a man (unknown) was found in the river Severn between Shrewsbury and Atcham (in county) which body was same date removed to Atcham Work- ' house. From inquiries made I find that a tramp giv- ing the name of Henry Littler did stay in this Workhouse same nipht. I. may state that we are not aware of any man haying been found drowned in February, and the one I have already mentioned is the only body that has been removed to the Workhouse. We had no reason at the time to suspect that a murder had beefs committed, but the Chief Constable would be glad if you would kindly get Littler remanded for a few days, and in the meantime every inquiry shall be made made, and you shall hear further.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, S. Challoner, Chief Constable." Under the circumstances, Mr. Hunt applied for a re- mand until Friday. The prisoner was asked what he had to say, when to the surprise of the court, he said that ho had never com- mitted a murder, and that he was tired of knocking about when he made the statement. Mr. Hunt said that the prisoner was quite sober when he made the above statement, and sometime after, when ho was brought before (Mr. Hunt) he persisted in his statement. He would now have to answer the charge. Ihe magistrates granted the remand.</p>
        <p>The prisoner Littler was again brought before the magistrates yesterday morning. The Clerk (Mr. White) read a letter from the Shrewsbury County police stating that every enquiry bad been made by the police, but they had been unable to get any evidence agaiLst the prisoner, and asked that he might be discharged'. Mr. Hunt asked the magistrates to discharge the prisoner. Alderman Harrison the presiding magistrate said ho was sorry it was not in their power to give the prisoner three months hard labour, for making a confession of murder, when he knew perfectly well there was nothing in it. The prisoner made no reply, and was discharged,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>MRS. S. A. ALLEN'S</p>
        <p>WORLD'S</p>
        <p>HAIR RESTORER.</p>
        <p>ing, delicately parfutned.</p>
        <p>it is not a dye.</p>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Quickly changes gray or</p>
        <p>faded Lair to its natura</p>
        <p>co'our. A perfect hair dres</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>in the House of Commons on Monday evening, Mr. LABOUCHERE further questioned the Under Secretary for the colonies in regard to Matabeleland,and was informed that the Chartered Company were being notified that no action taken on the spot would prejudice any future arrangement which might be come to. The PRE3IIER informed the hon. member that the Government had no intention of proposing to the House to devote a day to a discussion on the affairs of Matabeleland, where upon Mr. LABOUCHERE gave notice that at the earliest opportunity he should take the usual steps to obtain t day. Mr. GLADSTONE made a statement as to the Govern ment programme for the present sittings. He said they felt it to be their duty to confine business as far as pos- sible to the passing of the Local Go,,ernment Bill and the Employers' Liability Bill, and to the final disposal of those Government bills which were passed through the House in the earlier part of the session. They pro- posed to reserve the power of taking any non-conten- tious business of pressing necessity. He thought they were excluded by their agreement with the House from dealing with the question of the evicted Irish tenants. Mr. J. MORLEY intormed Mr. Sexton that he should be prepared to bring in a measure to revive the thirteenth section of the Land Act of 1891 for a time, provided the proposal received the assent of the Opposition. Mr. BALFOUR SAM he could not give an opinion on what was a fragmentary part of a larger policy. Tne debate on the motion for the second reading of the Local Government Bill was resumed by Mr. ACLAND. Mr. A. J. Be,roon criticised the Bill. He thought it did not contain the necessary safeguards against extrava- gant expenditure,and he declared himself opposed to the poor law portion of it. He thought, however, that if they discussed the bill in the tone of Mr. Fowler and Mr. Acland they might be able to turn it into a real and substintial improvement upon the existing system ot local administration. The debate was continued till midnight.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening several further questions were ad lressed to the Under Secret- ary for the Colonies in regard to recent events in South Africa. Mr. Emmet said there had not yet been time to receive a detailed despatch as to the death of Lobengula's envoys. As soon as he got any information he would communicate it to the House. In reply to Mr. TI Bowles, Mr. GLADSTONE said Her Majesty's Government were pqrfectly satisfied as to the adequacy and capacity of the Mediterranean fleet for the purposes for which it existed, and they had no in- tention of proposing a day for the discussion of the subject. The right hon. gentleman in answering a question respecting arbitration in labour disputes, said lie could not hold out any idea of the Government concurring in a plan of compulsory arbitration. He could not see how it could be made applicable in the present dispute in the coal trade, and he was afraid that to attempt it might cause a re-action unfavourable to atbitration itself. Mr. T. BOWLES returned to the subject of the attitude of the British warships during the blockade of Bangkok, and Sir E. GREY repeated that the ships received no " order" to move outside the blockade lines, and that no such demand would. have been admitted if made. - . .</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. A HONER informed Mr. Lough that a case of pleurn-prwomonia had occurred in the Metropolitan Cattle Market. The ii. 11,e again resumed the debate on the motion for the second reading of the Local Government (Parish Councils) Bill. Sir G. 0. MORGAN complained that charities which were only incidentally ecclestastieal and substantially civil were by the definition clauses taken out of the operation of the Bill. Mr. Gosam and Sir W. HARCOURT spoke towards midnight. The latter ' hon. gentleman said the Government could not throw over the poor-law part of the measure. The Bill was read a second time without a division.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons im Wednesday, on the order for the consideration of the Employers' Liability Bill as amended by the Standing Committee on Law, Mr. Tom- linson moved that the bill be recommitted to a Com- mittee of the whole House, on the ground of the great importance of the measure. Sir. E. Hill seconded the amendment, and Mr. Matthews appealed to the Hovern- meat to accede to it. Mr. Aaq,uitli said acceptance of the amendment weuld be fatal to the passing of the bill during the present session. Sir E.. Clark.. supported the Government. Mr. A. J. Balfour left the responsibility in this matter to the Government, and said he would not advise his hon. friend to divide the House. Leave to withdraw the amendment was refused, and it was negativ d. The House then proceeded to consider the bill as amended. Mr. W. M'Laren moved a new clause providing that contracts in certain cases should be ex- empted from the application of secton 2, which provides against contracting out beforehand, his desire being, he said, to strengthen the bill by preserving mutual insur- ance funds. Mr. Cobb seconded the new clause. Mr. Asquith strongly opposed the amendment. fie said the wosking classes had indicated their opinion that a bill that did not safeguard them against negligence in the conduct of their employers and did not protect them from improvident contracts would not satisfy their re- quirements. The debate was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>THURSDAY..</head>
        <p>THURSDAY..</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday evening Mr. GLADSTONE informed Mr. DODD that the LORD CHAN- CELLOS wan in many cases acting on the recommenda- tion ot the House respecting the appointment 01 county magistrates. Mr. LABOCCHERE moved the adjournment of the House in order to discuss "the impolioy of permitting the Chartered Company of South Africa to establish any claims to contract any engagements with regard to the territory or government of Matabeleland, or to continue its warlike operations In that territory." The hon. mem- ber went into the history of the Company, and said that its attack on Matabeleland had been undertaken to te- trieve its fortunes, and that it was the duty of the Plums Mt \ ism to say that henceforth all that was done should be done with justice, mercy, and humanity. Mr. J. Ettis seconded the motion. Mr. MAGUIRE, as one connected with the Company,defended its proceedings. and said the war was now practically over. Mr. Burrox said he believed the war was not of the Company's seeking, and certainly it was not of the Government's seeking. Until lie could show that one single man was killed more than was necessary to ensure victory Mr. Labouchere ought not to speak of a mas- sacre. Hostilities were now practically at an end, and the Government would take advantage of any opportu- nity to bring the war to a speedy conclusion. Public feeling in South Africa could not be ignored, but the settlement which the Government would endeavour to. I bring about would include all safeguards of the rights of the natives. Mr. titenscons spoke to similar effect,' and severely censured Mr. Labouchere for his use of extravagant language. The motion fey adjournment was negatived without a division. The House then resumed consideration of the Employers' Liability Bill as amended, and Mr. W. M'Laren's " contracting-out" clause was further dis- cussed. Just before the House rose Sir A. RoLur moved for the appointment of a Select Committee to inquire into th,e coal trade dispute. Sir W. llarcourt was pointing out the serious consequeneoi which such an inquiry would involve when by the Standing Orders the debate stood adjourned.</p>
        <p>The House of Lords assembled nn Thursday evening after the recess. The Margui3 of RIPON read a telegram from Sir H. Loch communicatin7, a message from Colonel Goold-A.dams which gave the Information that Pthe column from the cast was in possession of Bulu- wayo, and that Lobengula, and also Gambo and his large imp/. had fled.</p>
        <p>"CADBURY'S COCOA has, in a remarkable degree, those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endur- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders It a. tn et acceptable and reliable beverage."—ikalfh.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rider Haggard is busy writing at Ditchingham, his place at Norfolk. His eyes being delicate, he dictates most of his work to a lady secretary, who furnbhes a rough draft in type-writing, which the author ccrrects. in this way he is able to get through a large amount of work without much strain.</p>
