      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>BITESHIUE VOLUNTEEU BATTALIONS.</head>
        <p>BITESHIUE VOLUNTEEU BATTALIONS.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner of the 3rd and 4th Battalions of the Cheshire Regiment was held in the White- hall Rooms, Metropole Hotel, London, on Friday evening. Among those present were—Lieutenant- Colonel Hill, Lieutenant-Colonel Read, Sir Craven Goring, Major G. F. Scott, Captain Herbert Arm- strong. Sir Philip Egerton, Captain Lees, Captain Lea Jones, Captain Williamson, Mr. Howard, Mr. Mikes. Mr. Read, Mr. Greg, Mr. Brooke. Mr. Dods- worth,</p>
        <p>AN AMICALLE SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>A case came up at the Wa-Wi-t)--g-Coi;—.COunty Court on Thursday, ' before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, in which Mr. Appleton, junr. and others, Newton-le-Willows,sued Messrs.Borron and Co.,glass I bottle manufacturers, Newton-le-Willows, for wages I and damages for wrongful dismissal. Mr. Mull- ' holland instructed by Mr. H. L. Riley, who appeared for Appleton, said that the claim was brought by workmen against. Messrs. Borron and Co.,owing to a dispute which had arisen between employers and plaintiffs. There was also a counter claim against the workmen for not going to work, but the two parties bad come to an agreement for plaintiffs for £22 13s. 4d. without costs. Mr. J. 0. Swift who appeared for Messrs. Borron and Co. said that his client had agreed to the judgment, and withdrew the counter-claim.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. NOVEMBER 11. 1893.</p>
        <p>Dr the debate on the Employers P:mPl°Y"s Liability Bill in Parliament on Liability. Wednesday, Mr. ASQUITH declared the position the Government had decided to take with regard to "contracting out." As was gener- ally anticipated would be the case, the decision of the Government is against exempting any particu- lar industrial concern from the operation of the Act. Mr. ASQUITH'S speech clearly showed that this conclusion had not been lightly come to. Due weight had evidently been given to the arguments which could be advanced in favour of special treatment being accorded to the London and North-Western Railway Company and a number of other large employers of labour, who contribute liberally towards the insurance of their workmen against accidents, fatal or otherwise. It was admitted that in several instances the advantages which are derived from these special insurance arrangements are greater than what may be expected from the Act, subject, as Mr. ASQUITH pointed out, to the fact that in case of death the Act is likely to give greater benefits to the rela- tives than any of the funds which had been referred to in the debate. But, as far as this goes, it can hardly be disputed that the workmen are the best judges of their own interests, and a considerable proportion of the employ 6s of the London and North-Western Railway Company seem to be clearly of the opinion that existing arrangements are the best for them. There were, however, much weightier considerations which the Government could not ignore. The Act is to be of general application, and, therefore, it ailects workingmen in all parts of the country, and in a great diversity of trades and occupations. The Trades Union Congress,speaking for the combined workmen of the whole country, passed the follow- ing resolution unanimously :—" That the Congress heartily approves of the Employer's Liability Bill</p>
        <p>now before Parliament, containing, as it does, the principal points for which it has contended for years, amongst the most important of these being the provision against contracting out of the Act, without which no measure will be acceptable to the organised workers of the kingdom." And even the railway workers of the kingdom, so far as they are combined in trades unions, have declared against the contracting out proposals. The Government can, therefore, fairly claim that, in sticking out for "the Bill, the whole Bill and nothing but the Bill" they are acting up to the trades union sentiment of the country. Then, again, there is no reason whatever why the work- men of the London and North-Western Railway Company should net go on enjoying all the advantages of their own system of insurance. There is nothing in the new Bill that will render that system illegal. It has been said that the directors have threatened that if the Employer's Liability Bill passes as it is now framed they will cease to contribute as they have done in times past to the insurance fund. If that threat has been made, it would, we venture to suggest, not be advisable to take it too seri- ously? "For," asked Mr. ASQUITH, "what is the incentive to the employers to contribute to an in- surance fund of this kind? It cannot be to escape the payment of these insignificant claims. It is rather to gather the whole of the persons in their employ within a sort of industrial ring fence, so as to exclude all outside influence, which might sow dissension between master and men, and to secure to the railway companies such a hold over their men as will 'make them think once, twice, and thrice, before they resort to strikes. In fact, the contribution may be regarded as an insurance against strikes as much as an insurance against accident." We believe that, in the passage we have quoted, Mr. ASQUITH has thoroughly guaged the policy of the railway companies. Now, the inducement to insure agaiust strikes will be just as powerful after the passage of this Employers Liability Bill as it is now, and, therefore, there is good reason for supposing that the railway men will continue to receive all the benefits of their insurance system, while the general body of workers throughout the country will receive the m-ich-needed protection of the Bill.</p>
        <p>TUE conference between coal-owners The Coal and miners, from which so much was war. hoped, has been held, and to all ap- pearance, has brought us no nearer to a settle- ment of the most disastrous industrial conflict of the century. It seems to be now generally taken for granted that the dispute can only be settled by the defeat—more or less complete—of one or or the the two contending parties. This, to say the least of it, is a gloomy outlook. Neither side, as yet, is showingsigns of that exhaustion which must precede an unconditional surrender. The miners have, during the week, held meetings in all parts of the Federation district in which the lock-out is still maintained, and they display an unbroken unanimity in resisting any reduction in their wages. On the side of the employers there have been a few more pits re-opened at the old rate of wages, and there are rumour of further secessions. Whether these rumours will be rea- lised during the corning week remains to be seen. There is, of course, a strong and ever-present temptation to colliery proprietors—and parti- cularly the smaller proprietors—to re-open their pits and secure some of the golden advan- tages that are being reaped through the present great " boom" in prices. That some of the larger owners, tho ringleaders, so to speak, in .the movement which has brought about the present state of affairs, will hold out to the bitter end may be taken as certain. These men cannot rid them- selves of the enormous responsibilities they have incurred, nor overlook the very peculiar situation in which they have placed themselves. Four months ago they declared that miners wages must be reduced by the startling amount of twenty-five per cent. —subsequently explained to mean twenty five off forty per cent.—and that they would bo content with nothing less. That demand involved almost everything that has followed—all the hunger and accumulated miseries of hundreds of thousands of workmen who are dependent for em- ployment, directly or indirectly, upon coal. The miners were thoroughlyorganised,and it was known with absolute certainty that they would offer an obstinate and prolonged resistance to the demand for so large a reduction. Such a pronounced act of aggression against workmen's wages, entailing as it did, so many disastrous consequences, could, therefore, only be justified by the imperative neces- sities of the coal trade. The leaders in the mas- ters' movement contended that those necessities existed, and their continuance of the lock-out is one way of trying to prove that they were in the right.. For them to discontinue the lock-out would, in its way, be an admission that the demand for a large reduction of wages was not warranted, and that in fact the whole affair had been what some people do not hesitate to declare it to be—an attempt to make profit out of accu- mulated stocks, or to break down the Miners Federation. Seeing that this is the position of some of the larger mine-owners, it is to be feared that, as far as they are concerned, they will hold out till the last moment. At the same time it is to be noted that something like one hundred thou- sand miners are now again employed at the old rate of wages. Every pit that opens adds to this number, and, through the levies that are made, to the power of the unemployed miners to stare off that starvation which, is the only weapon by which the masters can enforce submission. Once it becomes evident that the miners can carry on the contest indefinitely from their own resources, it must be apparent to even the most determined of the masters that, so far as the lock-out goes, his " game is up," and that he is serving no useful purpose by keeping his mines closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>DREADFUL SUFFERINGS OF THE POOR.</head>
        <p>DREADFUL SUFFERINGS OF THE POOR.</p>
        <p>SOME SAMME CASES. BY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>The other day I was afforded an opportunity of seeing into the genuineness of many cases of dis- tress. It came about through accompairving, on a visitation, one of the generous lady workers, who are toiling with such earnest zeal in connection with the Relief Committee. It is said that many people are refusing to subscribe to the Relief Fund, because they think "too much fuse, is being made about people being out of work a week or two." So it seemed to me that a description of two or three typical cases of distress might impress more em- phatically upon these sceptical persons the horror of that all pervading combination of sickness, and poverty, and hunger, which is remorselessly affect- ing the homes of hundreds of our townspeople.</p>
        <p>It was a dull day, cold and damp, with no sun- shine to dissipate a keen frost of the previous night—no sunshine to give one ray of warmth to I the dozens of half dressed children who loitered shiveringly about Town's End, as we passed that ' uusalubrious spot. In a few moments we bad passed along a narrow entry, and were standing in a square, dim, dilapidated court. Low-roofed houses with broken windows, broken spouts, broken down steps, and broken doors surrounded us. Above, the sight of God's high firmament was almost hidden by overshadowing warehouses and chimneys. What trifle of fresh air did manage to get lost and steal its way into the court, was quickly polluted by the sickening stenches which arose from the dilapidated privies, whose hideous presence filled the centre of the court. A pace or two brought us to a door of one of the dwellings. The door stood slightly ajar. This enabled me to notice that the step receded some inches into the room and allowed all the rain and liquid mire to dribble into the apartment. We paused for a moment before knocking.</p>
        <p>The. piteous wail of a fretful child was audible. Then came half smothered words of comfort from a woman—a pause—and then a lisping little voice, full of woe and pain said " Slamee Foe so cold—so cold marnee." The lisping voice broke into sobs; the infant's fretful cry was raised again. We entered. But what a picture of human dejection, of human misery in all its Intensity, was before us I The room was about six feet by six and about six feet high. The walls were bare, even of paper; the room was bare of any furniture, save two boxes (which hadn't any claim on the name); the floor was of cement, dirty and uncovered. The atmosphere was dim and cold; there was no fire in the grate. A woman with a child at her breast rose from a box when we entered. She wore a ragged dress which she said was her only article of clothing. Iler cheeks were sunken and pale; her eyes protruded with the ghastliness of starvation, but they were flooded with tears—tears evidently of suffering and hopelessness, tears indeed, that must have been wrung from her mother's heart by the sight of her children's privations. For she had four of them with here. One was clinging to her with his right hand whilst his left was raised, in the act of brush- ing two pearly settings from two blue eyes, which were now forgetting to cry, in their wonderment. This child's feet were bare, and must have been numbed with the chilliness of the cement floor. Another child, perhaps about eleven, was crouched up inn dark corner of the room. She was induced to stand forth in,to the light. And then we saw that her face appeared quite bloodless and emaciated. The cheek bones protruded with distressing promin- ence, the lips, blue with cold were drawn back tightly over ths teeth, the eyes were sunken and lacked lustre, whilst the little head with its wealth of dirty flaxen hair, drooped with weakness. She only showed a momentary interest in our presence —then relapsed into a dozed, numbed condition, her head sinking between her knees, her cold naked feet and legs crouching together for warmth, and her fragile little form convulsively shivering with cold. Then there was another, a little boy—about six—who, though apparently pinched more than was good for him, had not yet lost his juvenile courage. For upon seeing me step towards his elder sister, he came between us and stoutly ex- claimed:—</p>
        <p>" You not doing to take Aladdin to school —den't fry to. Dladdie's got the tummy ache and no clothes, and you shan't take her."</p>
        <p>Ile evidently mistook me for another gentleman. His precocious courage however, even brought a melancholy smile to the mother's face, who assured him I was not the gentleman "who takes naughty boys to school"; whereupon this lively" olive brunch" grew friendly and tried to climb up my legs.</p>
        <p>The mother told us her husband, a labourer, had been out of employment 12 weeks. They had pawned their clothing, and the best of their furni- ture, the remainder they had broken up for fuel. For four days now, they have been without fire,and without bedding. They had passed the long cold nights crouched together in that room, whilst their only source of sustenance in the day time had been the soup and bread from the Relief Committee. As the Relief Committee only give coal twice to siok. people—my lady friend had to refuse a " coal ticket ;" but the case was such a lamentable one that she paid for a cwt. of fuel out of her own purse—and promised some clothes for the children. And how grateful that poor starving woman was I She tried to express her gratude, but it only amounted to a few poor disjointed words, followed by an outburst of tears more eloquent than the most finely turned of phrases could be.</p>
        <p>A few moments later we stood in a similar court, before a dwelling of similar construction. Again there was a house empty of furniture, except fora table. But there was a fire in the grate this time, and it looked cheerful. A little woman greeted my lady friend with fevid expressions of thankfulness. It seems that a fortnight ago when she was first visited this little woman was found lying on a bed of straw, with only a thin print dress on and 110 underclothing. She was too weak to rise. Priva- tion coming upon a recent confinement had placed her just on the edge of the Chasm of Death. Another day, and probably she would have sue- combed. But the lady who was visiting her had instantly had fuel, beef tea, bread, and soup sent, with some clothes from her own wardrobe. And now the recipient was able to get about her house again. It was plain she was no impostor—her sincere womanly gratitude simply seemed sacred in its depth. She said she was quite recovered now, wherein I did think she was guilty of a little grateful exaggeration because her chalky white face, and panting breath said " No " with a sad eloquence which overruled the " Yes "of her brave words and smile. At the table was her hus- hand,very meagrely clad,very quiet,very depressed. Ile was making a " quilt " for his wife to sleep under, his materal being bits of old cast-off soldiers tunic, bits of leather, and rags. He had been out of work sixteen weeks, had only had one " turn " with the Corporation and went out every day,with an empty stomach and .a hopeless heart, to seek employment—which he had never found. He was a teetotaller, and an intelligent mat), with appar- ently a great affection for his sick little wife and the tour children "who eat chief part of my soup," as he admitted. The same story of how destitution had gradually inverted its horrible presence in this humble domicile was told. There was a family of six—including tour small children. The bread- winner's earnings averaged 18s. a week. When this pittiance ended sixteen weeks ago the furni- ture, the clothing, and everything available had to be pawned. The fire had to be made only when the children could collect enough scraps of wood. When the mother was discovered Efter her confine- ment she had only a shawl to cover her child, only a print dress to cover herself, and zio food whatever that she could eat.</p>
        <p>, • Proceeding along the court we arrived at another house—no, better call it dwelling. 14e knocked. There was no answer—only the moan of a child in pain. We entered. But it was the same picture again. No fire, no furniture—no anything but a cold, wet flag floor, and bare dirty crumbling walls. A child of three lay on some straw only half covered with a ragged gown. It was almost deliri- ous with what was evidently an attack of measles. We loitered for a few moments. Then a girl of 14 or 15 came in. She was dressed in a sack, which simply served as a covering of common decency. for down the side where it was tied together with twine, the child's body was clearly exposed. She said her father had been out of work 15 weeks— and since "Poppy" had had the measels he went out 88 early as five in the morning to hunt wood for the fire, " but Dad says there's hundreds on the same look out, and he couldn't get no more'n handful." There were three other young children who were</p>
        <p>without shoes, so the girl said' and they had gc'n: to a free school to be warm." The mother Was ou, at the Drill Shed hunting food. They hadn't hait any bedding for a month, and as the girl sai " d keep ourselves warm, by crouching round dad 031" maim, at nights." There had never been a peall piece in the family for a month. hag°.</p>
        <p>The next six visits we made were all to similarly destitute, destitute, where mothers had be Qe confined within the last fortnight. In each there had been neither fuel, food, bedding nor the most necessitous clothing in the house for confinements. All the cases were within a co; parative small distance of the cases I have tioned. An account of one will be a fair portra3of of the others. We passed through a creaky 'Ode, into another dark kitchen. There was a bed 111"'ed up on the floor, the blankets having been suppli by the Relief Committee. It was the only thing P Ire the room. Upon it lay a youny mother—no 113°1d than I9—with her child. She declared she w!)-80 have died had it not been for what the corninitf had done for her,for the day she was confined thenrcl was no fire in the grate, no clothes on her bed, not a morsel of food in the house, exeept the S °Aro which she could not eat. She called upon Go' as the most fervid of tones to bless the " people ,5 putting themselves about to help us poor. husband sat in a corner. He seemed to be no 111 Le than 20. On his knee was another child. Like: husbands in all the other cases he could not could only offer his thanks iq the crudest' i But t seemed awful to see strong young men, whom hunger had told an emaciating tale, their suffering, their torture, their self-st with silent, dogged resignation, never silo' a word to pass their lips to betoken the anginal' agony of their pains and woes. But how much pitiful was it to see the wasted forms of the mothers facing their privations with pat silence !—But this weak woman can only be till° her beef tea and fuel this week—then, maYhe% days and nights, aye, for wee;:s, they will ha" live on soup, with little or no fire. And that man will have to spend the cold nights crouch' a corner of a damp cold room, hugging his baby to give it warmth. And it the funds not hold out—then what ?</p>
        <p>Space will not allow me to go on further these pictures of the dreadful sufferings of the people of Warrington. There are hundreds of —many worse, if that can be, than those I mentioned, many where people who have 1 been destitute before,are now in the throes of vation!</p>
