        <p>HER CHILD FOUND ON A DOORSTEP' At the Warrington Borough Court on Tuq° morning, Ellen Lawton, a widow, residing I° house off Ilaydock-st., was charged with drunk and disorderly at twenty minutes to that morning, and also with unlawfully exposing child, Mary Ellen aged seven years, in a manner to cause injury GO its health. Prisoner pleaded —lnspector Cox said at 12.30 that morning, pany with Sergeant Monaghan he was in Winwlc, near the Blue Coat School. They found the child sit on a doorstep. They ;awakened the people up house, but they said they knew nothing of the girl afterwards found that the girl belonged to the prO,Dei and lived about 300 yards from the place where,',o3 found her. It was a very gold night, and the chihinee9 ' starved, and w a.s poorly clad. On reaching the pris°,:mllit house they had to knock some time before thcY chili get her up to let the girl in. They had to pull the into the house, as she said she was frightened to Poief and asked to be taken from her mother. The The ho,r was drunk at the time, and was sleeping 96.°) 00 clothes on. They took the girl to the Police Stat)9l3, for got some coffee for her. She said she was 100130°300 " Blind Joe," who was a relative.—D.S. Monaghan.,ol3 he was in company with Inspector Cox. They f°"thero can half, full of beer in the house. The child told .oere that her mother was in bed " skenning" drunk. /TO was only an old sheet in the house for bed clothing'el •e p t woman was a widow, and worked at fustian cu, tbe The Chief Constable said the woman was one 3`„oed recipients of the soup ever since the kitchen was ol,` bet in the Liwn.—Mr. R. Garnett: And very punctua!Oti attendance I suppose too.—ln answer to a queBnooo, Mr. Garnett as to where she got the money for the dried she said a neighbour had given her the beer. She 0,.,°,r gtl • the previous week and earned 83. 10d., and paid tb. a week in rent for two rooms. The magistrates Sen prisoner to gaol for 14 days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY. eir Wednesday and Thursday evening next, ber 15th and 16th, are the datea of the per 10t1' ances to be given by the members of this soc—s J under the patronage and presence of the and Mayoress, in aid of the debt on the GP;dy iuni Building. Each evening the celebrated C 0,131000 " The Money Spinner," by one of the cleverest an„"„erot popular af modern playwrights, Mr. Arthur W. O r'rrce, will be played, preceded on Wednesday by the dsi " Freezing a Mother-in-law," and on Tholcel• by the comedietta. "Uncle's Will." An „enlor lent stage has been erected in the u),.;lice, sium, where the performances are to take the dimensions being as follows :—Acting space. 1711 wide by 201 t. deep ; extent of scenery, 34ft. wide de high ; proscenium, outside dimensions, 30ft• Winnare‘ 211 t. high, leaving an opening of 231 t., which colL4tre; very favourably with the size of any ordinary „riea The proscenium has been specially designed, and ~erlatil,°,„ out by Mr. John Hesketh, the well-known dee— rcia". artist, and the workmanship reflects the greatest coell on him. The style adopted is nvzantian. Tvlle modelled pilasters surmount a handsome and ob at each side with frieze,en which the Royal Arms areA tiod beautifully executed. The whole has been paiUtttØ decorated byhand,and presents a verymassive and 1 nent appearance, in fact to all intents and purPl,4tra building will be for the time being,practically a b` With reference to the cast, the female characters waiter,' undertaken by very talented lady amateurs, inebo are of the leading Liverpool Dramatic Societies, most kindly giving their services, and We belie": 00 1° correct in stating that this will be the first oc°°— Dee° the history of the society that these parts haVoter nu 0115 solely sustained by amateurs. Several of the 01" ola bets of the Dramatic Society will sustain t" tbeir characters, and several new members will maltf°l3olcl' bow to* a Warrington audience. Mr. Pearson, "ducts% street, has kindly undertaken the duties of On faraV of the orchestra, and both he and the membersceitot hag, it are giving their services. All round an • 005 perfermance may be looked forward to, and a 5 ,!: plays are are not produced without a very consideralo lay being involved, we trust that a large audi 0111 enc'infr reward the efforts of the members, and that a cong'o" able reduction will be made in the debt on the 76,4 slum. The comfort of the audience will be st° a, aau every way. The room will be thoroughly warme,„asere seated so as to enhance, as far as possible, the ra.`t ro. of those attending. Seats may now be bookeud Pearson's for the dross stalls, second seats, a°</p>
