        <p>ST. JOUR'S BAND OF ROPE, BI7NCORN. aoV? first public meeting of the seaqon was held in St. " School-nom, Runcorn, on Wednesday evening. to_e chair was taken by Mrs. E. Wynne-Humphreys, ii..!°,l.,'Poke to those present on the benefits of temperance. T. Cooke also gave a short address. tak,:n want your WATCH BAPLIRED by skilled workmen C RD 'a Adtt. • r, vEATH OF AN OLD WOMAN AT RUNCORN'. Wednesday morning, Elizabeth Jenkins, aged 79 Shah' 5:0, Norton-street, Runcorn, died very suddenly. h'acli3een into the house of a neighbour and returned da,"`"ei sat down on the sofa, .and requested her grand- aftueghter to prepare her a cup of tea. She immediately hourwards leaned back, and died within a quarter of an before medical attendance could be obtained. st.' 141 Pnrrcirsain Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, SU DEATII AT WIDNES. , Mr, land], • `o• ugh- Brown, chemist, and son of Mrs. Brown, the Castle Hotel, Widnes, died very suddenly d mot. He was apparently sai unclao • in good health, deyartaking of a hearty supper, when he sud- werri 'ell 'forward and expired without uttering a Lr. MacLennan has certified that heart disease fro- 'le cause of death. The deceased had only returned p:111 America on the previous Tue:diy, and much sym- Y is felt 'for his bereaved relatives and friends. The RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD. hon„ Inentidy meeting was held on Wednesday after- se,it"tat the office, the Cemetery, when there were pro- Messrs. T. Handley (chairman), Coleburn, Taylor, sta4lB, Littler, and W. E. Salkeld (secretary). The Oc-tZent showed that during the seven months ending: vith ner 25th there had been 322 interments, compared to, '62 in the same period last year, or an increase of to z2;he fees received for the same months amounted esi 13s. against £268 03. .81. last year, increase "s• 4d. ,a/re "44 s have now stood the test for fifty years, and deitt.Y and tiret class workmanship are still guaranteed.—</p>
        <p>4t the DUTTON SCROOL BOARD. Under , monthly meeting, held on Saturday afternoon, it was ',Pee _presidency of the Rev. L. D. Lyon Bennett for a suu. oided to levy a rate on the overseers of the poor nated cost of carrying on the Dutton School dur- , The est.tu of £9 i 4, this sum representing 2d. n the £. frig thou - current hall-wear was £lB5, hut after taking b for grant% an. the ba fee •atic .• e is the treasurer's ands the , . Sell I sum of £B5 was the sum calculated upon. The iloors magter reported that the number of scholars on his Dast Zoss nand that the average attendance during the , nth had reached 95 per cent., which speaks very tor the children. „ IMB9Ctra FROM D•RaWNING AT' RUNCORN. LON nc" Midnight on Saturday, Mr. Wm. Powell, land- the Bull's Head, Halton-road, heard screams,and can;i'ul! outside he saw a woman struggling in the , „: Without hesitation he mounted the wall and , Vor into the water, and succeeded in reaching the icrd as atd.seely placing her on the towing path. It oter;lerwards found that the woman was little the , the her immersion. Some doubt exists as to how lOat latnao got itrto the canal. Mr. Powell is to be con- )s4•ated upon his promptitude and bravery. The L and had to descend to reach the water is precipitious uangwous.</p>
