        <p>The 9 RUNCORN WAKES. Rrnunr,' Walk's commenced on Monday, when the fair the auai was occupied by shows and hobby hors" and all paraphranalia. On Tuesday a ship canal Atari' named Michael Sharkey, and a woman named and" ~""Ila Jones, were charged with disorderly conduc Seri:fre each sentenced to 14 dais hard labour. dent Atherton described the woman as an atre..i.V' for the wakes. A similar importation v ''farY Robi hnson was sentenced to seven days taerd 414':fre-z!'r beine drunk and disorderly and to fourteen n cribeci assaulting P.C. Gregory. T " ad drunk" and the ohffie womancersa w said she had ,ctelar; des- "B, mad attacked him in the police station. Messrs. ReYnolds and James Handley were the maps- At Rl:Neoax- SONS or TEMPERANCE. ,Ilinollneratrtlheeoing of this society, on Saturday evening. f Prejdell f Mr. James Johnson, the discus- Tho PerleY. at aPepr " previous mi‘ui re .browrriaa Clarke ,-,- letrl° "IC Pa c- ogam.lfhinego,ngreavc,lssb n, Smith, hneGd thegaedesb.ate 'in whcyiocnhMrtiiuessrs. D. Sutton, Edward Hey, j* and Tenoorrnas Cooke joined. It was held , ?trld afT;Peakers that the evil was of great magnitude. !. LCed every rank of society. Roth young and e it• The practice was wickal and sinful. ,setlFoll enacc°nlfield had saidm that the turf was a vat . 4Utv "' national d oralisation. It was therefore them waag„Z w" against gambling spirit. Ahearty—Per. th—ss Was accorded to Mr. femperley for his ,r,\VtSLIP 4he , 20E5IGN MISSION SERVICES AT RIINCOM 4414 in -.lllntlal sermons in aid of the Wesleyan Foreign th:ab were preached in S. Paul's Chapel, on Sunday, larp, Thom is Allen of Manchester. There was tli;ec:nrigregatio'n at each service. On Monday even- -4°11118; the „Is nual public meeting was held in the chapel. ""sftlee of Alderman Collier, of Manchester, Mr. conci-.PsOn presided. The Rev. W. Arrowsinith read ;R..% "11°1:ensa.t1on of the general connexional report. Mr. 110, the gee kra. Wed tilat the lowing read the local report, which