      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>1 THE VACANCY IN LATCHFORD WARD. The Conservatives have selected Mr. Percy Davies es their candidate for the vacancy caused in the Lttehford Ward, by the " elevation " of Coun- cillor Ashton to the rank of alderman. STRETTON CHORISTERS' TREAT. 'On Saturday, the choir boys connected with St. Matthew's Church, Stretton, were entertained at the Vicarage to an excellent tea. In the evening a dis- play of fireworks took place in the grounds. SUCCESSFUL WARRINGTON STUDENT. At the Classical examination held at Oxford last week,the results of which have just been published, Mr. Richard Fennelly, Owens College, Manchester, late scholar of the Warrington Grammar SchooLt as been awarded a classical exhibition at Corpus Christi tenable for four years. THE WARRINGTON DEBATING SOCIETY. A meeting of this society was held on Tuesday night at the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, Mr. Kent in the chair. In the absence of Mr. Horton, Mr. H. Nield reopened the debate on "the labour question" which was the subject of debate a fortnight prev- iously. Mr. Nield discussed at length the subjects of trades unionism, profit sharing. etc. An interest- ing discussion followed in which Messrs. Gandy, Caldercot. Haley and Coancillor Bennett took part. UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED,—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Adv'. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT HAIRDRESSERS' ASSOCIATION. The monthly meeting of the above was held in the Cocoa Room Ilorsemarket-st.,on Tuesday night There was a good attendance of members and the president Mr. J. McClew, occupied the chair. The business was of the ordinary character. Some con- versation took place as tolhe distress prevailing in the town owing to the lock-out in the coal trade, and it was unanimously agreed that a donation of XI, be forwarded to the Mayor (Alderman B. ,Greenall) for the Distress Fund. WARRINGTON WWEFLERS' "WALTZ." The Warrington Wheelers' Cycling Club held in the Co-operative gall, Sankey-st., on Monday even- ing a " waltz " or ball, to commemorate its fourth year of existence. There was a good attendance, and dancing was indulged in merrily to the strains of Mr. Waterfield's band. Some lime light and sha- dow dances were gone through, Mr. Kiernan offi- ciating with the lantern. Mr. W. Baniber made an excellent M.C. The company included contingents from the Manchester Wheelers, Leigh Athletic and other clubs. STOVES.—GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington. THE FIRM OF THE SESSION. The first monthly entertainment of the winter season was held in St. George's Mission on Monday night, when there was a crowded audience, and the proceedings passed off most pleasantly. Pianoforte solos were given by Miss Ridgway, violin solos by Miss Wilcox, musical sketches by Mr. Whittle, and songs by the Rev. A. A. Markham, Mrs. Atkin, Mr. Cliffe, and Miss McCarthy. The quartet party and the St. George's Mission Minstrel Band gave several performances. The proceeds are to be devoted towards furnishing the Sunday school with Bibles. UMBRELLAS RE-COVERBD.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-St. Wnrrllgtofl.A rt. WYCLIFFE XOUNG PEOPLE'S SOCIETY. There was a well-attended meeting of the Wycliffe Young People's Society on Monday even- ing. Mr. R. Wyllie occupied the chair. Two essays were given on Handwriting v. Typewriting. Mr. F. Longshaw took the former subject and Mr. W. Booth the latter. The papers were excellent, and the tests on the typewriter, illustrating its ad- vantages were varied and effective. An interesting di,cussion took place afterwards on the subjects, and on labour saving machines in general. A vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Fleming for the loan of the typewriter, and a pleasant and profitable even- ing was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>TRH DORTON CYCLE COMPANY. W. Chetwood Burton, of the Burton Cycle Co , came up for his adjourned examination at the Was:- rington Bankruptcy Court before Mr. Registrar Ridgway, yesterday. Mr. Edgar, solicitor, Man- chester, continued the examination and witness said that about January, 1892, he advertised in the Manchester "Guardian" for a partner with a capital of £1,500, and Mr. Mellor, of Southport, was recommended to him by Messrs. Langhurst and Barker, of Southport The advertisement stated that the partner was to receive a half of the profits. , Mellor eaid that he could not arrange for £1,500 but he offered to put £5OO or £6OO in the business. It was finally arranged that he should put £5OO in the business and take a third of the profits instead of a half. An agreement was then prepared. Us (Mr. Burton) paid five per cent. commission to Messrs. Langhurst and Barker upon the £5OO. Mellor left the business in April or May this year. During the time he was a partner he had equal powers with the debtor in his own department. He had told Mr. Smethurst that a gentleman was coming into the business with some capital. Mr. Smethurst never interfered in the business, and he never represented that Smethurst was a partner. He had a number of prospectuses for the limited liability company printed at Warrington, without the authority of Mr. Spafford, his solicitor. The examination was further postponed until December Bth. The regis- trar expressed his dissatisfaction that the examina- tion should have to be adjourned so often owing to the failure of Mr. Burton to file his accounts. Mr. Burton promised that the accounts should be filed before December Bth. UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED.—J. and NV. DUTTON, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Adrt. WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.—A Pt- BLICAN'S CASE. Joseph Coop, of the Hop Pole Inn, Ilorse.market- st., appeared to undergo his public examination at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court, yesterday,before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. The bankrupt stated that his gross liabilities amounted to £1,203 Bs. Id. and the total assets £6O, the total deficiency being £1,043 Bs. id. The bankrupt alleged as the causes of failure a loss by fire in 1886, and litigation over the "Eagle and Child,"public-house,Bolton, and Lil- ford Hotel,Bedford Leigh. Be also added that about 1870 he commenced business as a hatter at Totting- ton near Bury, about November, 1886, the factory was destroyed by fire. His loss was over .£2,000. He only received £6OO from the Insurance Corn-. pany. After being out of work for nine months,he took the Engle and Child Inn, Bolton-road, Bury. In May, 1890, he left this public-house to go to the Lilford Hotel, Bedford Leigh, but owing to the pur- chase of that hotel falling through, he was out of employment until March, 1891, when he took his present premises, the [lop Pole Inn, where he paid no good will. The debtor gave the following explanation of his deficiencies : excess of liabilities over assets January 1, 1888, .£800; owing to his not having completed the purchase of the Lilford hotel. he forfeited the deposited £5O; a lawsuit about the fixtures cost him £3O; the purchaser of the "Eagle and Child " sued him for damages about some fix- tures which cost him £lOO. The " Hop l'ole " had not paid lately.—Cross-examined by Mr. Potter, the assistant official receiver, the bankrupt said that he knew be was insolvent six years ago,but had hoped to pull through, He had no evidence to show that the 105 by fire at the hat factory had amounted to £2,000. After a long cross-examination which Mr. Potter said was very unsatisfactory, the public examination closed.—The public examination of Wihhiaw Tyldeeley, WellAeld-street, builder, was also eland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EX ,k 'INITNEP, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EX ,k 'INITNEP, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 11, 1893.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Ralph Johnson, 3-6-..--E:re"--ot age, living at 24, Factory-yard, met with an accident on Saturday by which he sustained a fractured thigh. He was taken to the Infirmary, where he now lies.</p>
        <p>A SMALL BLAZE. A small tire broke out at the house of Mrs. Walmsley, White-street, on Monday evening, at twenty minutes to six. It seems that Mrs. Walms- ley, who is a widow, was about to light the gas with a taper, when the table cloth caught fire. A curtain was also ignited, but the flames were quickly subdued With a few buckets of water. ALL HOUSEIHOLD LINE.r, hemmed and marked, free of charge. —J. and W. Durrox. Sankey-ot.—Adet.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD DRAT'S NEW DESTRUCTOR. Dr. Sergeant, medical officer of health to the Lan- cashire County Council, and other gentlemen had arranged for a visit to Longford Depot on Monday to inspect the new destructor there, a description of which has already appeared in the" Examiner." At the last moment, however, the doctor tele- graphed to say that he was una.nle to attend owing to indisposition. . •</p>
        <p>SHOW OF CHRYBANTHRUU3IB.</p>
        <p>Commencing on WedneNdayo he new greenhouses in the Park were thrown open for an iu&amp;pect ion of chrysanthemums for about a fortnight. Mr. Parker, the head gardener, has got together an excellent collection, consisting of about 25 varieties.</p>
        <p>2s. TO 58. IN TILE E.- 0 FP, on all cash ordera at It. W. MARSH'S, Furniture NVarehouse, Bridge-Foot, Warrington. —Bee taller advt., page 4. Advt.</p>
        <p>TIM SIDE DOOR QUESTION. A meeting of the committee of magistrates, appointed to carry out the decision of the Bench in connection with the side doors of public-houses, was held un Monday. The cases of a dozen out-door license houses were dealt with, and in each case the committee were met in an amicaule spirit by the landlords. GUY FAWKES COMMEMORATED.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the I—in powder plot was celebrated in Warrington in an uninteresting fashion on Monday. It afforded an opportunity to juveniles, under the plea of commemorating a notable historical event to frizzle key-holes with crackers, create fits of hysteria in old ladies, stay out an hour later at night, and revel in the ex- penditure of extra pocket money for fire works. There was no organised display of any kind made, the usual " commemoration " on the cricket ground being abandoned.</p>
        <p>NO REASONABLE OFFER REFUSED, If made at once at Mama's Furniture Warehouse, Bridge Foot, Warrington, See fuller Adv., page 4.—Advt.</p>
        <p>A CORPORATION OFFICIAL IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>Edward Reddish, Stockton Heath, a Corporation official in the Water Works office, was brought before the Warrington magistrates on Monday on the charge of feloniously embezzling and stealing on the 25th March last, and at divers other dates, £BO, the moneys of the Warrington Corporation. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) who prose- cuted, said that the defendant had been cashier in the waterworks department of the corporation of Warrington and in the employ of the old water- works company, for a period of 17 years. He (the town clerk) did not propose to go into the case further that morning as the defendant's solicitor, Mr. A. Browne, had a proposal to make, to which he would agree.—Mr. A. Browne said that he had only gone partially into the matter, and he asked for a remand until Monday next, and he also asked that the defendant might be kept in the bridewell at Warrington during that time so that he might be seen at any time during the week.--The remand was agreed to.—The defendant appeared to feel his position very acutely. SOAKING RAIN PROOF OVERCOATS, yet porous, 553., C. jACKSON HOLMRS, General Outfitter, Market Gate, Warring- ton. —Advt.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above was held on Tuesday at the Ilorsemarket-st., Cocoa Ilooms,there being a good attendance of delegates. Au animated discusssott arose as to labour representation on the Town Council, and other public bodies and the matter stands adjourned for further consideration at the next meeting of the Trades Council. A deputa- tion was appointed to wait on those societies not yet connected with the Council with a view of strengthening the position of the Council. It was pointed out that there are several societies which still keep aloof from co-operation, and it was hoped that these would see the necessity of closer federa- tion for the common good. Mr. T. Malkin, president, gave his presidential address to the Council,dealing with many questions of interest to the working classes of ‘Varrington, the conditions undrr which they live, the distress in the town, ana conciliation Boards for the prevention ot strikes. As concilia- tion is the first and right step in all cases of labour disputes, it was considered essential that employers and employed should meet and discuss their griev- ances, whereby arrangements could be come to, and thus avert disastrous struggles between capital and labour.</p>
        <p>- - - ALL HoussaoLn LINEN, hemme 1 and marked, free of charv. —J. and W. DurroN, Sankey st.—Advt. FOILMA.TION OF A_ SUNDAY. SCHOOL ljzzioN. • •</p>
        <p>A meeting was held in Wycliffe Schools on Mon- day night to take into consideration the formation of a Sunday School Union in Warrington. The chair was occupied by the Rev. J. Yonge, and there were also present Revs. J. W. Varley, and D. W. Roberts, Mr. S. Fernley, secretary, Mr. W. Geddes, delegate from the London Sunday School Union, and over thirty representatives from various Sun- day Schools in the town. Mr. Yonge opened the meeting with a few remarks, and called upon Mr. Geddes to address the meeting. Mr. Gedoes laid the claims and advantages of a Sunday School Union before the meeting. The Rev. J. W. Varley spoke advocating the formation of a Union, especi- ally referring to the conferences held from time rto time, and the benefits which were held out for those engaged in teaching. Messrs. Hawthorn. Lang, Dale, Jolley, Fernley and others, also spoke in favour of the formation of a union, and stated that their respective Sunday ' Schools had decided in the event of a union being formed to join it. It was unanimously decided to form a `iunday School Union in Warrington and district. A small committee was appointed to draw up a table of rules and to lay them before a , meeting of Sunday School representatives to beheld ' early in December. The following schools have joined the Union: Wycliffe Congregational, Ool- borne-street Baptist, Latchford Baptist, Hill Cliff Baptist, Legh-atreet Primitive Methodist, Working- men's Mission, Brick-street Independent Methodist, Buckley-street Independent Methodists, Friars' Green Independent Methodists, and Brick-street Mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A.</head>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>- There was a very large attendance at the usual P.S.A. meeting held in the Young Men's Christian Association Rooms on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Kent, the president, occupied the chair. The Rev. E. T. Reed, delivered an address on "The Gunpowder Plot." Ile took it as a divine interposition that the plot was frustrated, and that Protestantism was thus more firmly established. Mr. F. Hickman presided at the organ. Y.M.C.A. CRICKET CLUB. The " cricket social " promoted by the members of the cricket club in connection with the Young Men's Christian Association was held on Thursday evening in the lecture hall. Mr. William Roberts occupied the chair. A most interesting programme was submitted, in which the following artistes took part :—Miss E. Fieldhouse, Miss Bennett, NI r. M. Hale (St. Helens), Dlr. A.Griffiths, and Miss Furness, who gave several recitations. A collection was ,taken in aid of the Cricket Club funds. A vote of thanks to the chairman, and performers was passed on the proposition of Mr. W. Bayley, secretary, seconded by Mr. M. Woods. AV V.SiT'S ScAtAss.—Agent GAII.L.LND, Bridge-street, War- rington. LOCAL MARRIAGE. .</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the marriage of Miss Nelly Merton, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Merton of this town, a niece of the late Mr. and Mrs. Can- trell, and a cousin of Miss Cantrell of Sankey-street, with Commander Adams, R.N., took place at Saint Alban's Catholic Church, Bewsev-street. Captain Woolacotte R.N., acted as best man. The marriage ceremony was perforn- ed by the Rev. Father Craven 0.5.8. Among the guests at the wedding breakta-t were the Rev. Fathers Craven, Cody and Duggan, 0.5.8. The happy pair tor,k their departure by train for London to spend the honeymoon. The bridesmaids were Miss Cantrell, cousin, and Miss Merton, sister of the bride. Mr. Merton gave the bride away. The bride was very becomingly dressed and looked very pretty. HONOURS FOR MR. T. MORRIR, F.G.S. The Trustees of the West Premium have, on the recommendation of the committee appointed by the 'Council of the Liverpool Engineering Society for the purpose, awarded two premiums, of the value of .C 5 ' each,—one to Mr. Thos. Morris, F.G.S., of Warring- ton, for his paper entitled, " Geological, manufac- turing, and other notes on iron," and the other to Mr. Ernest W. Pierce, for his paper on " Engineer- ing contracts." This is the second year these pre- miums have been awarded, and considering the clever engineers that were, so to speak, in compe- tition, Mr. Morris is to be congratulated on the honour that has been conferred upon him. It will be remembered that about three years ago, Mr. Morris was also awarded by the Polytechnic So- ciety their premium gold medal, or .£lO. LAMPS. —J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Warrington and •Earlestown.</p>
        <p>AN ALDERMA'N FORESTER. At the Court 240 of the Ancient Order of Fores- ters, held at the Corporation Inn, Market-place, an interesting ceremony was performed on Saturday. The initiation of Alderman Wright as a Forester was sufficient guarantee for a large attendance of members, and the assembly was presided over by Bro. G. Maybury, D.C.R., who impressively read the ceremony of initiation. Bro. Alderman J. E. Wright. in response to the toast of "The newly- elected brother," spoke in high terms of friendly- societies, and said that they tended to bring work- ingmen more into harmony with each other than anything he knew of. Another interesting ceremony also took place, namely, the presentation of a framed emblem and a Past District Chief Ranger's sash to Bro G. Maybury, whose terra of office to the district has just expired. Bro. E. PitcMford (Court 420), the newly-elected D.C.R., spoke in high terms of Bro. Maybury's past services, whilst the recipient gracefully acknowledged the gift. ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W. DUTTON. Sankev-st.—Advt. WARRINGTON NEEDLEWORK GUILD. A general meeting of the above was held in the Town Hall on Wednesday afternoon, and was well attended. It was decided to request all associates and members to send in garments and subscriptions by December 12th, and to hold a show of the former on Wednesday,December 20th in the Trinity Schoolroom kindly lent by the Rev. E. C. E. Carle- ton for that purpose. Parcels of clothing will be distributed to the different clergy on December 21st, Admission will be free to inspect the goods. There will be tea provided by the ladies of the ex- ecutive committee for which a charge of 6d. will be made, the sum realized by the tea will be a source of clear profit to the Guild, and will be devoted to the purpose of buying clogs, which are greatly needed by the poor of Warrington, in the present time of distress. Amongst tie ladies present we noticed, Mrs. Greenall, Harrison, Armitage, Fair- dough, Long, Smeth urst, Tunstall, Monks and MISS Pierpoint of the Committee, also Mrs. Carleton, Beamont, Butterworth, Barker, A. Edelsten, Vere Davies, R. T. Fairclough, Forshaw, Holford,Moseley Madeley, Slater, Percival, Whittle, H, Woods, Spinks,Yonge,the Misses Dunn, Long, Smitli,White (2), Westwell. etc,,etc.</p>
        <p>Tn NEw STBllif FIRE ENGINE. The new steam fire engine which the Corporation decided to purchase in May last, arrived in town yesterday. We have seen the engine and find that it is one of the most improved class of steam fire engines. It is capable of delivering 480 gallons of water per minute, and has all the latest improve- menta both as regards the boiler, and also the gen- eral mechanism of the engine which is similar to that of the old " Major" purchased in 1880. It is, equipped in every way with modern improvements and the- makers are so confident of its efficiency that tbey have guaranteed it for ten years against any defects in workmanship. This stamp of engine has been used in several towns, and has proved to be efficient in every respect. It will be named " Captain " after the present chairman of the Fire Engine Committee. Captain Hutchinson, just as was the " Major" called after the then chairman of the committee, Major Pickmere. It has been de- cided that the trial of the engine will take place next Friday rooming, when the proceedings will be rendered all the more attractive to persons inter- ested in sanitary affairs, in consequence of a new sewer flushing machine made by the same firm be- ing tested,</p>
        <p>PERFECT TIIIIIFT SOCIETY—ELECTION OF SECRETARY. A general meeting of the members of the Perfect Thrift Building tiociety was held on Tuesday in the Mission room, Academy-st.,for the purpose of elect- ing a secretary in the place of the late Mr. 11. 11. Dutton. There was a good muster of members,and on the motion of Mr. G. Wallington,Mr. A. Horrocks took the chair. bin Horrocks after reading the notice convening the meeting, spoke in terms of regret of the loss the society had bus tamed in the death of their secretary.—Mr. G. Wallington moved a vote of condolence with the family and relatives of the late Mr. H. 11. Dutton. He spoke in high terms of Nir. Dutton's personal character, and said that his death was a severe blow to the society.— Mr. S. Blythe, in seconding the motion. said that Mr Dutton was one of the rising young men of the town. His loss would be felt in other places besides the Perfect Thrift Society, as he was prominent in almost everything reiigious, social, or political.— The vote was carried unanimously .—The chairman said that the next business was the election of secretary, and proceeded to read the applications, seven in number. Mr. G. Wallington moved and Mr. 11. Thornton seconded the election of Mr. J. Gittens to the vacant secretaryship. They each bore testimony to his ability, and trustworthiness, and hoped that his election would be unanimous. Various other members spoke in suppoq of Mr. Gittens, and Messrs. Lattimer, Mason, and Harding, (three of the applicants) also withdrew in his favour, the result being that he was elected unani- mously. Mr. Gittens thanked the members for their confidence in him, and the meeting ter- , 111.111atiA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>Runcorn School Board, at their meeting on Tues- day, decided to consult their architects, Messrs. F. and 0, liolme with respect to the enlargement of the Girls school in Victoria road. From the speeches of the members present, however, it would appear that much more is needed. The boys school is seriously overcrowded, and there is no provision for infants residing in the now rapidly growing upper portion of the town. Under these circum- stances the suggestion that the subject should be dealt with as a whole and not piecemeal must com- mend itself to the ratepayers as the proper and more economical course.</p>
        <p>Alderman Gossage has again been unanimously re-elected Mayor of Widnes. It must be highly gratifying to his Worship to find the several sec- tions into which the Council is split up, so cordially agreeing on a subject so personal to himself. Alder- man Gossage performed the duties and the honours of the honourable position with dignity and gener- ous liberality. 11 e attends St. Paul's Church to- morrow when the offertory will be devoted to the distress fund, and a cordial invitation is extended to a large number of gentlemen to accompany him there.</p>
        <p>-- Instead of the long looked for determination in the distress at Widnes, caused 1 y the coal strike, the continued dispute between tie colliers and their owners taking place, the number of persons thrown out of work is increasing week by week. There are upwards of 4,000 who through no fault of their own, are walking idly about the streets and the outlook for the winter is in)st cheerless. The Mayor's Relief Committee is alleyiating, the suffer- ings of thousands of men,women and children daily in a systematic and fin reaching manner, but the funds are now running short, and subscriptions and donations will be thankfully received and acknow- ledged by the Mayor, the Town Clerk, or any mem- ber of the Committee.</p>
        <p>--- In Runcorn, happily, there does not appear to be much distress. A great many ot the men who have been thrown out of employment have found other work, and the boating population is supported by advances from the owners. Certainly the bad trade had no effect upon the collections at tit.Paurs Chapel. last week end. The Wesleyans look forward with great interest to their annual services and speeches on behalf of their Foreign Missionary Society, and last Sunday and Monday, on the occasion of their anniversary, large congregations assembled. The collection in chapel and school realised no less a sum than £lBO, or £4 more than last year.</p>
        <p>Paragraphs are continually appearing in the daily papers announcing the opening of the Ship Canal at a more or less early date. The latest, an- nounced that the waterway would be opened out to Manchester on Tuesday last, but the canal above Widnes Lock to Acton Orange is still dry. The work at Old Quay is not yet tinished,alchough in an advanced stage towards completion, and probably water will be admitted there during the coming week. There is now a regular weekly service of steamships running between Runcorn and London, carrying cargoes of general goods, and local trades- men. I hear,bave found this service both cheap and immaint.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ELECTION OF ALDERMAN FOR LATCIIFORD.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTION OF ALDERMAN FOR LATCIIFORD.</p>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE IN THE COUNCIL,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>--- COUN6LLOR ROBERTS MAKES A DETER-</head>
        <p>--- COUN6LLOR ROBERTS MAKES A DETER-</p>
        <p>MINED STAND.</p>
        <p>A scene of excitement almost unequalled by any- thing that has occurred at the Council meetings for yesre, took place on Thursday, at the annual meeting of the Warrington Town Council. The election of Mayor and other business bad been transacted, when the election of an Alderman to fill the vacancy in Latchford caused by the resig- nation of Alderman Chandley came on. The portion of the chamber allotted to the public was crowded with the spectators, chiefly prominent members of the two political parties, whilst in addition to these there were two Police Inspectors and two Constables present. The M.trott said " We will now proceed with the election of an alderman for Latchford. Coun- cillors Ashton and Tinsley have been nominated.' "I want to know "said Councillor Robert " if I shall be permitted to discuss this question." " No" replied the Town Clerk in answer to an appeal made by the Mayor. "It is out of order." Councillor ROBERTS : "Then I want to know your authority.* • " I can give it you better by quoting the Editor of " Arnolds Municipal Elections," said the Town Clerk " who states that—" " Oh, that won't do for me. It's an editor's opinion. I want the law," exclaimed Councillor Roberta "Allow me to finish " exclaimed the Town Clerk, " and you will see." Arnold states, 'The method of election here set forth must be strictly followed There is a growing desire on the part of Councils to enter upon a preliminary discussion, and with this view to propose in open Council cettain per- sons as candidates for the Vacant chairs. The Act does not authorise any such procedure, but, observ- ations of a general character, have been allowed in some instances on a motion for an adjournment of the hall, but this is a dangerous practice.' " And " continued the Town Clerk, " I agree with the opinion. I have also taken "Councils " opinion. It states " The method of proceeclure as prescribed by the Municipal Election Act 1882 most be strictly followed ; ne motion for adjournment or other business should be allowed." Both these are stand- ing authorities." " Then I move the adjournment of the Council" said Councillor Roberts. " But it cannot be done.' " That ruling is not the law—where is the Act ?" Up to this time the excitement of the remainder of the Council had been kept suppressed, but now an uproar suddenly commenced, members rose to their feet crying " chair" and " order." Councillor Roberts endeavoured to speak again, hut loud cries of interruption from members standing on their feet drowned his voice. Presently the uproar sub- sided, then Councillor ROBERTS calmly continued ; On other occasions discussion has been allowed, but as you refuse it, then 1 move the adjournment of the meeting." "Lien I cannot accept the motion " exclaimed the Mayor. " Not accept it ! Why it is in consonance with the ruling the Town Clerk has read." Here several members rose and tried to speak. Loud cries of "Shame "and "Hear, hear—go it," resounded through the Chamber and for some' moments there was a " clamouring of many voices' all trying to address the Chairman. When an oppor- tunity presented itself the Town Clerk explained that Councillor Roberts would be justified in mov- ing the adjournment of the meeting, if it was not at the point of the election of an alderman. Councillor ROBEZTS : "But that is a new ruling." "It is not, sir. During the whole time I have served as legal adviser it has held good:' "I tell you I have entered upon a discussion some years ago when Councillor Ashton's name was mentioned as a huge joke." _ . _</p>
        <p>_ "Aye" put in Councillor Smethurst " It's because you will talk." "And I intend to do it" come the indignant retort, whereupon another wave of agitation went round the council table. Loud shouts of " Shut up " and " Chair" and "Hear hear, you're right," all intermingled with a confusing effect. The Mayor's cries for " order" were totally disregarded, members banged their fists on the table and entered upon personal altercations. Amidst all this Coun- cillor Roberts stood quietly waiting for silence. When it came he said :</p>
        <p>"1 am not going to sit down, Mr. Mayor, until this question is settled to my satisfaction." Councillor SiLaw "You had not ought to a/low him to intrude upon your good nature. He's out of order."</p>
        <p>" I rule you out of order" said the Mayor. Alderman PLATT : I beg diet the standing orders be suspended that, Councillor Roberts may discuss the question. The MAYOR: "It cannot be done. Sit down Councillor Roberts." " Then if that is so Mr. Mayor I defy the chair," exclaimed Councillor Roberts emphatically. This produced an unprecedented sensation in the Council. The Mayor vacated his chair and promptly left the room. No one spoke for some moments, and Councillor Roberts emphasized his defiance by remaining standing. At length the ominous silence was broken by Councillor Smethurst exclaiming " Phew ! This is a nice state of things—a pretty kettle of fish." And then all at the same moment half-a-dozen Tory councillors rose and directed a shower of indignant epithets at Councillor Roberts. He paid ne attention to them but remained stand- ing, with a smile on his face, which had gone very pale,and contented himself by looking at the vacant mayoral chair. In the commotion which followed several councillors left their seats. The deafening confusion of voices presently abated and then Alderman Surroll, looking towards "the way- ward councillor," as lie was described, said in a most solicitous manner : "Councillor Roberts do sit down. You know you are out ot order." "if! am," said Councillor Roberts, " the Mayor has his alternative. Let him have me removed— there are the police," and he pointed to the officers. This further show of determination brought on another scene. And now, as there was no one at the helm to conduct affairs,the most unprecedented things were indulged in. Several members called for "police "—othere called " shame," and then another silence followed, which Councillor Roberts broke by reiterating that "The Mayor has his alter- native.' _ .</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANcoatII next rose and tried to per- suade Councillor Roberts to sit down. " For," said he, " you are out of order—the Clerk has giveu us the legal ruling." Councillor RODERTS "Yes,but there is a growing disposition on the part of the Town Clerk to always rule my opinions on legal matters out of order.' (Cries of order and " shaine.,') The TOWN CLERK : I emphatically deny the imputation. Several members here cried out "Have him re- moved," to which Councillor Roberts replied "Very well, do so. I shall not be the first who has had to suffer for his opinions." Loud applause fol- lowed this. Presently the /dayor returned. Ile , refused to put the " alternative " into force, and asked for the voting papers, which bad been dis- tributed to be returned, filled. More private alter- cations followed, and Councillor Roberts, among the others handed in his voting paper. Whilst he was returning to his seat, be was met by Council- lor Smethurst, who commenced to pour out such a storm of invective upon Mr. Roberts,that the whole Council had to interfere with cries of " order." The following is the result of the voting : For Councillor J. Ashton—Aldermen Sutton, Wright, Franr;omb : Councillors Joseph Ashton, A. Bennett, J. Muir, Tames Fairelough. W. Bolton, IL J. Longton, W. P. Skelton, J. llepherd, J. Shillcock, W. J. Shaw, J. Smethurst, llutchinson, Hill, Green- ing, and Cannel'. For Councillor Tinsley—Aldermen Holmes, Black- hurst : Councillors Roberts, Garner, Beaman, Green, Plumpton. The Mayor next announced that Councillor Ash- ton had been elected Alderman for Latchford by a majority of ten.</p>
        <p>Says a correspondent: "The spectators in the Council Chamber were just about as excited as the aldermen and councillors. During the more heated part of the discussion an unusual scene of excite- ment NM noticeable among them. Personal alter- cations took place on the merits of the question. Near where I had the pleasure of standing theie was a member of the faculty—perhaps,as the Tories are said to have expected the "scene," he was there to attend to business. At any rate, he expressed very strong opinions about Councillor Roberts, and was promptly taken up by a stalwart champion of "Harry's." Both gentlemen worked themselves up into a rage, others joined in the discussion, and soon there was such a big row in a 'little corner,' that S u inspector of police bad to insist upon more do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>corous behaviour. Sometimes I thought that certain excited partisans would have plunged into the dis- cussion that was raging around the table before them. Then again,as I was leaving the room,when the 'show was over,' I was quite shocked to hear Alderman Ashton endeavouring to ' get round' the gentlemen of the Press, by presenting them with a gracious new Alderaranic smile, and asking in re- turn, You will give me a good report, won't you— and leave Henry Roberts out r"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There is very slight change in the state of affairs at Warrington consequent upon the coal dispute. In fact it has become almost a matter of course now to see the streets crowded with people out of work. The result of the conference at London last week was a severe disappointment to hundreds in War- rington, as the ironworkers were confidently .look- ing forward to an early re-opening of the works. But these hopes have been doomed to disappoint- ment, and a restart appears to be as far off as ever. In the meantime, the Corporation are doing what they can to provide work for the unemployed, and they have received the permission of the Local Government Board to borrow .V 2,000 for road mak- ing, &amp;c., and the work will therefore be continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>The numbers relieved during the present week at the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel have been: Saturday, 190 men; Tuesday, 420 children; Wednesday, 180 women; Thursday, 380 children. On Saturday evening an entertainment was provided after the tea was over. Mr. T. Woolley occupied the chair and opened the meet- ing with a short speech after which the following inter- esting programme was gone through :—Glee, " Happy peasants"; the glee party; song, " Anchored " ; Mr. Panshavr, who was encored and sang "The village blacksmith"; oboe solo, " The better land," Mr. Ward, encored; song, "Mary of Argylo."Mr. Cliff; recitation, " Sermon by a stone," Mr. Seddon, encored ; "The skin" ; oboe solo, "The lost chord," Mr. Ward,encored ; song, "Deep in toe mine," Mr. Fanshaw ; song, "Fair and false," Mrs. Chile. also "There is a flower that bloome.th." A vote of thanks te the Chairman brought an enjoyable meeting to a close.—During the week the committee have received £3, collected from the Padgate Wesleyan Chapel, and also .£1 is. from some friends at Padgate. Mr. Thomas Bennett also of Padgett) has given a load of potatoes to the committee, and at the football match at Penketh on Saturday .£2 was realized and given towards the same object. The funds at present are low, however, and a little more help is required to keep on the work.</p>
        <p>RELIEF COMMITTEE'S WEEKLY MEeTING. INCREASED DESTITIJTION.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting )f the Committee for the relief of distress in Warrington was held in the Town Hall yes- terday afternoon. The Mayor occupied the chair. Mr. F. Monks in giving his weekly statement said that he was sorry to have to announce an increase in the dis- tribution of relief. During the week 25,012 pints of soup had been distributed ageing 23.196 last week ; 29,029 lbs of bread Against 29,193 ; 1,600 #lbs of sugar and OZ3 of teb. against 1.279 lilbs and 1.355 ozs. respectively. The cost this week had been £lB7 93. 91, against £169 119. 9d.last week. The total amount of subscriptions up to date was £1,604 17s. lid., and the expenditure £1,381 183. 2d., leaving a balance M hand of £222 19s. 9d. The amount received this week had been £lB3 Os. id. which added to the weekly subscriptions of £3O made a total of £213 Os. ld. As there were no signs of the coal dispute coining to an end the committee would have to appeal to their friends to continue their subicriptione. In con- sequence of a letter received from the Rector stating that the managers required Hank-st. school, the distribu- tion of that district would be transferred to the Drill Hall. The Executive Committee thought that in view of the continued distress, it would be well to make an appeal to other localities. He, therefore, proposed that the following letter be sent to the Manchester, Liver- pool, London, and other papers :—"The widespread dis- tress in consequence of the enforced idleness of a large part of the population of Warrington, owing to the want ! of fuel, makes this coal strike most severely felt, and as a result, Nearly 6,000 men, women, and children have to be provided daily with food. For twelve weeks, this has been done by means of local funds, the demands upon which is now increasing every week, end require about £2OO per week. There has only been given lib.of bread, and a pint of soup per head per day for every adult, and half this amount for children under twelve years, and with the exception of the substitution of one ounce of tea an half pound of sugar on Sunday in the place of the soup, .this is all the food these poor people have to sustain themselves. It is nothing less than stow starvation to a large number of them who have no money nor furniture nor any means af provid- ing fuel or other necessaries of life. During the last few days, we have had frost, which has intenuified their sufferings beyond description. To add to this, the price of coal even in this district has risen to famine priceii,and those who can get it, have to pay from Is. to is. 61. per cwt. There is a large number of special cases of most respectable workingmen who before this strike had comfortable homes and houses full of furniture, who are now parting with it by degrees at ruinous prices to keep body and soul together. They want employment and cannot get it by reason of the coal strike, and we appeal to the public to assist the executive committee formed for this relief to provide them with fuel and a little clothing to protect them from the inclemency of the , weather, by subscriptions or donations, either in money or clothing, which may be sent to the Mayor of War- rington, the treasurer of the local fund.—Signed on behalf of the Committee, F. Greenall, mayor, F. Monks, chairman of the executive committee. ' The Rev. Owen Watkins seconded the motion. He said that the committee were dismayed at the scenes they had to witness in Warrington. They gratefully acknowledged the wonderfll liberality of their friends In Warrington, but he remarked that the money came from the same people each week, and the time had arrived when they ought to make a wider appeal. He thought that the public of Warrington might assist the committee by gifts of cast off clothing. During the week several articles of clothing had been given and those poor people who had received them, had received them very gratefnlly. The resolution on being put to the meeting was passed unanimously. On the motion of the Mayoress, seconded by Mrs. Carleton, it was decided to continue the distribution of tea and sugar for Sundae. Mr. Monks said that they had been hoping that the struggle would have ended last week but he must cons fess that as far as he could see, there was no hope of the dispute ending this year, unless there was some common- sense on both sides. In the face of such a possibility, he thought that the Committee might organise some sort of Industrial work, by which the people might earn a few Shillings. If it could be done under the auspices of the Committee it would be far better than any independent organisation in the town. Mr. Wilson had informed him that the Local Government Hoard had given permission to borrow £2,000, and that the borough surveyor had arranged for about 200 limn to be employed In making streets from Monday. (Hear, hear.) It would be a help, forward. The Rev. W. 11. Drummond said that he thought it ' right that the public should know that the Executive Committee had appointed a competent visitor. Reports had gone about that the Committee had not been suffi- ciently Careful. The Committee had been very careful, but it was satisfactory to know that a viaitor had been appointed who had all his time at his disposal. Rev. Owen Watkins again brought up the question of rent. He mentioned the case of a man who was ill, and unable to pay his rent regularly. He was threatened with ejection and he was obliged to leave,and he had died this week. He knew that it was hard for the landlord but he thought there were cases of extreme distress where they should not use such hard measures. The Mayor thought it inadvisable to interfere in this matter as there was a lot of people who had their all in their houses. Mr. Monks supported the Mayor. It was is difficult question to deal with, and although there was a num- ber of deserving cases, tl.ere were others who were tak- ing advantage of the distress and would not pay their</p>
        <p>allow I re- spectably</p>
        <p>rates. no Mayor said that great credit was due to the Ladies Committee for their work, which was increasing—(hear, hear),—and especially to those who so willingly walked so many miles to investig.ae the cases. (Rear, hear.) Father Wray expressed his pleasure at the Mayor's re-election to office. HO was sure he was expressing the unanimous opinion of the Commi;tee, when he said that there could be no worthier successor to himself than himeelf. Ile had performed the duties of his office dur- ing the past two years in a very efficient manner, and he (Father Wray) was sure that everyone could con- gratulate themselves upon the Mayor's re-election for the third time, and he Ix ped that his future term of office would be as successful as it had been in the past. The Mayor thanked Father Wray for his congratula- tions, and the meeting terminated. DELP FROM THE CHILDREN. A bazaar got up entirely by the children in aid of the Warrington Infirmary and the local distress was success- fully carried through on Wednesday. Great praise is duo to the following little ladies and gentlemen for their untiring perseverence and demoted attention in aid of so good and noble a cause :—Miss Madge Silcock, Miss Mollie Silcock, Miss Ida Silcock, and Miss Ethel Roberts; also Master Nicholls Silcock, and Master Harold Siloock. The bazaar was held at the house of Mr. Reginald Sil- cock, Museum-street, and was very liberally patronized by several ladies, among whoni might be mentioned the Misses Nicholls (of Timperley), Mrs. Edwin Woods, Mrs. George Venn Mrs. H. Roberts. Mrs. W. Woo4s, the Mises Bradbury, Mrs. Herbert Woods, Mrs. Shaw Green and several others. The sum realized amounted to £9 45., which the little ladies wish to be devoted as fol- lows :—LS to the Warrington Infirmary, £4 4s. to the local distress, to be placed in the hands of the Rev. Owen Wa'kis, to be used at tits discretion.</p>
        <p>CONCERT LAST MIGHT. Last evening an exceedingly successful concert was given at the Cairo-st. Co-operative Hall, in support of the distress fund. It had been arranged solely by Miss N. Pickton and Mr. T. S. Kent, whose successful and arduous endeavours in making the arrangements merit the most cordial approbation. The following are the artistes who took part in the performance, Miss Bessie franshaw, Miss Pickton, Miss Kent, and Messrs. A. Griffiths, W. H. Wallington, C. Furness. T. Boothroyd, and W. 11. Payton. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Morris and during an interval Mr. Kent mentioned that thanks were due to printers and newspaper offices of the town for tha gratis printing and advertisements they had given, and also to the Co-operative Society far allowing them the free use of the hall. The proceeds it is expected will amount to something over £lB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINUTON.</p>
        <p>Is the Town Council thoroughly on the down grade—and so soon after the elections too? What a disorderly lot the councillors—and the aldermen as well—were on Thursday. And all because Council- lor 11. Roberts wanted to give some reasons why Mr. Tinsley should be elected an alderman rather than Mr. Ashton. Both Mr. Tinsley and Mr. Ashton are Conservatives, so that it could not be on the ground of politics that the Tory councillors seemed to be so anxious to suppress discussion. On other grounds, I should say that at least nine out of every ten persons would say that Mr. Tinsley was the fitter of the two men for the post. But then, you see, Mr. Ashton is a publican, and that is a quali- fication which counts for everything amongst Warrington Tories at present. As you said last ' week, the publicans have won the municipal battle this year, and the spoils go to the victors.</p>
        <p>How completely the Tory councillors have been taught who are their real masters. Only three years ago, Councillor Ashton was proposed as an alderman, and the very suggestion was at that time spoken of and enjoyed as a" huge joke," both by Liberals and Conservatives. The venerable coun- cillor only received four votes against the twenty eight that were given to Councillor—now Alder- man—Platt. But much has happened in the three years, and one of the things that has happened is that the Conservatives have been brought down to taking their orders from the Licensed Victuallers Association. When a publican is now proposed for an aldermauship—no matter who he is—it must not be looked upon and treated as a " huge joke." The poor dumb-driven Conservative majority in the Town CCouncil must vote as they are told. If they canuat j.ustify their action—well, they can sup- press discussion and howl down those who attempt " to speak.</p>
        <p>There is one point which has been left in an un- satisfactory position, ana which, it is to be hoped, will be cleared up before another alderman has to 133 elected. That is, as to the right of members of the Town Council to speak at the election of an alderman. The ruling of the Mayor, supported by the Town Clerk, seems to be that a member of the Council has no right to express his opinion, but only to vote. But, surely, this is such a monstrous anomaly that it can only be supported by the ex- plicit words of an Act of Parliament, or of standing orders duly sanctioned. lam told that there is no such prohibition. If it lie not distinctly prohibited, a councillor must certainly be within his right in demi-II:ling to express his opinion before giving his vote. Ile should not be denied his right to speak simply because this or that individual would, rather that he didn't speak.</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact there have been discussions Warrington Town Council upon the election tf Aldermen. When the casual vacancy caused by the death of Mr. John Pierpoint was filled in 1839, the. very point was raised, and Mr. Brook, the then Town Clerk, simply ruled that it was not necessary that speeches should be made as no nomination was needed, and that it was not usual to raise a discussion on such occasion. Nevertheless, a dis- cussion of considerable length did take place; and, in November of the same year, when several Alder- men were elected, a similar discussion was raised by Councillor F. Monks, and Mr. JMonks' action was not challenged. It,therefore, seems tome that it would he for the advantage of everybody con- cerned, if the point was thoroughly cleared up,once for all. If there be not a clear and distinct prohibi- tion to the contrary, the Councillors should claim to discuss the appointment of Aldermen before giving their votes just as they discuss any other matter.</p>
        <p>We have been treated to a sensation during the present week in the elispe of a man confessing to murder. This is not an everyday occurrence, al- though it is not the first time it has taken place in Warrington. About two years ago, a man came into the police station and just in the same man- ner as in the present case,said that he had something heavy on his mind. In that case the man adhered to his story during the two or three weeks that he was detained, and he was only discharged npen want of independent evidence. The man who has given himself up this week,however, when brought before the magistrates, appeared to have repented making the statement,for he said that it was a false one. It is rather singular that a portion of his tale— and an important portion too—is confirmed by the police authorities at Shrewsbury. It would be in- teresting to know what can be the motive which leads men to confess themselves murderers—alwa)s of course, supposing that the deed does not really lie on their conscience—for in both Warrington cases the men were perfectly sober when they made their statement. However, this is a psychological question which is rather too abStruse for any "lay" person to grapple with. The man was diseharged for want of evidence.</p>
        <p>The Social Questions Union is now a very distinct and potent factor in the public life of Warrington. In .many ways it is exerting an influence tor good. It is satisfactory to note that the penny concerts that are held under its auspices on Saturday nights are a complete success,the Co-operative Hall barely providing accommodation for the large numbers who attend. The meeting that is to be held on Monday evening will also help to bring the Union into still greater prominence, and speeches of an interesting character may be expected from the Chairmen (Mr. F. Monks, J.P.) and the Revs. J. Yonge, It. A. Armstrong, and C. P. Aked. MERLIN.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>• FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>• FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>TRINITY CRAPEL.—On Sunday morning and evening, the Rev. Joseph Burrows, of West Kirby, preached. The rev.gentleman conducted special revival mission services during the week. llsmut OF SACRED MUSIC.—On Sunday afternoon an address was delivered in the Parish Church, Frocisham. designated " Musk in religion, and religion in music," by the Vicar, the Rev. H. B. Blogg ; after much M. Tivadar Nacber, violinist, accompanied on the organ by ' Mr. T. Ardern organist of the Training College, Chester, gave a skilful recital of sacred music, which was listened to by an entranced audience. After the even- ing service, M. Naallez gave a similar recital. Each service was vred attended. FREEMASONRY IstsratrAirox.—The annual festival of St. John, in connection with the Cholmondeley Lodge. 1,908, Frodsham, was celebrated on Tuesday afternoon, at the Town Hall. The installation of Bro. Harold W. Abernethy as W.M. was efficiently performed by Bros. J. B. Holland and J. W. White. The newly-installed W.M. subsequently invested the following officers : 1.P.M., J. B. Holland; S.W., Samuel Davies; J.W., J. Fitzgibbon; treasurer, T. D. Timmins; secretary, J. Walwyn White; Harry Harrison; S.D., W. H, Crouchley • S.D., J. J. W. Potter.; 1.G., Henry Tiley organist, Smith; S.S., Juliurßoschen ; tyler, John Roberts; charity representative, B. E. Heywood ; and almoner, J. K. Digges. A banquet afterwards took place, when the usudl loyal and Masonic toasts were honoured. Mr. Heywood, in submitting the Masonic charities, stated that during the past twelve months : they had dispensed E7l 18t. in charities, which sum was considered eminently satisfactory. The visitors included the following brethren from Runcorn :—T. H. Annett, P.P.G. treasurer; C. Phillips, P.P.G. Steward; M Smith- Burgess, P.P.G. steward; and Burch. Bro. Salmon, P.P.G. senior steward, of Chester, was also present.</p>
        <p>ALDRUNIAN FRANCOSIn AND TUN " CASTING VOTE." Alderman Ilrancomb has handed to us a letter from the representative of a Liverpool contempor- ary in which the writer points out that the casting vote, given to a retiring Conservative, in the recent municipal election at St. Helens, was not that of the Mayor, Mr. Sineleir,J.P., a Liberal, but that of Alderman Colonel Gamble, a Liberal Unionist. The writer further points out that the error occurred owing to some confusion at the declaration of the poll. As this error was used as an argument against Alderman Francomb's action in giving his casting vote against the retiring Councillor in the Howley ward election, we publish the correction with pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FIGHT FOR MA TABELELAND.</head>
        <p>THE FIGHT FOR MA TABELELAND.</p>
        <p>-- COMPLETE DEFEAT OF THE NATIVES.</p>
        <p>------- FORT VICTORIA, Thursday afternoon.—News has just been received from Dr. Jameson reporting the complete and final defeat of the Matabele and the occupation of Buluwayo. Nothing now remains but to capture Loben gula, who is a fugitive, and arrange a settlement in con- junction with the High Commissioner. The combined columns of Major Forbes and Dr. Jame- son, alter the big fight at Indaima, advanced cautiously towards Buluwayo,having many skirmishes on the way. On the first inst. the columns had got within ten miles of Buluwavo, and were much harassed on that day by -- crowds of Matabele, who several times attempted to take WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. the forces by surprise. Finally at noon the two columns Mosnar.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman) Alderman formed laa ger, and the Matabele, seven thousand strong, , Harrison, Messrs. William Pierpoint, J. W. Hayward, accepting the challenge, furiously attacked. The machine guns worked admirably and with extraordinary S. Wright, Dr. Gornall. effect, and the Matabele were easily kept in check from Ontrut AND DISORDERLY.—Richard Garvin, Bank-st. ; the first. The fight lasted about an hour. The enemy Sarah Maloney, 8, Pereivaps Fold; Christopher Fox, used their rifles but ineffectively and finally they fled In Padgate ; Thomas Galvin, 10, Friars Green, were fined complete disorder. They were pursued for some dis- for this offence. tance by the mounted men, who, however, were not CARD PLAYING BY CANDLELIGHT.—Frank Rabbit (17), able to do much execution and were soon recalled. William Mather (18), Thomas Wright (18), Thomas Of the 7,000 Matabele who went into the fight 1,000 Hoarty (18), were each charged with playing cards in remained on the ground dead or wounded, 90 per cent. James-street, on Oct. 27th. P.C. Evans said that defen- of them by machine gun bullets. The entire loss of the dants were playing the cards with the light of a candle, Chartered Company's forces was three killed and seven and wlie