      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>COMMON LOT.</head>
        <p>THE COMMON LOT.</p>
        <p>However diffelently situated as regards social p tion, or separated as regards location, it is the common lot of all sorts and conditions of men to be liable to various ailments and diseases. All men under these circumstances are, to a certain extent, placed on the Same footing. "A fellow-feeling makes us wondrous kind," and nothing interests a sick man more than to hear the experience of others who have suffered from the same complaint as himself. Now, though the names of vari- ous forms of disease are legion, in this country people ale subject, more or less, to one of the following form , a ailment. 1. Disease of the Digestive organs, such as Indigestion, Flatulence, Headaches, Dyspepsia, Heartburn, Drowsiness, Palpitation, Sze. 2. Diseases of Liver, such as Billiousness, Jaundice, Depressed Spirits, Impure Blood, Melancholy. Sm. 3. Nervous Diseases, such as Fainting Fits, Neuralgia, Despondency, Nervousness, Nerve Pains, General Weakness, Sm. 4. Diseases ot the Respiratory Organs, such as Com- mon Colds, Asthma, Bronchitis, Spitting of Blood, Shortness of Breath, Sm. Strictly speaking, all these ailments have a common origin. Clergymen, professional men, merchants, and others not inured to hardships, are especially liable to attacks, and we cannot give such better advice than to have recourse at once to a course of GwiLym EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. This excellent tonic remedy is absolutely unrivalled and the universal testimony of all who have given it a fair trial is, that, for those who suffer from any form of disease which tends to weaken the bodily powers in any degree, the best possible thing is to take a Course Of GWILYM EVANS' QUININE Birrxas. ffir LOOK OUT FOR FCOUNTERFEITS.—AS Crwilynn Evans' Quinine Bitters" have become so popular, and has gained so great a reputation as a specific for the above disorders, numerous imitations of it are being substituted and offered to the public. Every purchaser should see that the name" Gwilym Evans' is on label, stamp, and bottle. Sold by all chemists, and Patent Medicine Vendors in Is. lid., 2s. 9d., and 4s. 6d. bottles, or free by parcels post for the above ptices)direct from the Proprietors. QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING COMPANY, LIMITED, LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>, American Depot—Mr. R. D. Williams, Plymouth, Pa.</p>
        <p>(Laughter.) The magistrates sent prisoner to gaol for two months. ASSAULT.—Jane Johnson, 9, Silver Sguare, was fined 10s. including costs or seven days' imprisonment for as- saulting her mother-in-law, Margaret Jdanson, on October 28th. Bscow STRALING.—James Tobin, 25, Portland-street, was charged with stealing from the Liverpool House, Winwick-street on Saturday, a side of bacon, valued 17s. Prisoner was seen by an assistant in the Liverpool House to be eyeing a flitch of bacon very eagerly, and after a little time, he made a jump at it and ran off with it. He was followed by the assistant, and in his flight he dropped the bacon. He was arrested by De- tective Monaghan and P.C. Barlow, and taken to the police station where he pleaded guilty. He was fined 10s. or seven days. TUESDAY--Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Mr. E. J. Guest. CHARGE AGAINST A SOLDIER.—SamueI Dagnall, of Warrington was charged with being an absentee without leave frem his regiment stationed at Kinsale, Ireland, since the 3rd inst. The prisoner's defence was that he went to Liverpool on the date named to go to Ireland, but missed the boat, and there were no other boats until Tuesday night. He was discharged. THIIRSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman), Coun- cillor James Fairclough, Major Ridgway, and Mr. J.</p>
        <p>H. Fleming. THarr or VEST.—Samuel Taylor, a navvy, who said he came from Neston was charged with stealing from the shop of William Leslie, 65, Mersey-st., the previous afternoon, a vest, value 7s. 6d. Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—From the Chief Constable's statement it ap- peared that about five o'clock the vest was seen hanging inside the doorway of Mr. Leslie's shop by Mrs. Leslie, and very soon afterwards it was missed. A girl was sent to the various pawnshops with a description of the missing article, and asking the pawnbrokers not to accept it if offered in pledge. At 5.30, the defendant went to the Tower, in Bridge-st., and asked Mr. Dalton for ss. upon the vest. During his stay in the shop, the girl sent by Mrs. Leslie entered the shop, and said the vest had just been stolen from Leslie's shop. P.C. San- key was called in. The prisoner said a man at the door had given it him to pledge, but no man was found. After evidence by Mrs. Leslie, Mr. J. C. Dalton, and P.C. Sankey, bearing out the Chief Constable's statement pri- soner was gent to gaol for 14 days' hard labour. AN OLD OFFENCE.—Margaret Kelly was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-st., on 28th March, 1892.—P.C. Corbett gave evidence of the offence, and said the defendant failed to appear to the summons at the time and left the town. Bhe was fined 10s. THEFT BY Boys.—Thomas Leicester. (14), and Wm. Hill (8), both pleaded guilty to stealing from the Long- ford Gas Works, at four o'clock the previous afternoon, a quantity of cannel coal, value the property of the Warrington Corporation. It appeared from the Chief Constable's statement that at four o'clock the police received a telephonic message from Longford to send a police officer. The officers found the two boys in the office of Mr. Haddock, the gas manager. they having been caught carrying cannel away from the works. They were charged with stealing the cannel and each replied "I have nothing to say.' There had been great complaints of cannel going from Longford Works. W. Day employed at the Gas Works deposed to seeing the boys taking the cannel away,and to stopping them. The magistrates ordered the boys to be imprisoned until three o'clock. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- :man Platt, Councillor H. Roberts, and Dr. Gornall. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Thomas Daley, 10, Per- cival's Fold, was fined ss. including costa for this offence the previous evening. ASSAULTING HIS WlFE.—Thomas Grady, Smith-st., was charged with assaulting and beating his wife Mary Grady on the Bth inst. The evidence of the complain- ant deduced that on the date named the defendant came downstairs and thrashed her because she made a noise about him not going to work.—Sergeant Chantler de- posed to the complainant coming to the Police Station with her face bleeding very much and a black eye, on the previous Wednesday. Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months. PREPARING FOR THEIR BONFIRE.—Six boys named Thomas Delooze (16) 18, Pierpoint-street, Alfred Fitton (15) 97,Catherine-street, and George Rogers ‘l4),Charles Perry (14), Harry Pugh (14), and Joseph Davies (14), all of Catherine-street, were summoned by Mr. J. Basely Selby, Leigh, agent for Lord Lilford, for having on the 3rd inst., cut a fence the property of Lord Lilford, and done damage to the amount of 15s. Prisoners pleaded guilty. P.C. Clough said he was near Lodge-lane rail- way bridge, in company with P.C. Wood, when they heard a noise as of chopping wood, and on going there they saw all the boys very busy chopping trees down. They caught Delooze and Rogers, who said they were getting it for bonfire night. P.C. Wood corroborated. Each defendant was fined 3s. 6d. each.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAY.—Before Mr. C. Reynolds and Mr. James Handley. FURIOUS DRIVING—Edward Roberts, a young man, fish hawker, was charged with having on th.: 28th Oct., furiously driven a horse and a cart in Bridge-et., to the danger of the passenaers. P.C. Morris said the defen- dant was driving between the Market and the Police Station, at a gallop and travelling at about twelve miles an hour. The street was crowded.—The defendant said he had neither whip nor stick, and if he were to flog the pony it could not travel six miles an hour. P.C. Richardson gave evidence, and the defendant called James Pownall. The Bench ordered the defendant to pay costs Ts. 6d. MORE COAL STEALING. —Mary Fryer (16), and Mary Gregson (12), were charged with stealing at Weston Point, a quantity of coal, the property of the Manches- ter Ship Canal Co. The prisoners were found taking the coal from the coal stage. Both defendants pleaded guilty, and said they were taking the coal home.—Mr. Reynolds said if the parents did not burn the coal the children would not steal it. Each defendant was fined DRUNKEN MEN IN COURT.—Charles Rice, a Ship Canal employe, was summoned for assaulting his wife, Alice Rice. It was alleged that on Sunday night, the 29th Oct., the defendant came home drunk, pulled her out of bed and illused her. He threw her downstairs. She had to seek refuge in a neighbour's house. She had had no sleep on Saturday night and refused on Sunday night to get up and prepare his supper. A fine of 53. and costs was imposed or in default 14 days' hard labour.— Jane Hunt, a neighbour, was then summoned for as- saulting Charles Rice. The complainant alleged that the woman threw a stone at him and kicked him.—The Bench dismissed the case, telling him he was half drunk, and not fit to appear in court, and Mary Elizabeth Jones was charged with assaulting Robert Ha rdesty. In this case the complainant was palpably drunk, and Mr. Reynolds said they could not allow the court to be treated in this way. He suggested that Supt. Atherton should charge the man with being drunk in court, and commit him for seven days.—Supt. Atherton advised that he should be placed in the cells and then charged. This course was adopted. The woman was informed that the charge against her was dismissed. Later a man named Wm. Hazlehurst appeared to answer two summonses charging him with not sending his children regularly te school. He was fined 55. in each case. He had mumbled out his defence and Mr. Reynolds informed him that if be had not refreshed himself before he came to court, he would be able to make a better statement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>HIS 26T11 APPEARANCE. Thomas Sauce, made his 26th appearance at the Borough Court, yesterday morning. He was 'charged with stealing from is father's house, 4, Coronations- Buildings, the previous morning two men's cotton shirts, value 55., the property of William Sauce, his father, who is a tailor. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The Chief Con- stable said the prisoner worked and resided with his father. Two shirts were missed from the house, and Thomas was also missing. D.S. Monaghan received information and from enquiries he found that one of the shirts had been sold by the prisoner to Charlotte Leslie. second-hand clothes dealer, for one shilling, and he had pledged the other shirt at Mr. Jackson's in Church-street. The prisoner was met by D.S. Monaghan about, eight o'clock, and admitted selling the shirts. The prisoner was sent to gaol for the maximum penalty, three months hard labour.</p>
        <p>Timex oe Ilona CLIPPERS. Yesterday, at the Borough Court, Matthew Law- rence Carley, groom, living at 4, Refuge-square, was charged with stealing from the Patten Arms Hotel stables, a pair of horse clippers, value 7s. 6d., on Fri- diY, the 3rd inst. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that the complainant was Mrs.Kiernan, and the prisoner was a young man who was sent from that court to Father Nugent's Industrial School, and since his discharge from the school had been working for several persons, including Mrs. Kiernan. He was em- ployed on Wednesday and Thursday the previous week,at the Patten Arms stables,and was told that he would not be required on Friday,and paid up. The prisoner, however, went to the stables on Friday and assisted in the clipping of a horse. The clippers were missed from the cupboard a day or two after, and were seen in the window of Mr. Leslie, second hand dealer. The clippers were identified as the stolen property, and suspicion fell on the prisoner. He was arrested, and it was found that he was the man who had sold the clippers. Evidence was given by Millwood, a groom, and Mrs. Kiernan.—The magistrates lined the defendant 55., including costs. _</p>
        <p>DEFAULTING AFPRENTICFE. - At the Warrington Borough Court, yesterday, Thos. Clark, an apprentice, living in Stamford-st., was sum- moned by Messrs. William Silcock and Sons, King-st works, for unlawfully absenting himself from their ser- vice, and putting them to a loss of £l. Mr. Browne, solicitor, prosecuted on behalf of Messrs. Silcock. He Stated that the defendant was apprenticed to Messrs. Silcock, wire weavers, in January, 1891, !for 5i years. The defendant's wages for the month previous to leav- ing them averaged 12s. 10d. per week. Ile drew his wages on the 23rd September, and never came to his work again, causing a considerable loss to his employers. They afterwards learned chat the defendant was lodging in Stamford-st., and was working on the Ship Canal. Mr. Silcock corroborated Mr. Browne's statement, and said they had not the slightest reasorr for the defehdant leaving them. They wished the magistrates to make an example of the defendant as a deterent to other ap- prentices. The defendant said he was told by Mr. Silcock's brother he was a nuisance to the place, and to go as they 'didn't want him, and also complained of not learning the trade. The magistrates ordered him to pay the costs, and the damages claimed, and to fulfil his con- tract with Messrs. Silcock.—Catherine Fallon, appren- tice, Bakehouse-yard, Latchford, was summoned by Thos. Mills, fustian cutter, 4, Orford-street, .for absent- ing herself from her work, and putting him to a loss of 10s. Prisoner pleaded guilty. She was ordered to pay the amount claimed and fulfil her apprenticeship con- tract.</p>
        <p>PROCEEDINGS FOR UNJUST SCALES. Before the Borough magistrates yesterday, Ellen Bate, 8, Friars-green ; Margaret Caldwell, 9,Friars-gate ; Alice Potts, Factory-yard; Ralph Woods, 40, Powys-st., all small shopkeepers were charged with having in their possession scales not stamped. Defendants pleaded guilty. Mr. J. Lyon Whittle prosecuted on behalf of the Corporation. Mr. Whittle said the defendants had been to the inspector of weights and measures with their scales and he told them they were unfit for use, as the bearings were made of soft material, and not in accor- dance with the new Act of Parliament. The inspector had no power to destroy them, and their object in prose- cuting was simply to get an order for the destruction of them. He didn't wish to press the case against the defendants. Mr. Place said the law required that the bearings of a scale should be made of steel, and the scales of the defendants didn't comply with the law,and had been condemned but the defendants had not pro- cured new ones. The first three defendants were fined 3s. Bd. each and Mr. Woods was fined ss. Bd.—John Whittaker, hawker, no address, was charged with hav- ing on the 4th November unlawfully committed a fraud in the use of a scale, by" tipping the beam." Inspector Crabtree said he found the defendant with a handcart, Selling apples at the end of Lyme-st. He heard a woman ask for two lbs. of apples. He saw the defendant weigh the apples and noticed him "tip the beam "and put the apples in the woman's basket. He had the apples weighed again,and they were found to be 3i ounces short weight. It was a common trick. Defendant was fined 20s. and costs or one month's imprisonment. Ile went to gaol. All the scales were forfeited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MIN olt CASES.</head>
        <p>MIN olt CASES.</p>
        <p>. . . I.oj. x . 4W, , . '. . . 0 , il ,"ti ..,..,7 1.- 411' .1. 1 , . ',KO` .... ... , 1--'..1 ~. . : . N at.. , lil. C,;.' ee . :&lt; ;;:,%! ' ''',. , e.V..; .44,, J..? ~</p>
