        <p>E FNAMENT.R, ATE-R.DAY. NOVEMFER 11, 1893.</p>
        <p>MARVELLOUS RESULTS.</p>
        <p>If we said these things of ourselves the public might well .doubt, but never has a remedy received the un- qualified endorsement, as " Homocea " has received. We ask one and all to read these testimonials and ask themselves should they be without" Homocea " in their homes.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL AND 0 rHER TESTIMONIALS.</p>
        <p>RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>LORD COMBERMERE writes :—" Carlton Club, London, 9th Nov., 1887.-1 have tried your Homocea ' upon myself for Rheumatism, and I found it do more good than any embrocation I have ever used,and several of my friends have benefitted by its use.</p>
        <p>Comszamanz.</p>
        <p>BRUISES AND FLESH WOUNDS.</p>
        <p>BISHOP WILLIAM TAYLOR writes : " High Barnet. 28th Oct., 1891.—My dear old Friend,—l have used Homocea,' and proved its healing virtue both for severe bruises and flesh wounds, and also to kill the virtus of mosquitoes and chiggou (jiggers).—Yours very truly, (Bishop) Wm. TAYLOR, American Methodist Epis- copal Mission."</p>
        <p>Bandalls, forwards. Manchester Rangers :J. E. Birk, back; Edge, Morris, and Lightfoot, three-quarter backs; Taylor and Hargreaves, half backs ; J. T. Lewis, Ham- nett, Pearson, Conner, Hasleham, T. Emery, Walker, Hargreaves, and Smith, forwards.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A v. MANCHESTER RANGERS A.—These two teams met at the Wilderspool causeway enclosure on Saturday to play off their initial encounter this sea- son. The Warrington team presented a very strange ap- pearance owing to the many new faces which of late have taken the places of the better known players. The Rangers were decidedly the burlier team of the two, and had they possessed skill in proportion they could have had the game just as they pleased. However the result proved that their skill was not of a very superlative character. Ww.rington won the toss and undertook the task of defending the railway goal. Parkinson, who was the most conspicuous player on the field owing to having a white jersey kicked off for the Rangers, but within two minutes from the start Mason had registered the first try for Warrington. This did not satisfy War- rington, and after keeping the Rangers near the line for about five minutes, Mason again got over, but neither of these points were augmented. The Rangers made des- perate efforts to get away, and as a result they crossed half way, but they were not allowed to stop there long as Chapman made them beat a retreat, and another struggle took place near the goal post from which Buck- ley scored, Lawless having the necessary surefoot this time to land a goal. Although Warrington had almost the whole of the came up to half time, the score was only Warrington one goal, two tries, two minors to nil, a good score truly, but it does not show the full dispar- ity of the teams. The second half was a most uninter- esting one, there being scarcely any good play from be- ginning to end. The great ambition of the Warrington- I ians appeared to be to score minors. They were scarcely ' even more than ten or twelve yards away from their opponents goal line,but they made very poor attempts to utilise their chances. The gall kept going over the line, but as no Warrington player was near by, only minors resulted, and this continued until near the end of the game, when Ravbeld managed to get a try, but although it was in a good position no good resulted. A scrambling and most uninteresting game resulted in the scores of Warrington one goal, four tries, seven minors to nil. This was a decidedly low score, when the superiority of Warrington is considered, and a number of the minors with more care could have been made into tries. Chap- man, Coggin% Raybold, Lawless and Mason wete about the best of the Warrington team. WARRINGTON 2ND v. DROYLSDEN HORNETS.—At Droyls- den. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors the game was limited to two fifteens. The Hornets started, and at once made tracks for the lines, Houghton saving. The home forwards went with a rush, and Garside scored, Shaw hitting the post with the kick at goal. From the drop-out Shaw was at fault, and Warrington dribbled past the home back and almost scored, but Leadbetter relieved. Turning round Houghton was conspicuous with a run and kick, and then Gibson and Hughes were busy in defending their lines. However, the Hornets took play to the lines, and from a strong rush scored. Dropping out the home team again pressed, and had the best of matters up to the call of time, when they ran out winners by two tries to Warrington nil. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S v. WARRINGTON RANGERS:— This match was played on Saturday, before a large num- ber of spectators, on the St. Mary's ground, Liverpool- road. The home team started the ball, and at once called upon the Rangers to defend, when Burton had a splendid opportunity, but failed to take the pass. After this the Rangers relieved, the ball being taken to mid- field, where play settled for a time. J. Savage broke away with a dribble and scored, the kick at goal failing. The Saints dropped out, and again pressed, but were unable to score, and on the call of half-time the score stood Rangers, one try; St. Mary's, nil. On changing ends the Saints had the best of the game. They gained a decisive victory by one goal (dropped) 2 tries to Ran- gers 1 try.</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. BLACICLEY RkSGEßS.—Representatives of the above teams met on Saturday afternoon, on the Widnes ground, before about 1,500 spectators. The home men started the game, and at once began to press their opponents. The Rangers pulled together and made play look dangerous, but Barber neatly saved and put in a long kick. Toe Widnesians made their way down the field in the direction of home, but the visitors rushed the leather back again. Hurst dribbled up nicely, and was successful in scoring for the Rangers. The kick was, however, a failure, This spurred the homesters on, and they made a determined rush once more into the visitors defence. Quick play followed, and the Rangers got away down field, but Alforde stopped their career. A moment afterwards half time was called, the result being Blaciclev one try, Widnes nothing. On resump- tion of play the game was of an even character, and became very exciting, the home men displaying much better form than they had done during the first portion of the game. The oval was dribbled into the visitors' defence, when Toohey picked up and passed to Forster, and he handed to Barber, who got safely home. A fine kick by the same player landed the leather over the bar This was the result of really splendid play. The visitors began to press,and when in a favourable position White- head .ot possession of the ball, and dropped it over the bar. 'Brennan and Drummond were very much missed The final result was—Blackley, seven points ; Widnes five points. The following was the Widnes team :—L.' Case. full-back ; Alforde, H. Barber, M. Toohey, and-J. Clarke, three-quarter backs ; G. Wright and W. Cox, half-backs : T. Forster, P. Kenyon, W. Forster, It. Ridge- way, W. Wynne; J. Whelan, J. Johnson, and J. Hogan, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SEVERE INFLIMMATION.</head>
        <p>SEVERE INFLIMMATION.</p>
        <p>H. M. STANLEY (the great African explorer) says " Homocea " is a most soothing and efficacious unguent It is as soft as oil and almost instantly mollifying in the case of severe inflamation.</p>
        <p>SKIN ERUPTION OF FOURTY-FOUR YEARS' STANDING.</p>
        <p>A lady writes from Stockport, 17th April, 1889 : " ' Flomocea 'is not known as it ought to be for skin disorders. I myself, have suffered very severely for forty-four years from Scurvy or Eczema left by small- pox. Have always been able to have first medical ad- vice, but doctors have not been able to do all these years what Homocea . has done in three weeks. My doctor is surprised at the result. I earnestly desire to assist others to the relief I myself have received." Lord Combermere, Lord Carrick, Bishop Taylor, and many others also bear witness to the marvellous effects of," Homocea.,'</p>
        <p>" Homocea " sold by most Chemists at is. 10., and 2s. 9d. per box, or sent post free on receipt of stamps to 21, HAMILTON SQ., BIRKENHEAD. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morninc.</p>
        <p>GEORGE G. BIISSEY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey, Golf, and requisites for Sports. Games, and Gymnasia. Catalogue may be had on application to the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London, S.B. BEST 46-C-R4t. BILVAD.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. MANCHESTER RANGERS.</p>
        <p>The counter attraction at Whalley Range interfered with the attendance at the match between the above teams at Rush°line, on Saturday. There were no more than a hundred persons present. Warrington were without the services of Nevins (who was doing duty at Whalley Range), James Bate (injured), Webster, Potts, Turner, and Berry, whilst the Rangers had Parlane away at the county match. Hamilton started for the home team, and so early as the first few minutes of the game it was easy to be seen which team would become victors. What passing did take place was shown by the visitors, and although Bate, Lally, and Evans failed to pass the half-way flag at the first essay, subsequently Jolley, Heesom, and Evans forced the Rangers on the defensive. Exchange kicking was next witnessed, Carey, Edleston, and Lightfoot doing duty, and for once in a way the locals had the better of the deal. Lally saved well, and then Lawless ran and punted well, and the first-named joining issue in a race to the line a certain try was lost by the half-backs' overeagerness. A minor was all that was allowed, but the Warringtonians would not be I denied, and Edleston displayed good form, finding I touch and assuming the aggressive once more. Hemp- son, Donohue, and Jolley were the leaders in one rush, but it was left to Evans and Lawless to make a bold bid for a try, which effort was nullified a couple of yards from the line. Burke relieved, only to see Taylor strive with a short run, and then Lawless made another bid, but this time finding his way blocked, he kicked judiciously to the open, and Jolley being placed quickly on side that player scored easily, but Heesom missed an easy place. The Warrington backs were again to the fore with clever passing, Evans fileesotri, Lawless, Carey, and Bate handling the leather and Morris only saved near the line. Edelston had a kick charged and the Rangers now trespassed on the Warrington quarters, but their stay was ot brief dem tion, for Evans and Carey each in turn kicked well, and the backs had again to resort to saving tactics. An I effort by Taylor gained the half-way flag, and a penalty I kick taken by Hammett was very near. However, the venue was quickly changed, and after Lawless had again been disappointed Jolley gathered in the loose and scattering his opponents scored a corner try, which Evans could not improve upon. Warrington continued to have all the best of the game, and only a plentiful distribution of free kicks saved the Rangers' lines from being crossed frequently. Near the close of this half the Rangers were awarded a penalty kick in a good posi- tion, and Lightfoot dropped a really clever goal, which point brought half-time, with the score—Warrington, two tries (six points) ; Manchester Rangers, a penalty goal (three points). On re-starting the game was taken direct to the home lines, and several futile essays were made by Bate and Lally to get in. Chances were missed in quick succession by the visitors, whilst the game altogether was a libel on those we have seen Warring- ton perform in. By degrees the Rangemreached War- rington's quarters, and Evans now snbstituted Lally at half back with a result that a quick visit was made to the Manchester end, and Evans getting possession from a park, dodged beautifully through the opposinz backs. and Phillips this time placed a goal. A clinking run and pass by Carey should have been turned to good account whilst an effort by Jolley was deserving of something substantial. Phillips made a good run near the close, but nothing accrued from it, and an uninteresting and one-sided game ended in favour of Warrington by one goal and two tries (eleven points) to Manchester Rangers a penalty goal (three points). Teams :—War- rington Edleston, back ; Carey, F. Heesom, J. W. Evans, and Lawless, three quarter backs ; John Bate and Lally, half backs; J. T. Thorniley,J. Jolley, Hamp- son, T. Donohue, J. Taylor, Phillips, E. Bailey, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN" A" v. CLAVORTON.—On Saturday Run- corn engaged Claughton. • Runcorn had a representative fifteen. From the first scrimmage G. Robinson got hold, and with Nixon in attendance some pretty passing was seen, the ball being taken over the line, where Nixon, who did not know he was over, repassed again, and one lof the Claughtou players got hold and touched down. The pressure was kept up for a considerable time, until runs by Hughes and Jones were the means of invading the Runcorn half. Another passing mOvement was in- augurated by G. Robinson, but Runcorn again singu- larly failed when within measurable distance of the line. The play that followed up to half-time was of the most uninteresting kind, owing to the tactics displayed by the home team, who had -evidently laid themselves out to prevent any real football being shown. The score when the interval was reached was Runcorn, six minors ; Claughton, nil. Some effective passing between the Run- corn backs was the next item, and upon T. Robinson getting possession he dodged through the thick of his opponents, and scored a brilliant try between the posts. The same player took the place, but failed to enhance its value. Runcorn now attacked vigorous- ly, and in close following T. Robinson again got over with an apparently fair try, but the point was dis- allowed, as were also a dropped goal by the same player and a try by Hollingsworth. The remaining play was all in favour of Runcorn, the ball only once crossing the half way line. Nothing worthy of notice, however, took place. A rough aud miserable game ended—Runcom, 1 try, 8 minors ; Claughton nil.</p>
        <p>SoNticurr v. HELSBY.—Plaved at Port Sunlight. Helsby turned out a stronger team than last year owing to the inclusion of several Frodsham players who have thrown in their lot with flelshy. In the first half Helsby scored, and converted. Resuming, Sunlight played</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>What happy recollections are recalled to those who have helped a Bride- with her Toilet! Friends ask themselves, has the bride a thorough knowledge of all the duties of a household, especially of that ever= recurring worry, Washing Day and Spring Cleaning? Does she know what SUNLIGHT SOAP can do? Does she know that for a few pence, without boiling or bleaching, she can, by using SUNLIGHT SOAP, have all the house= hold linen washed at home and made to look white as snow and fresh as roses?</p>
        <p>Happy is the bride who has been instructed in these matters, because it is on such simple household details as these that the future happiness and comfort of herself and husband must depend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>• • • .;,•-j .4" • ' • , "..- 0</head>
        <p>• • • .;,•-j .4" • ' • , "..- 0</p>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>with more dash, and Lee crossed the line, Clara convert-</p>
        <p>log. Owing to the darkness the game ended ten minutes befsre time. Result—one goal each. The annual meeting was held at the Town Hall, RuNcourt VICTORIA V. IVIDNES CRESCENT.—PIayed at on Tuesday at noon. Mr. Alderman Gossage pre- Runcorn before a good number of spectators. Both i sided, and there were present Aldermen Wareing, teams were well represented. By some good forward 'Jimmie, Williamson, Tilley, and Sadler, Councillors work the Vice, took the ball into their opponents tern- Owens, Barlow, O'Donnell, Shuttleworth, Gough, tory, and from the scrimmage M,lyneux cleverly scored, Johnson kicking a splendid goal. Later on Carruthers Singleton, Quinn, Carney, Shores, Hodgkinson, got the ball from the scrimmage, ran strongly, and W. W. Gossage, Bullough, Smith, G. I. Neil, Christie, evading his opponents, scored a meritorious try, Wright and Davies, with Oppenheim (town clerk), Craik Johnson kicking a good goal. A good game resulted (assistant clerk),Hoyle (accountant),Sinclair (surveyor), Loweinspector), dC. in a decided win for the Vice, by two goals (10 points)(nuisance an Carr(engineer). to nil. THE ELECTION OF MAYOR. I Alderman Wareing said the first duty imposed upon him was the pleasurable one of proposing the re-election of Mr. Alderman Gossage to the position of Mayor for a second term. When he had this pleasure twelve months ago he ventured to predict that in Mr.. Gossage they would find a gentleman who would conduct the :Just- ness of the Council and the affairs of the town in such a manner as to reflect credit upon himself. He also ven- tured to suggest that Mr. Gossagc possessed all the at- tributes that went towards making an ideal Mayor, and the Council would agree with him that that prediction had been fully borne out. The mayoralty was the most. distinguished honour the Council could confer upon a citizen of the borough, and he was convinced that Mr.. Gossage would fill the office with as much eclat in the future as in the past.—Councillor Owens seconded the resolution, and endorsed Alderman Wareing's remarks. He believed that Alderman Gossage would be as sig- nally successful in the management of the chair and in the direction of the town's affairs generally as had dis- tinguished his past year of office. The resolution was carried unanimously by the whole of the members of the Council rising to their feet. The Mayor, after taking the usual oaths, said he felt greatly the honour conferred 'upon him, and in again electing him mayor. He took the position with pleasure and thanks. He would endeavour to do in future as he had done in the past ;and he hoped that with more ex, perience, that he would be better able to perform the the duties. Possibly he might have more leisure and that he would be able to give more time to the office than he had been able to do hitherto. He thanked the mem- bers sincerely.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RESERVE V. LYMM GRAMMAR SCEOOL.-- The above match was played on the Cricket Ground at Arpley on Saturday,in splendid football weather. Both teams were strongly represented, and an excellent game was the result. The home team during the first half played a grand game, but in consequence of the sound defence of the visitors scoring was not rapid. The final result was Warrington 3 goals, Lymm nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH V.BENVSEY COMBINATION. —This match was played on the ground of the former on Saturday in fine weather. The Rector (Rev. F. W. Willis) kicked off for the visitors. The Parish Church team won by nine goals to nil. The Parish Church team has but recently been formed by the elder members of the Sunday school, and judging from their play on Saturday they should be able to give a good account of themselves before the season is *yen</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON Sr. ELPH/N'S V. ALLERTON PARK.-7 his match was played at Allerton in fine weather on Satur- day,the Saints playing six reserve men. The game was a very fast one, the strength of both teams being very evenly balanced. Final result—Allerton Park 1 goal, St. Elphin's 1 goal.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WIDNES.—These. teams met at Runcorn on Saturday afternoon. The visitors played down hill and had the advantage of the wind. Under these cir- cumstances they severely pressed their opponents, and despite the efforts of the Runcornians,they secured three goals before the arrival of half time. After the interval the contest became very keen indeed, the home team showing a determination to wipe off the score against them, while the visitors were equally anxious to main- tain the lead. Runcorn, however,were soon aggressive, and as the result of improved combination in front of the goal, the visiting custodian was beaten four times in quick succession. The winning point spurred the Wid- nesians, and they did all they could to equalise. Their rushes proved of no avail, for the defence was capital, and Carter in goal played with ability, he manfuny stopping several dangerous shots. A severely contested match ended in a victory for Runcorn by four goals to Widnes three goals.</p>
        <p>RETURNING OFFICERS,</p>
        <p>On the motion oTEZn-ciliCii--51--Vies, seconded by Councillor Gossage, it was resolved that the following aldermen be assigned to be returning officers for the eeveral wards set opposite to their respective namesi viz. :—Mr. Alderman Wareing, Farnworth Ward; Mr. Alderman Williamson, Halton Ward; Mr. Alderman Sadler, Simm's Cross Ward; Mr. Alderman Tilley, Vic- toria Ward;: Mr. Alderman Gossage, Waterloo Ward; Mr. Alderman Timmis, West Bank Ward.</p>
        <p>THE TIME OP IMMO. Alderman Timmis moved and Councillor Smith seconded that the quarterly meetings of the Council be held during the current municipal year on the second Tuesday in the months of February, May and August at 3 p,m.—Councillor Gough moved an amendment that the meetings be held at 7 p.m.. Ho argued that it would be better for all concerned and would give the labour candidates at the municipal elections a better chance of being returned. It had been one of the objvc- tions-against them on the present occasion that they would not be able to attend to the duties, and if the meetings were held at Tp.m. this objection would be done away with. In Warrington and many othertowns the meetings were held in the evening.—Councillor O'Donnell seconded, and said there was no reason whlt the meetings should not be held in the evening as not a meeting during his term hadiasted over an hour. Wid- nes was a working population and they hoped some day to have labour representatives, and if the hour of-meet- ing was fixed at seven o'Clook, it would give gentlemen engaged in labour a better chance.—The amendMent was lost, only the mover and seconder voting in favour and the rest of the council against.—Alderman Titamis moved and Councillor Christie seconded that the monthly meetings be held at 3; p.m.—Councillors Gough and O'Donnell again proposed their amendment, whichwas defeated as before.</p>
        <p>APPOINT3IEN7 OF COMMITTEFS,</p>
        <p>—....-----, --, ---- ----- The several Committees were re-appointed without , • • change.. _</p>
        <p>THE MAYORAL VISIT TO CI:LURCH.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said he proposed to attend service at St. Paul'S Church on SUnday. morning, and he wonid be glad to meet at the Town Hall any members of the Coun- cil, the Library and Technical Instruction Committee, the magistrates, the chairman and members of the School Board, and the members of the Relief Committee and any other gentleman who wished to be present. (Hear, hear.) The offertory would be for the distress fund. (Hear, hear.) His Worship invited the members to take luncheon with him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NEWTON BOARD OF COHMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON BOARD OF COHMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held at the Newton Town Hall, on Monday afternoon. There were present Messrs. 3. W. Emmett (chair- man), T. Ormand, G. Ratcliffe, T. Forster, E. T. Evans, P. AlcMalion, P. Thompson, H. Collingwood, and T. Brierley (surveyor).—The chairman read a letter from the Law Clerk (Mr. Worsley) stating that he would not be present, owing to his be- ing at the funeral of his sister, Mrs. Harding, in Northamptonshire. Mr. Brierley would undertake his duties.—Mr. Forster thought that a representa- tive from Mr. Worsley ought to have been present. HIGHWAY COMMITTEE. -DOCTOR'S QUARTERLY REPORT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Highway Committee held on the 16th October, Dr. Watkins submitted hi 3 quarterly re- port, which gave the births during the quarter to be 121, deaths 91, excess of births over deaths 30. The births were equal to 36 01 per thousand, and the deaths 27.8 per thousand. 44 cases of infectieus diseases had been reported during the quarter. Mr. Gregory's report for the past month was read, and he was instructed to in- spect at once, and make a special report of the condition of the closets in Regent-st., Viaduct-st., and Wood-st., complained of by the medical officer. Mr. Brierley sub- mitted Mr. Thorp's letter of the 4th inst., re hospital charges, and it was ordered the same be referred to the Law Clerk as to liability. Tenders for the sewerin,g of Water-st , off Crow-lane, for Lord Newton, were read. Resolved that Mr. E. Bunting's tender of £220 be ac- cepted. Applications for the situation of hall keeper and attendant were gone through, and the number was reduced to the following :—Mr. and Mrs. R. Collier, Wargrave ; Mr. and Mrs.. J. Parry, 19, Sankey-st. • Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Sherratt. 61, Houghton-st. ; and Mr. and Mrs. M.. McCoy,. 24, King-st. Another meeting of the committee was held on October 22, when Mr. and Mrs. J. Parry were appointed to the position of hall keeper and attendant.—The minutes were passed on the motion of Mr. Ratcliffe, seconded by Mr. Ormand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GAS AND WATER COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gas and Water Committee held October 18, Mr. Brierley submitted a quotation from Messrs_ Woodhouse and Rawson VI 13s. for providing' a hand rail round the new gas holder. It was resolved that the same be deferred for the present. The gas ' manager's report was read, showing the present cost of coal lobs 8.9r1. per ton in excess of last year's prices, and he was ordered to keep a month's stock of cannel and coals beforehand. It was resolved that in conse- quence of the continued advance in the price of cannel and coals, the price of gas be advanced twopence per 1,000 feet from, the Tat November until further notice. It was further resolved that in consequence of the con- tinued damage to the street lamps, placards be issued offering a reward of £2 for information that would lead to a conviction of the offenders. The report of the Steam Users Society on the condition of No. 2 boiler at the. gas works was read and being repudiated by Mr. Swann, it, was resoNed that the society be informed that the remarks of the report were questioned. The carbonising account for the past month was reacl,giving fuel used to be 225 tons 6ewts. ; gas made 2,228,8001 C., being an average of 9,870 ft. per ton of fuel used.—The minutes were passed on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. E. T. Evans. In answer to Mr. Em- mett, Mr. Brierley said that the new gas holder was giv- ing satisfaction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee held on 16th October, Mr. Bait (school attendance officer) read his report with special reference to the decrease in numbers on the books in June last, which was adopted. Mr. Ball was requested to prepare a computed summary of total Chil- dren in the district of school-going age. The following quarterly report of total attendance at the schools for the quarter ending September, was given, total num- ber on books 2,580, corresponding quarter last year 2,555, average attendance 1,945, last year 2,071 ; per- centage 752-5, last year 81. It was resolved that the byelaws of the committee be altered to comply with the Elementary Education (School Attendance) Act, 1893, which comes into operation on Ist Jannary, 1894.—The minutes were moved by Mr. Ormand, seconded by Mr. P. Thompson, and passed.</p>
        <p>THE BEST NIGHTCAP.—IIave your sheets washed with Hudson's Soap, and they will always be sweet as roses, fresh es sea breezes. There will be none of the disagreeable smell, so objectionable in other soaps.—A pure Dry Soap in fhae powder.—Advt. (2).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TEE WIDNES-. RELIEF COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>TEE WIDNES-. RELIEF COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>. The weekly meeting of the Widnes Distress Re- lief Committee was held on Wednesday afternoon, in the Council Chamber of the Town Etat. The Catering Committee recommended the payment of accounts amounting to £261 195.51., and that 1,315 hot pots be distributed to-morrow (Sunday). The minutes were passed.. Mr. J. Alorrison,in moving the adoption of the Finance Committee's minutes, ex- pressedsiegret that there was a serious falling off in the subscriptions,, and their balance at the bank was now very small. Since, the commeneement, the sum of £1829,145. 18.. had been subscribed, and of this £1738 4s. 7d. had been spent, leaving a balance in the bank of £9l4s. 78. This was something like £77rless than last week, and the cause of this was an increase.of £33 in the expenditure,-and'a decrease in the subscriptions of £44. He had received in response to Mr. Wade Deacon's ap- peal in the Press, about £2O, and from the Chemical workers at Newcastle-on-Tyne £34 To. LI, the amount of last week's collection among the men in the employ of the Alkali Union there for the relief of the dis- tress among the chemical workers. in, Widnes and St. Helens. He (ber.Morrison) hadteen•Mr. Wilde, the manager of the St. 'Helens Works, and had arranged that £lO of this money should be given to St. Helens, and the remaining, £24 7s. id. to Widnes. He believed the subscriptions from Newcastle would be a weekly one. He had, also received £lO, the amount of a collection made at Farnworth Parish Church on Sunday last, and £7 the amount of a collection made in the Primitive) Methodist Chanel, Widnes. The Widnes Football Club had sent £5,, and had promised to play a match with Runcorn for the benefit of the fund on the 23rd inst. The club was also playing Runcrrn.at Runcsrn on Wed- nesday next, in aid of the distress in that town. The Widnes Cycle Club had also come to the assistance,of the committee by suggesting a lantern parade when a collection would be made for the fund. Mr. James Kiddie had again placed the Alexandra Theatre at the disposal of the Committee for another sacred concert', and Mr. Ohrny bad promised a benefit performance at his circus. Mr. W. P. Hartley had sent a furthez sub- scription of £lO towards the fund.</p>
        <p>Chang, the Chinese giant, has just died, at his resi- dence, Houthcote-road, Bournemouth. He 13ac.F been in poor health for some years,anct went to Bournemouth in the !gone of recovery.</p>
