        <p>LECTURE ON "ABRAHAM LINeOLN."</p>
        <p>The lecture season at the Viaduct Institute, Ear- lestown, was opened on Monday evening, by the Rev. J. W. Dawson, of London, who delivered an oration on-" Abraham Lincoln," 'to a very large audience. The Rev. E. R. Davies presided, and he was supported on the platform by the Revs. W. Graham, J. Camm, and T. Cottam, Messrs. J. W. Emmett, T. Ormand. C. W. Emmett, and Dr. Hayward. The Chairman briefly introduced the lecturer, and re- marked that they had a man there that night who could do justice to such a great theme as Abraham Lincoln, aad with a great theme and a great lecturer,they ought to have a grand evening.—The Rev. J. Dawson said the chairman spoke the truth when he said the theme was a great one, because Abraham Lincoln was a great man, and his life opened to them,much that was instructive to the making of America. It was to the wave for the consolidation of the Union, and the emancipation of the slaves that he had •to speak that night. Lincoln said with touching modesty "I do not claim to control events, but events control me. What was the nature of those events, which made the life and career of Lin- coln prosper, and the history of those movements,which had destroyed slavery in civilised countries. Everywhere there was growing up in America, that greac Upas tree of slavery. Slavery did not merely mean the degrada- tion of a black race, it meant another thing, it meant the equal degradation of a white race. He referred to the efforts of John Brown, a backwoodsman, who armed with a text of scripture and surrounded by a handful of men, dared to draw the sword for his nation, and at- tempted to free the slaves. An enthusiast and a fana- tic, but with a fanaticism that inspired Paul. At a moment when this juggernaut of slavery was rolling on, suddenly there arose up this one man, who believed in God and nothing else, and John Brown struck the blow. They all knew bow the Southern states outbid each other id ignoble rivalry as to which should provide the halter to hang him with. When he was led out t die, a poor slave mother with a little child drew nigh, and amidst the jeers of the crowd, he stooped to kiss the slave's child. (Hear, hear.) It was in December of 1859 when John Brown was hung' because he hated slavery. In the following year Abraham Lincoln was elected President of the United States, as an avowed abolitionist—and in 1861 the first blow was struck of that great civil • war, whicn swept away from America, the great evil of slavery. Abraham Liwzoln Avis the noblest type of western man- hood that the world had ever known. His parents were not merely poor, they were helplessly poor, they were helplessly ignorant. He was a simple ragged lad, illused by his father and his mother. Suddenly a change came in his life, a new mother came, a woman of whom Lin • coin spoke to his dying day, as that angel, woman. Love could not break the bond of peverty, but it could light the fire of a pure ambition in the heart of the boy, and it did. Eighteen miles a day he would walk to get the slightest smattering of education. He had never seen a church, and vet he Could be strong, moral, deep-hearted and patriotic. After tracing his early life, which was rugged and simple, he said that on the 6th November, 1860, Lincoln became President of the United States of America, being then 51 years of age, and his popularity was phenomenal. He was unknown, and a bitterly mis- judged man. Beneath his rough exterior was a genius, which had shaped the American life, and American his- tory. The lecturer then proceeded to the outbreak of the great civil war, and in 1862, the proclamation that from the first of January, all persons found in slavery in the States then in rebellion sii ould be then and thencefor- ward free, was issued. That was the grandest piece of legislation ever made by pen stroke. A strange, sad heavily burdened, quaintly humorous man, he began the war with 75,000 raw recruits and ended a million veteran soldiers. On the 15th April, 1865, he died, and they might surmise that he was not without some premoni- tion of his end. In four years his manhood had been tried by every test that man could be tried by ; he was mourned by millions, but not more than by that warm hearted race, which be had made for ever free. (Ap- plause.)—Dr. Hayward moved and the Rev. W. Graham seconded a vote of thanks to the lecturer, and a similar compliment was paid the Chairman on the motion of the Rev. J. Camm, seconded by the Rev. J. Macpherson.</p>
