      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>AN ASHTON TRADESMAN'S CLAIM.</head>
        <p>AN ASHTON TRADESMAN'S CLAIM.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED FAI.,-"E IMPRISONMENT. Mr. Mullholland, barrister, of Liverpool, (in: structed by Mr. Forshaw), prosecuted at the War- rington County Court, on Thursday in a claim by John Virood,coal dealer,and green grocer, 33,BoIton- road, Ashton-in-Makerfield, against Jos. Caldwell,' green grocer, Warrington, to recover damages for malicious prosetution, and false imprisonment. He claimed the return of a grey mare, his property, or also in the alternative damages for breach of warranty. The case was heard before His Honour Judge Ffoulkes and a jury. Mr. Mulholland said that this was a case in which the plaintiff complained of being arrested, clapped into prison, and charged with felony, a very serious thing to happen to any respectable man. When this did hap- pen, that man had a right to turn round and ask what , warrant there was for such a serious and terrible , charge being brought against him. That was a question to inquire into that day. The question of the dispute about the mare was an entirely subsidiary one as it was interwoven in the other case. On September 30Lii Wood came into Warrington at about 1 o'clock with his waggon drawn by a dapple grey horse. When he arrived at Warrington he met Joseph Caldwell who ap- peared to have taken a fancy to the horse. Caldwell said that he had a chestnut mare which he would "swop" for the dapple grey. It was finally agreed to "swop" the animals, Woods agreeing to passss. extra. Caldwell said that he would take the chestnut horse back if anything was the matter with it. When Wood was taking the animal home, ehe commenced kicking and lamed him, and he was finally so disgusted with the horse that he took it back to Caldwell. Caldwell, however, refused to give him satisfaction, and Wood had to be Content with leaving the horse for the night in a stable belonging to Mr. Richardson. Wood came next day to Warrington and again asked Mr. Caldwell to take the horse back, but he again refused absolutely. Wood thereupon told him that he would take the dapple grey out of the field and leave the chest- ' nut instead. Wood did as he had threatened on Mon- day morning_ On Tuesday evening, he received a letter from Mr. Browne' solicitor stating that if he did not give up the grey horse, his client (Mr. Caldwell) would apply to the magistrates for a warrant against him. Mr. Forshaw (Wood's solicitor) wrote in reply that he had a right to the horse and the case should be settled in the county court. Caldwell thereupon applied for lied obtained a warrant which was granted on his ex parte statement, and the plaintiff was locked up and brought before the magistrates the next morning. The case was discharged by the magistrates on the ground that it was a case for the County Court. There was nothing against which judges set their faces more than using criminal proceedings which were intended to suppress crime, for the purpose of enforcing a civil debt. There was noth- ing which juries so severely marked their displeasure of than the same thing. Before a man charged his neigh- bour with felony he should have reasonable cause for suspecting or believing that his neighbour had com- mitted a felony—John Wood was then called and corroborated Mr. Mulholland's statement. He said he was in the "Horse and Jockey." when the bargain was made, and he set off home with the chestnut horse. On crossing Longford bridge, the horse kicked him in the knee and knocked him to the ground. He was then sitting on the sha:ts. In Hulme the horse knocked him across the road several times. On Wednesday morning after he had taken the grey mare back again, Detective Monaghan and P.S. Williams came to him while he was at a neighbour's house and arrested him. He was taken to the Ashton police statien until night, and was then taken to War- rington in Caldwell's trap, the grey horse being also brought to Warrington. He was taken to the police station and kept there during the night. When before the magistrates next morning, Detective Monaghan, Wright, and Caldwell gave evidence against him, but he was discharged without having to say a word. He saw the grey horse the next morning but had never seen it since. He had had to pay Mr. Forshaw, his solicitor, 324. for his services and advice.—Mr. Browne's cross- examination of the plaintiff lasted an hour and a quarter. —Detective Monaghan said that he had not known of warrants being refused when applied for after the appli- cant had been sworn.—Other witnesses were called for the prosecution, after which the case was adjourned until December 7th. A BRAVE DRUMMER BOY. Another name, a Paris Correspondent says, is added to the list of boy heroes of thei French wars by the death of a drummer troy at the seige of Maubeuge a hundred years ago. The circumstances overlooked at the time, were brought to light by modern historians of the French Revolution. &amp;twill or Stroh, as it is also spelt, was a drummer of fourteen in the French Royal Swedish Re- giment. It was on October 15th, 1793. The army of the Convention had undertaken to raise the siege of Maubenge by Austrians, and Strauh's Regiment had been sent to occupy the village of Dourlers. Regardless of danger, the young drummer slipped through the lines of Austrian skirmishers by hedges and ditches, and reached the centre of the village, where he proceeded to perform on his drum a rattling call to arms. The enemy believing that the French had occupied the village, retreated in disorder. This enabled the French to get up to the first houses of the village. By this time the Austrians had discovered how they had been played with, and were in no burnout 6o for- give the author of their defeat. Straub ran for his life, but too late. Surrounded in front of the church by Hungarian Grenadiers befell, having himself shot down several of his assailants. A peasant hidden in a loft had witnessed the whole scene. Some years later in the army he met Strauh's brothers, who were aware that the drummer boy had been killed, but were ignorant of the circumstances of his death until the peasant told them. In 1337, in opening the ground in front of the Church of Douriera, a boy's skeleton was found among the bodies of seven Hungarian Grenadiers. Thus the peasant's story, which it appeared impasible to authen- ticate, received a confirmation.</p>
        <p>FIRE AT A ST. HELENS BOTTLE WO RIO. A YOUTH'S RECKLESSNESS. At St. Helens Police Court, on Thursday, before Alderman Harrison and Councillor Martin, a boy named Edward Hill, aged fourteen, of Reglan-st., who is employed by Messrs. Carrington, Shaw, and Co., at their Sherdley Bottle Works, was charged with setting fire to a store shed in the works, knd doing damage to the extent of £5. Mr. T. Garner (Messrs. J. 0. Swift and Co.), appeared to prosecute. Robert , Houghton, aged fourteen, who is also employed by the ' firm, stated that between nine o'clock and half-pact nine o'clock on Wednesday morning he went to the store- room for tallow. He there saw the prisoner, Ned Hill, and Aaron Jones. The boy Hill turned on the tap of a paraffin cask, and allowed it to run on a cask beneath. He then said to Jones, " Have you &amp; match ?" Jones replied, " No." He then fumbled in his own pocket and round a match, which he struck. Witness blew it out, remarking, "Are you going to blow us up ?" Prisoner then produced another match, which he struck, and when it had burnt half way he dropped it on the para- ffin. Flames sprang out, and witness ran to the lodge and said the storeroom was on fire. The hosepipe was immediately taken by men and laid on, and the fire was put out. Mr. David Chadwick, J.F., works manager, said he was called to the storeroom and found the door and doorposts, straw, and matting had been destroyed The damage was estimated at £5. The room contained tallow, paraffin, resin, Sze. There were four casks of oil. If the fire had not been checked promptly the consequ- ences would have been serious, as it would have blown part of the works up. Next the storeroom was a shop in which men and boys were working, and they would undoubtedly have been injured. The magis- trates imposed a fine of 10s. and ss. ed. costs and £3 damage, or fourteen days. They hoped it would be a lesson to lads. it the same time the bench thought such dangerous articles should be kept under better super- vision and that boys ought not to be allowed to go into such places. leir ST. HELENS TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>• The first meeting of the St. Helens Town Council was.held at noon on Thursday, at the St. Helens Town Hall. The flag was flying from the building in honour of the customary election of a chief magistrate. There was a large attendance of mem- bers. Councillor F. R. Dixon-Nuttall, deputy Niayor. was voted to the chair. The first business being the election of Mayor, Alderman M'Kechnie rose and said it was with very great pleasure—as it would give equal pleasure to every member it they had been called upon—to propose that Mr. Arthur Sinclair, J. P., he re-elected Mayor for the ensuing year. (Applause.) _councillor Green, who seconded the resolution, said Mr. Sinclair had during the past year conducted the arduous business of the Corporation in a satisfactory and admirable manner, and one in which he had gained all their esteem. (Applause.) The resolution was car- ried unanimously, and Mr. Sinclair was introduced by his proposer and seconder and again invested with the chain of office. The Mayor,after taking the eath and re-electing Coun- cillor Frederick Richard Dixon-Nuttall as Deputy Mayor, said he thanked the Council for again unani- mously electing him to the honourable office of Mayor, to which he was first elected twelve months since. He was very sorry. that he did not take office under bright auspices as last year. There was a cloud over the dis- trict, which had debarred him from exercising the hos- pitalities of the Town Hall—a cloud which he sincerely hoped would soon pass away. (Applause.) 'lhe election of committees was then proceeded with, and it was decided that the duties of the Burial Board and the oversight of the cemeteries slimed be taken over by the Parks Committee. The Watch Committee also take over the arrangements and appliances for the prevention of fire, the fire stations, and the firemen's cottages.</p>
        <p>TIIE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. NOVEMBER 11. 18'3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>AUSTRALIAN MOUNTEDS.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN MOUNTEDS.</p>
        <p>It is doubtful whether there is in the whole world,and that is putting it strongly, a finer body of men than the Australian Mounted Police, as they stand to-day, numbering something like twelve or thirteen hundred inclusive of a few score of aboriginal allies. Smart, thoroughly disciplined,first-class bushmen, and splendid horsemen, they are a force of which any country might be proud. The pay ranges from Bs. per diem for an ordinary constable, with light, fuel, and quarter (when practicable, and a stated allowance otherwise) to £325 per annum for an inspector. These figures, as does the uniform,differ,slightly in several colonies. The New South Wales troopers number about 646 ; and perhaps their uniform is as neat and workmanlike as any. It consists of a loose blue tunic with silver buttons, Bedford cord breeches, riding boots, and a cap peaked :and braided. The arms are a Martini-Henri carbine, sword ,and heavy Colt's six-chambered revolver. A couple of first-class weight carriers are as a rule, allowed to each man, but this depends greatly on circumstances. But the bush trooper appears in full array only at very special times indeed. Meeting him shogging along in much worn tweeds, slouched felt hat, leggings, and rusty spurs,you would unless your eye happened to catch the ominous bulge of the holster beneath the short coat, or you hear the slight jingle of handcuffs at the belt, take him for a simple traveller. Even in the towns he is, from the very nature of his duties, as often in mufti as in uniform. And when he is after cattle-duffers, horse-sweaters, or any other disturbers of his somewhat monotonous existence, he gets himself up in the most curious disguises' the better to enable him 0 mix with station bands, ikc., and obtain hints and information which would certainly be with- held were his real character guessed. Often a trooper stationed at a township is also clerk of the Petty Ses- sion, Inspector of Weights and Measures and Slaughter houses, Registrar of Births and Deaths, Affidavits, Dog Licenses, and holder of half a dozen other honorary offices. Presently, perhaps, he gets maimed, buys land, builds houses, owns horses and cattle, and is generally, if a good man, left alone by the authorities. He has too many vested interests to care about promoti An and is indifferent to it. When a man settles down in this man- ner his career is practically over, and he is often al- allowed to peaceably work out his thirty years' service, entitling him to retire on full pay. But this sort of thing does not suit the eager and ambitious, who want at at least a sub-inspectorshiP. There are twenty-five of these, and promotion through the grades of sergeant and senior sergeant is slow. As in other services, in- fluence is an important fact•r. Barring this, the next best thing is to kill a man—a Bushranger, if possible. Some of the younger men are at first, therefore, a thought no free with their firearms. But they soon steady down. All through, the recruiting has been mainly done from the Bush, and the supply is for greater than the demand. The aspirant must be sober, sound, able to sit anything that looks through a bridle, read and write fail ly, and know the general principles of the first , "four rules." After undergoing a course of instruction mimmapnrda cstuiffie aci eynat nr at ei nr eesdt i in he e is, f which he eca leaves ncoh drafted off to a large inland town. Otherwise he is most likely sent to some unheard of collection of shanties right away on the border of the Never Never country, whose inhabitants have applied for " police protection." Here, however, he is, within reasonable limits, his own master, at liberty either to settle down resignedly to the dull bent of his duty, make a splash among the wild lads of the district, and so try for promotion, or blow his brains out from melancholia and bad rum. University honour men and younger sons, the latter especially, very often find a refuge in the mounteds," and Trooper Brown, whom y.O see there digging in his garden at Croctjingolong Barracks, with a wife and crowd of children around him, could, if ho so pleased,tell you some curious old-time memoirs when his haunts were Pall Mall clubs and his name was familar in the ranks of the Household Brigade. There was Robinson, too—the chap who's new at Tallegalla, "way hack" on the Maranoa. He was on duty at Government House when the Duke of and the Russian Prince—recognised and called to him ; only they didn't say Robinson. But he clapped spurs to his horse and galloped off, and very nearly got broke over it. Nor would he ever take orderly duty again. So they sent him to the worst station in the colony.— "Globe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FEARFUL OUTRAGE AT A THEATRE.</head>
        <p>FEARFUL OUTRAGE AT A THEATRE.</p>
        <p>A frightful outrage, by which at least twenty-three perssais have lost their lives, was perpetrated in the Liceo Theatre at Barcelona on Tuesday night. The house was filled, a favourite opera being announced. Suddenly a bomb was thrown from one of the galleries into the stalls, where it exploded, spreading death around. The explosion caused a terrible panic, and in the rush for the doors several lives were lost. The dead numbered twenty-three, and the injured were very numerous. The outrage is probably one of the " reprisals " which the Anarchists threatened when one of their leaders was executed for the attempt to murder Marshal Martinez Campos.</p>
      </div>
