      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>W.H.—The Conservative Club,Sankey-street,was opened by Sir Richard Cross, M.P.,—now Lord Cross—on Wednesday, October 15th, 1884. " JEOPARDIZED."—Your letter is too full of detail ; and, besides, it is closely written on both sides of the paper, which is very inconvenient and contrary to rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>t etters to the Obitor.</head>
        <p>t etters to the Obitor.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR H. ROBERTS AND THE BEER</p>
        <p>TRADE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Emerson's hero is the man who will "take both reputation and life in hand and with perfect urbanity dare the gibbet and the mob by the absolute truth of his speech and rectitude of his behaviour." If that is the conception of Emeison,then the citizens of Warring- ton ought to be thankful (and the worthy Councillor can be congratulated) that they have at least one man who bas the .courage of his convictions.</p>
        <p>For those who were onlookers at the recent meeting of the Town Council where Publican Ashton wai lifted up to the Aldermanic bench, must have seea that the struggle which there took place was hltween prin- ciples of rightmugness and the demoralizing forces of the drink trade. Drink won,but not without a vigorous protest from one who was there. For about 20 years the virtues of the newly-elected /alderman seemed to have b‘len forgotten by his friends, but at last he has been rewarded for voting as he has been told. How long! 0 ye citizens of this "good old old town of Warrington" are ye going to let the beer barrel roll over your rights? Be thankful that you can breathe.—l am, yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A CITIZEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>STREET MAKING.</head>
        <p>STREET MAKING.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reading your report of the Town Council meeting, I see that Councillor Plumpton has been calling the attention of the Paving and Sewerage Committee to the bad state of Monks-street and Goulden-street, off Lovely-lane. He says the houses are damp and the plaster is falling off the wails. He says some of the property owners are anxious to have them paved. As I am one of them, lean tell him I am not anxious to have the street done, because I am not in a position to pay for it, as the houses are not clear to me, nor won't be for some time; 84 if the strrat is made, I shall have to do what many a workingman has had to do, after striving for years to get a little property to help him when he gets in years, I shall have to sell them, just when I should be receiving a benefit from them. As regards the condition of the streets, there is a good tile parapet on either side of them, and there is no road through them, so there cannot be much traffic in them, to make them in such a state as Councillor Plumpton represents them to be in. But, perhaps, Councillor Plumpton meats that the streets should be done at the town's expense, the same as Crosfie!d-street, Arpley-street, Hall. street, Howley-lane, and Oliver-street, and the owners of the houses in Monks-street and Goulden-sti eet have .as much right to have the streets made for them, as the owners of the houses in the streets I have mentioned, and anything that is for the good of the town, the town ought to pay for it. ONE WHO WILL HAVE TO PAY. Warrington, Nov. 9, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>DRINK TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>THE DRINK TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Schofield has evidently mistaken the re- marks of my letter. I did not seek information as to the constitution of the Town Council or any other chamber. Brewers must look after their interests as well as any other business men. Excepting the Watch Committee, Ido not really see why the various cam- mitten must be exclusively composed of persons not engaged in the sale of intoxicating liquors. Of course, if these ,publicans had a majority on any sommittee they might abuse its privileges for their own benefit. They might resolve that Corporation employee should be paid in beer, instead of money; that licenses he applied for, for the .ale of drink in the park, museum and reading room, &amp;c : that the Councillors be supplied with liquors at any "hell-den "in the borough, the cost to be defrayed by the Corporation. It is said that to persecute any cause tends to naake it thrive. It looks like the same thing in the drink business. No man. abstainer or otherwise, seems to have any idea as to an antidote to the misery that is caused by the abuse of alcohol. The main argument is this : beer must not he brewed. What will be the consequeuee if this line of policy is carried Out? Ns one knows. There is no suc- cessful rival to beer.in the matter of temperance drinks. The beerite cries "I can't drink such stuff as lemonade, lime juice, or ginger beer. Give me something with body in it." There are two music halls in a city not far from here. The Frivolity is stuffy, soon filled with tobacco smoke, and is inferior in every respect. It pays well, and is filled nightly. On the other hand the Tivolity was erected at great cost, is splendidly venti- lated, engages more expensive talent, and is in every way superior to its rival. Yet it is not paying its way; people will not go. The Frivolity has a drink license, the Tivolity has nat.—Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NEUTER.</head>
        <p>NEUTER.</p>
        <p>THE ELECTION OF ALDERMAN.—LETT hilt FROM COUNCILLOR ROBERTS. Sir,—The Town Clerk stated on Thursday last that the "question of the election of alderman could not be dis- cussed," and that "this had been the custom for the past 10 years." I challenged the statement at the time, and am now able to give it a flat contradiction. The Town Clerk's memory is evidently unreliable and if he will refer to the file of the Examiner for July 1889 (4 years ago, not 10) he will find a Town Council debate reported, in which the following members of the Council took part— Alderman Crosfield, Councillors F. A. Robert-, Platt, Bolton, Marsh, and Mmnks,and upon which occasion the Tories, not then realising the virtue of long service, elected Alderman Francomb over the head of the late Mr. John Greening. Again in the same year (1889) in November, at the triennial election of aldermen, another discussion occurred, which is reported at length and in which Messrs. Monks, Platt, Bolton, Wright, Harrison, Greenall (the present Mayor) J. S. Green, and myself tiok part, and when the Tories for the third time passed over the senior member, the late Mr. Jno. Greening. On both these occasions the discussion occurred prior to the vote being taken, and in both instances the Town Clerk was appealed to and he stated that a discussion was These are by n 9 means the only occasions on which discussion has taken place upon the election of aldermen, and, therefore, custom has established what the law car- tainly does not forbid, viz., the right of the members of a Town Council to discuss even the election of an alder- man.</p>
        <p>My only ree,:, he Mayor did not commit a technical assat,lt iporll, 9 by calling in the aid of the police for my forcible ejectment, Is I should then have had a chance of ascertaining in a court of law the value of the Town Clerk's ruling. I have striven to maintain the right of "freedom of speech "—a right which has l'een dearly bought—and, as far as lies in my power, I will protect that right at any risk and at any cost.— Yours truly,</p>
        <p>11. ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT EARTHQUAKE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you allow me to ask the help of your readers in obtaining materials for a memoir on the re- cent earthquake felt in Wales and the West of England on Nov. 2nd.</p>
        <p>My object in this memoir is to trace as accurately as possible the boundary of the area over which the shock was felt, or the accompanying sound heard, and to draw lines through all places at which the shock was of approximately the same intensity. It would be of great service to know simply the names cf as many places as possible where the stock was felt, or the accompanying sound heard. Still more useful would it be to have answers to any of the questions printed below, especially to those numbered 2,3, 5, and 6. I shall be most glad and thankful to receive accounts from any places whatever, especially from places in the neighbourhcod of Warrington, Run- , corn, itc., and I may add that noexact account, however scanty the information given, can fail to possess same value or to help in throwing light on the nature and origin of the shock. 17.—Name of the place where the earthquake was observed. 2.—Time at which it was felt, if possible to the near- est minute. 3.—Nature 0 the shock: (a) Were two distinct shocks felt, separated by an interval of a few seconds? (b) lf so, which was the stronger? Kc) What was the duration of each, and of the interval between them ? During this interval was (a) any tremulous tnotisin felt, or (e) the rumbling sound heard ? 4.—How many seconds did the shock last, not includ- ing the accompanying Sound? s.—Was the shock strong. enough (a) to make doorsi, windows, fire irons, crockery, kc., rattle ; (b) to cause the chair, &amp;c., on which the observer was resting to be perceptibly raised or moved ; (e) to make chandeliers, pictures, Ix., swing, or to stop clocks? 6.--(a) Was the shock accompanied by any unusual rumbling sound ; and, if so, what did it resemble? (b) Did the beginning of the sound precede, coincide with, or follow, the beginning of the shock and by how many seconds ? (c) Did the end of the sound precede, coincide with, or follow, the end of the shock, and by how many, seconds 2—Yours obediently CHARLES DAVISON. .373, Gillott-road, Eirmingham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>r</p>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The Federation's conference for the settlement of the wage question has so far failed to bring about a settlement, and things are going from bad to worse. Destitution, starvation, and beggary, with all the atten- dant demoralizing effects, are bringinff commercial and financial ruin on people outside the coal trade, who, be- fore this disastrous strike,were comparatively well to do, ' but who are now within a measurable distance of poverty and want, and who are so circumstanced that they will never recover their former position. Small tradespeople who were just able to keep their heads above water can do so no longer, and frugal, thrifty folks, who by strict economy have managed to save a little against a rainy day or time of sickness, have to fall back upon their little hoard. Nat only are they obliged to fall back on the savings of former days but are re- quested to share it with men on strike, whose wages have been far in excess of those who are idle from neces- sity and not from choice. In conversation with a large colliery owner to-day he showed roe that his colliers through the whole of this year, with the exception of June, had made full time and had earned between £2 and £3 a week. We hear of a "living wage." What is a living wage? Opinions may differ as to the amount. What one man may consider a "living wage" another may call starvation, and would declare his de- termination to eat grass sooner than accept it. " The living wage" is an unknown quantity; a problem diffi- cult to solve. Is the public-house a factor in the " liv- ' ing wage " ? I have lived respectably on 345. a week, paying my way, dressing respectably, and keeping my family in comfort and credit ; but no public-house, no handicap, running dogs, or horse racing. These amuse- ments are beyond my reach, past, present, and future, as they should have no place in the category of working men's amusements and recreations; they absorb the "living wage.' It has been the practice during this dispute for the Press, some portion of it at least, and the M.P. to pat the "poor collier" on the back, and lay the whole of the blame of this strike on the shoulders of the coal owner. ',with many others,think this scarcely fair, but believe that the blame should be fairly apportioned. The miner says, "No surrender" ; nothing less—charity, e beggary, soup, but "No surrender " ; the late rate is the" minimum wage," no reduction, " grass first." The coal owner says, "I will show you by my books,that I cannot carry on my colliery except at a reduction in wages and a curtailment of expenses all round." This should satisfy Mr. Sam Woods who wants an inquiry by Parliament into colliery owners' profits ; their books will show income and expenditure, profits and losses. A large coal consumer in Manchester, who was last week paying Ils.per ton for coal,but in anticipation of a settle- ment of the dispute would offer only ss. 6d. per ton for the same coal. Be eventually moved up to 63.,pleading that resumption of work would bring down coal to the level of prices before the strike. This is how a portion of the public support the collier by beating down prices, thereby crushing colliery owner and collier in common ruin. The Press may flatter and cajole, and the M.P. pat the collier on the back and tell him he is a very good fellow, because he wants his vote ;:but let the whole truth be told, and a public investigation of colliery books be made by impartial and responsible authority, that the public may be satisfigd as to the right and the wrong of this terrible affair.—l am, sir, yours respect- fully, CYMRO. "HOME RULE AND THE PROTESTANT MINORITY."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, Mr. Foulkes of Frodsham, as is his wont. is again most offensive and garrulous. He stigmatizes my tine history of the present state of Catholic nations regarding toleration as untruths with- out even taking the trouble to prove them so. Then he goes on with a tirade against the Catholics of different nations, supplying only confused and haphazard testi- mony. Now, sir, will this gentleman, who has the audacity to call me bigot, prove that I ever, without very grave cause, attacked or ridiculed the Protestant faith. I have never in my life written anything, or said anything against the Protestant religion, only when my own religion was ;Marled in the most scandalous way by such Orange fanatics as this Mr. Foulkes, who is principally known as a disturber of Easter Vestry meet- ings. Now as to religious persecution, one would have thought that the memory of such a man as the late Cardinal Archbishop of Westminster, Henry Edward Manning, would have at least demanded the respect of every Christian who was not imbued with such anti- Christian feeling as usually obtains with the followersi of the Dutch profligate, William Prince of Orange. As to the charge of intolerance brought against Cardinal Manning, let us ask ourselves this question : Did Christ and his Apostles leave it free for men to decide, by their private judgment, whether they would receive or reject the doctrines they taught P No ! emphatically no ! He who would not. believe is menaced with eternal damna- tion--St. Mark xvi. What Hi 3 Eminence meant to con- vey was that there was only one true religion and for those latitudinarians who would put all sects on an equal footing with that one faith and one true religion ordained and instituted by Christ himself, he the Cardinal preached against such an idea of .olerance as being opposed to Christ's teaching and the waching of Holy Writ. As to Mr. Foulkes' clap trap cries of " who did this and who did the other" and his parrot-like answer "the Papists,'" the Papists," all that need be said is that if I choose to follow this style I could enumerate in a very startling way on the other side. But it would serve no purpose, and, as a Catholic, I would wish to be at peace with my Protestant brothers. That both sides have per- secuted is doubtless true, and no good will be done by meditating on all those horrors of the past which are to be lamented. What, then, was my object in writing you on the subject of toleration ? Simply this, that as the Catholic Church never yet advocated religious persecu- tion, the fourth Lateran council notwithstanding, so also Catholic countries at the present time have given an object lesson to Protestant Powers and States which it would be well for the latter to imitate. Where, I ask any candid man, who is not afflicted with Roman night- mare—as is this bellicose Orangeman from Frodsham-- where do you find throughout the whole history of the German mission, in this country or abroad one instance like to that of the people of Belgium, a Catholic people el:losing a Protestant Prince to rule over them. Why, take recent events. See the outcry against the appoint- ment of Sir Stuart EMU as Lord Mayor of London, the appointment of the Marquis of Ripon as Viceroy of India, not to speak of the outcry of these Christian people against Catholic Emancipation. Why, sir, these religious people, who are usually designated Orange Tories, have at all times during their history opposed every act of justice done to our poor down trodden race, and is it from these tvrants,that we are to be dubbed intolerant? Where, I ask, is there evidence of Christian love in the acts of their accredited agents and their heroic spouters of the McNeil type, who during the Irish famine told their people that it was against the law of God to employ these idolators, and these " ministers of the Gospel," were directly responsible for driving many a poor Irish girl on the streets because in a Christian land every door and entrance to honest labour was closed against them because of their faith.—Ycurs,&amp;c., Warrington, Nov. 7, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>J. C. DALTON.</head>
        <p>J. C. DALTON.</p>
        <p>THE MINERS AND THEIR CRITIC. Sir,—John Peers last week, and a week or two before, had something to say about the lock-out and I cannot tell whether I have been more amused than mad at his observations; I have been both by turns. Can't quite tell what John is in politics: I take him to he about the most remarkable example of mixed politics that I ever came across. It is quite evident he likes 'em blended. But I did think you would have some sympathy for the poor miner, John, in the heroic struggle he is mak- ing against the combined forces of selfishness, wealth, arrogance, ignorance,and insolence. But you have not, John, and you have flopped down very considerably in my estimation, in consequence. And you prefer the tyranny of the aristocrat—the coalowner—to that of the Plebeian—the miner. But unfortunately. John, labour cannot have its innings, which you admit it is entitled to, without some degree of tyranny. It seems to be necessary in order to wake up those good easy men, of whom, John, you are so conspicuous an example. "A labour tyranny is not by any means an improbability." These are sour words, John, and though you utter them _ .</p>
        <p>, in alarm, I confess!, John, they afford me much comfort, quotations so as to make believe that he is a friend and I had almost given up in despair, John ; thinking that would-be guide and saviour of the workingmen of War- labour was never, no never, going to have an innings, rington. I think I need not trouble the intelligent but I thank thee, John' for teaching me those words, readers with any further comments on Cymro or Mr. "And they have selected a set of men" to "represent John Peers' letters as those documents speak for them- their ignorance and prejudices in Parliament." So they selves the thoughts underlying the minds of their writers have,John ; the old plan of allowing a member of Parlia- as altogeter one-sided. ment represent only his own ignorance and prejudice Now, kind readers, I would say a word or two as to doesn't work welt—for the toilers, and so you see they the different way the employers treat the question of are trying a different way,John. As to the secrets of the foreign competition. In every possible way our great lock-out between masters and men you say you are not railway companies handicap our home industries by qualified to judge. Quite so; but the information, John carrying foreign goods at cheaper rates than English was quite needless, I can assure you. But in spite of goods or farm produce, thus placing our farmers and this, you hold strong opinions as to the " folly and im- manufacturers at a great disadvantage even in the home morality" of the miners leaders. Well, there is nothing market. It is an undeniable fact that they will under- unusual in that, John ; there are plenty of men who do take to deliver goods from Chicago to ilanchesser not trouble to grasp the merits of a dispute, who never- cheaper than they will carry and deliver the same weight theless believe they are entitled to hold " strong of goods from Herefordshire to Manchester. Belgium opinions" on the question. And when the enalowners and German iron was