      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER .11, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER .11, 1893.</p>
        <p>But, Mr. Editor, I am disappointed with the tone of your correspondent's letters on the lock-out. This lock- out is a great incident in a great movement. I have ben amazed at the patience and the heroism displayed by the miners in the struggle. They are fighting my battle and the battle of every working-man in England. They are fighting for the right to live a decent existence; for a fuller recognition of the claims of labour, and the con- sumers ought to help them in this fight against great ' odds. It is alleged that "bolstering up prices is not in the interests of the community who have to pay the in- crease in the shape of higher coal prices." Therefore the man who gives the coal its value should be kept at starvation point! This may be the kind of morality and political economy that your correspondent revels in as he sits cosily in his arm-chair by the side of a warm fire these cold winter evenings; but as for me I will have none of it; I renounce it as an outrage on our humanity and as opposed to common sense and COMMON JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>their workpeople ; they turned them adrift and imported I some Sweee glass makers and set them up in a big building in the Borough of St. Helens converted into barracks for the occasion. They requisitioned English police, paid for by English ratepayers to protect foreign workingmen when they were doing the work the Eng,- lishmen should have done, and the Englishmen had to apply for parish relief supplied by the English ratepayers. That state of things lasted several months and cost a great deal of money to the ratepayers of St. Helens. The same firm are large coal proprietors and to day their colliers are idle through the lock-out, and people write to the papers and point out to the public that the colliers are helping foreign competition. Inasmuch as all this writing is intended to damage the colliers case before benevolent public I hope the public will look at the true side of the question, and if they do I have no doubt that, in spite of all the efforts that are made to the contrary, everybody will do their best to help the colliers in their struggle for right against might—Yours, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>- ALFRED BROUCHEMITE. Cosmo politan Hotel, Friars Green, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>IWNICIPAL ELECTIONS AND THE DRINK</head>
        <p>IWNICIPAL ELECTIONS AND THE DRINK</p>
        <p>INTEREST.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lf there be such another creature or influence as " Neuter " ("Examiner," Oct. 28th) in the universe, it must be the mysterious " Will-o'-the-Wisp " seen in the brewer's quagmire, glorying in such a fool's paradise about " one or even two glasses of beer doing a man no harm." " Neuter" ought never to have escaped from the mother of all mischief, the root of crimes,the spring of vices, the whirlwind of the brain, the tempest of the tongue, the ruin of the body, the wreck of chastity. He naturally confesses that " He cannot see why a brewer who supplies a demard (to ruin) should be hung,drawn. and quartered by a certain section of the community." But the state of crime in Warrington exposed, running he by line with" Neuter's" subtle "Will-o'-the Wisp," handcuffed by policemen and published to the gaze of the world by the Chief Constable (Mr. Hunt) in his annual report, are the saddest results of " Neu- ter's " so styled "no harm" tippling consequences. Oh, "Neuter, such false logic as that leads to the rein of tens of thousands of human bodies, and immortal souls from age to age. " Neuter's " quack medicine utterly fails ; his brewer's passion never ! "Neuter," as can only become neutrality, has no word of sympathy for the victims of brewers and publicans. He coolly calls those, the pitiable prey of brewers and publicans, " gluttons "or he has a "depraved appetite." Although beset on the right hand and on the left with all the sub- tlety that only brewers and publicans can lead them into temptations—to fleece them of their hard-earned wages which ought to be spent on home and family. What does follow locally? Why while during the present year 530 persons were convicted for drunkenness only four publicans wero proceeded against, and only three convicted, and one beer- house-keeper proceeded againA, but not convicted. Let ":Neuter " prove the justice of the publican's free- dom to cause his victims to be fined or imprisoned while he goes unpunished though guilty of breaking the law in making them drunk. It is the pitiable dupe of a brewer who is hampered, and not the rich brewers or their allies with whom I" Neuter " is so much in love. Drink causes one in every fifteen persons in this country to die in the Workhouse. The coal war is an angel of mercy compared to the endless famine, the ceaseless moral pestilence and everlasting inhuman war waged by the brewers, publicans, and all their allies. . .</p>
        <p>"Neuter ;,` says "'shat a cry there would be if the drink trade forced its principles down the throats of the workmen in the same way as the various 'Temperance Associations force their theories, what a cry there would be! Can " Neuter" disprove the fact that generally less wages are paid to the brewers hands in lieu of the drink offered. I have worked where such has been the deplorable fact, yet I could not be induced to taste the poison that was offered, under which influence my kindred generally rolled home more or less under the baleful effects of drink. Talk of forcing it down their throats, only look at tho inflamed features of most of the brewery workers. No class of workers present such sure signs of having the poisonous cup forced down their throats. None but " Neuter's " eyes can fail to see that it must be infinitely better that moral and sober example should be resortsd to in the future, as in the past, by Temperance Federa- tions for the good of humanity. Over 300,000 persons are apprehended annually for drunkenness awl not less than ten times that number get drunk. Not a few, comparatively speaking, of them are in the so-called , upper grade of society, and educated. This education often fails to prevent their fall from the highest to the lowest station of society. Education fails in thou- sands of cases to prevent the fall of those who, like " Neuter" worship the one or even two " neutral " glasses of poison which charms but to destroy body and soul in one united mass of ruin. When " Neuter " writes again, if he wishes me again to oblige him, I cannot do so until he shows me that be is not an interested party, by signing his correct name. The so-called neutrals in religion, politics, drink traffic. are the merest dupes and most monstrous creatures of delusion on the face of the earth. Too many of such have come under my notice during the municipal elec- tions. They said "It did not depend on their vote, there were plenty to vote without them voting and bothering themsel ies " ; one, or even two, were of no consequence. Hundreds of ones or two's thus deluded themselves, and slept in a fool's paradise, while the wily publicans and the Tories united hands, hearts, heads and purses to carry on to more desperate lengths the co.nmercial, social, and moral ruin in our midst. Each voter in favour of the drink traffic, and each voter who withheld his vote are guilty of setting in motion influences for evil which cannot cease while the wheels of the universe move, and the God of the universe rewards each person according to his works.—Faithfully yours, R. STOIEY. November 3, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>COAL CRISIS.-RAILWAY FREIGHTS AND FOREIGN COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.-RAILWAY FREIGHTS AND FOREIGN COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Some people who do their beat to champion the coal masters' cause, through your paper, would do better if they put forth arguments which would not confute ' each other. Cymro tells us that coals cost ss. per ton at Swansea and 6s. per ton to get to Manchester or Oldham by rail, thus giving the London and North-Western , Railway Company one shilling per ton more to carry coals than the same coals cost at the pit mouth, or 55 per cent, of the entire sale price. lam thankful to Cymro for his information as he is undoubtedly to my mind a master's friend and agent if not actually a master. We , must take it for granted he knows what be is talking ,1 about when quoting prices and freights. 1 The coal industry, the iron and cotton and many other industries are entirely dependent on and mostly ruled by the railway freights, and in the case of the present crisis it was entirely caused by the freights being so increased that the masters, powerless against the rail- way companies are trying to take from the men the 25 per cent, the railways have put on the freights of coal. The privilege to vary and augment the freights was given to the railway companies by the last Unionist Govern- ment. Thus by law protecting the dividends of capital invested in railway stocks. Now, my dear Cymro, would it not be as logical and more human and generous for the present Liberal Government to protect the price of one collier's labour as it was for the late Unionist Government to protect the railway stock holders. The simplest and most equitable way to settle the dispute is 1 for the State to assert its lawful rights to all minerals, to pay the owners of the pits the value of all their plant and implements, and nothing for the ungot coals as that appertains to the Crown ; then to work the mines for the nation; to buy up the railways and canals, as also the tdocks and harbours, and work them for the nation. This is not an idle dream, but is fast becoming a necessity if England is to be allowed to carry on her commerce one need not be a Socialist to be struck with the thought that something must be done in the immediate future to prevent the possibility of a few unscrupulous speculators (to use a mild expression) starving a nation of hard- working honest men and women, arid through the cruelty, reducing them to want and starvation, sow the seeds of consumption in the rising generation. The efforts of Mr. John Peers are also directed, like those of Cymro, to do his best through tye Press to stay the merciful hand of charity or brotherly love, and help, by slandering the colliers and their leaders, and as far as possible, to mix up .statements and. garble facts and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The game between Warrington and Manchester Ran- gers on Saturday was a decidedly one sided one, and in consequence of this was far from being interesting. The Warrington team was made up chiefly of A team men, and as their form was not particularly bril- liant, it is pretty evident that the Rangers have fallen upon evil times, for three seasons ago they would have swept before them such a combination as opposed them on Saturday. Warrington were without the assistance of James Bate, Nevins, Potts, Webster, and Turner.</p>
        <p>However, if the game was poor on the part of the Rangers, it had the merit of conferring upon the War- rington A team some considerable amount of honour, for cannot they claim, with half the team made up from their men, to have played a great part in the defeat. Indeed the full back, Edleston, was a recruit from their ranks. He showed good form too—considering the little chance he had of doing any work. Evans, Carey, and Heesom all played well. But really if they hadn't done so against such poor opposition, I should never any more have commemorated their victories, by clanging together a " spanner " and a salmon can, and ejaculating with unctuous flavour "good old Warrington."</p>
        <p>Nevins played well in the championship match between Cheshire and Lancashire on Saturday. But he wasn't allowed many chances to show what he is cap- able of. There was a largo assortment of Warrington- ians at one end of the field, and it was quits delightful to observe the animation and excitement they displayed when Nevins even ran the distance of his shadow.</p>
        <p>The " A " team made their appearance at Warrington after an absence of several weeks, and had they taken all the chances they had during the game they would almost have " smothered " the Manchester Rangers. The Rangers have a lot to learn in football, and I rather fancy that St. Mary's, the Warrington Rangers, the Parish Church or several other local teams would satis- factorily account for them. Goodness knows what the "Champion A" of last year and also the preceding year would have dote with them. It is no use denying the fact that this year, the A team is not quite as strong as they have been in preceding years, but for all that, they were the complete masters of the Rangers, not even al- lowing them the poor satisfaction of scoring a minor. But just fancy Warrington scoring seven minors! This shows a considerable amount of carelessness. The War- rington team missed a chance of putting on a tremend- ous score which they will probably not have again for some time. However 17 points to nil is not a bad score. The white robed player for the Rangers caused a vast amount of amusement by his fussy play. He did a great deal of work, but he did not help his side to a very great extent. He will perhaps hide his identity a little more with a large jersey after the " chaffing " he received on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Westmorland tried conclusions at Kendal on Thursday. There were three changes in the Lancashire team. Jolly, represented " Smack " Bate, who is incapacitated from play owing to a sprained ankle. His substitute, however, played well, and made ati impression, so I am told upon the Counties Commit- teemen. Murray of Swinton, and Halliwell of Wizan, took the places of Ashworth and Jones. Lancashire won by a goal to a try. Good.</p>
        <p>The same day at Runcorn Cheshire met Cumberland in the championship match. The game resulted in a draw in two senses—it was a draw in its scoring results, and it was a" draw" in the way it brought spectators. Each team seored a try and two minors. The Cheshire team was different in two instances from the one which de- feated Lancashire on Saturday. Saville and Ashton at three-quarter being replaced by Warder (Runcorn) end J. Hutchinson (Birkenhead Park), while there was one chance in the composition of Cumberland, Blacklock's k Mary port) position as forwtrd being taken by J. Edwards (Seaton). The Cumberland Union introduced a new idea with their players, the backs having coloured distinguishing bands on their arms, and the forwards carrying numbers on their arms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ANTECEDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANTECEDENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Pemberton, the genial treasurer of the Warring- ton club, is the subject of the sketch this week. He entered the football world in 1877 with the Padgate Ex- celsior Cricket and Football Club, and played half- back, with Mr. Warren, the subject of our last sketch. The first match in which Mr. Pemberton took part was against the Warrington Grammar School. The club was successful, scoring something like 17 tries to nil, the only member of the team who failed to score being Mr. A. Bennett. For a few seasons the club had their head- quarters at Padgate, but as the membership increased and mestly from Warrington, the members decided in the year 1880 to remove their headquarters to Warring- ton. This caused a great loss to the club, as Mr. A. Bennett felt compelled to resign. At the general meet- ing of the members held at the White hart Hotel, San- key-street, on the 22nd June, 1880, the following officers were elected :—President, Walter Brierley ; captain, William Hough ;vice cap,ain, T. Pemberton ; treasurer, Char Yes Leigh ; secretary,T. Pemberton. The new com- mittee decided to get a field in Slutcher's-lane, and charge an admission fee of one penny. The members were very enthusiastic, and the people began to recognise the new club which had sprung up. They won nearly all their matches at homer and away. The players did not consider "Jack Frost," and were just as willing to play on ice as on a carpet. On one occasion when en- gaged in a match at Widnes, Turner missed his train, but this did not prey. nt a playing, as he ran to Widnes, and took p.at in the m;</p>
        <p>In the season of 1881-2 the Padgate club amalgamated with the Warrington club and they rented a ground in Sankey-st. Mr. Pemberton was elected treasurer. The public did not seem to appreciate the efforts of the mem- bers at the commencement, but by dint of hard work the club finished the season very well, financially and otherwise, having a balance in hand of £l4 53. 9-Id. Pi i'</p>
        <p>The following season the club established themsel• behind St. James Church,but they were not very succ fulfinanci ally. The Associ ati or ieople had started a club in Latchford,and the Wanderers Football Club were making a name for themselves. At he commencement of 1884-5 the Wanderers amalgamated with the Warrington Foot- ball Club. Mr. J. R. Calvert was appointed to take the post of secretary and he was uraparing in his efforts to make the club a success,and from that date the club has gone on progressing. In this swason the dub made a profit of £9O 10. 2d. The next season Warrington entered the West Lancashire Competition, and being successful in their matches the gals increased by leaps and bounds. Mr. Calvert resigned the post of secretary at the com- mencement of season 1886, owing to his time being fully occupied. and Mr. J. E. Warren again filled the breach. During the years Mr. Pemberton has been connected with the club the various committees have always been ready to help any charity or other deserving objects, having devoted at various times about £220 to the War- rington Infirmary, Warrington Ragged School, Ladies Charity, Charity Organization Society, Warrington Gymnasium, The Padgate Schools, Hamilton-at. Schools, Cradles' Heath Chainmakers, Bromsgrove Nailmakers, , the Widow and Orphans, caused by the explosion at ' Morfa Colliery, in South Wales &amp;e.., and the present dis- tress in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton can well claim to be a veteran athlete— tho' by no means an old one, although he is not now in the heyday of his youth. The work he is doing, and has done for years in connection with the club entitles him to a place in the front rank of football pioneers, few in number, to whom is due the credit of bringing the game up to the excellent condition it now is. What the club would be without its old friend and treasurer, T. Pemberton, I don't know, for although his earnest labours in connection with the " cause " have won him the esteem and gratitude of the club, he has one quality that wins him a place in every one's rega.:(l, and that is his genial disposition and kindly nature.</p>
        <p>OBTAINING RELIEF BY FALSE PRETENCES AT WIDNES..</p>
        <p>Michael Milligan of Nelson-street, was summoned to Widnes Petty Sessions on Thursda), to answer a charge of obtaining relief by false pretences. Mr. Wood, reliev- ing officer, prosecuted on bekalf of the Guardians. On the 23rd October the prisoner told Mr. Wood that he had no means of burying his wife who had died en the previous day and an order for coffin, burial fees and hearse was given to him. Subsequently Mr. Wood found that the defendant had ordered a more costly coffin and hearse, and that he had obtained £5 from the club of the Liverpool Silver and Copper Comprny,where he was employed, and £4 from the Blackburn Arms Club. The cost to the Guardians was £1 lls. 6d.—The defendant said be did not know he was entitled to the money when ho made the application. He would refund the Guadiana the money if they would allow him. Mr. Woods said the Guardians considered this a serious case andpressed for a conviction.—The defendant was sen- tenced to 21 days hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>During the week, which was the 44th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :—Births : Warrington, 46 ; Latchford, 1; t0ta1,47. Deaths : Warrington,lB ; Latchford, 2; total 20. The excess of births over deaths was 27. ' The death-rate from all causes was equal to 18.8 per 1,000, against 20 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 35.6 the corresponding week ef last year. The death-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 6.6 per 1,000, against 34 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 8.6 the corresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were :-0 to 1,4; 1 to 5, 6 ; 5 to 15, 1 ; 15 to 25, 1 ; 25 to 60, 4 ;60 up 4 ; total, 20. The deaths included-1 from measles ; 1 from whooping cough ; 1 from scarlet fever; 1 from diphtheria ; 1 from diarrhcea ; 1 from enteric fever; 1 from acute rheumatism; 1 from phthisis ; 3 old age ; 3 from brain disease; 2 from lung disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week:— scarlet fever, 29 ; diphtheria, 1; enteric fever, 1. —JOHN H. 0011.11 ALL. MeOicel Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PE,NitETH.</head>
        <p>PE,NitETH.</p>
        <p>BAND OF 110 PE.—On Friday evening last week a public meeting of the Band of Hope was held in the Wesleyan schoolroom. A varied programme consisting of songs and recitations was gone through, and the Rev. J. Gunnell who presided also delivered an interesting address.</p>
        <p>" Our FAWKES " NIGHT.— nig once popular annual festival passed off without any special preparation, the local fair evidently taking all the atter tion. One or two small fires were lighted in different parts of the village. There was no fire in the park this year. The usual fire, however, blazed away at the Friends School on Monday nisht and drew a few villagers to enjoy the display of fireworks.</p>
        <p>DISTRESS FUND.—The continuance of the coal dispute iS beginning to make itself seriously felt in many families in the village. The persons mostly affected are the cap- tains and hands of the coal flats belonging to Messrs. R. Evans acid Co. With a view to alleviating the distress which is now prevailing, collections were taken amongst the Tanning Co.'s employ 63, who responded generously. A football match also took place on Saturday in aid of the Distress Fund when the sum of £l. 2s. 3d. was raised. We understand that a concert is also to be held for the same purpose shortly. THE CHORAL SOCIETY.—'I here was a fair attendance of members at the first meeting of this society on Monday night. It then transpired that Mr. John Newcombe had undertaken the office of conductor, and Miss Mary Standish has consented to be the accompanist. Mr. William Owen was unanimously elected honorary secre- tary in ths place of Mr. Clarke, he having resigned on account of business engagements. There is every pros- pect of some useful work being done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>LICHNSES.—At the Altrincham Petty Sessions, on Mon- day, before Messrs. R. H. Joynson, A. W. Mills, B. Allen, and H. Cawley, the transfer of an out-door beer license trom James Gregson, Lymm, to Thomas Thorn- ton Gregson, his son, was allowed.—William West, of Warburton, was granted a fireworks license. LOCAL BOARD.—A meeting of the above Board was held on Tuesday evening, when there were present— Messrs. James Gregson (chairman), H. Harrison, J. Holt, W. Brabazon, R. Whitelegge, A. Mclntyre, S. Appleton, Edwin Holt and J. Perrin, J. T. Ridgway (law clerk), William Mallard (clerk).—Dr. Fox reported that during the third quarter of the year, the number of deaths from all causes was 12, which was equal to an annual death rate of 9•6 per thousand He had much pleasure in being able to give a satisfactory report. Dr. Fox asked the Law Clerk whether if certain houses at the Rockery were demolished, the Board would have to pay compensation.—Mr.Ridgway thought that it would be better to let the owner have notice before any action was taken. He was of opinion that compensation could not be claimed by the property owner under the 32nd section of the Act.—Mr. E. Holt thought it would be a shame not to give compensation to ,the property owner. Dr. Fox asked the Chairman if he would have a resolution passed to the effect that tho owner be approached on the subject.—The Chairman stated that he was quite willing that this course be adopted.—Mr. E. Holt proposed, Mr. Whitelegge seconded, and the resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. SPEARING AND MISS BANKES AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. SPEARING AND MISS BANKES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A marriage in which more than ordinary interest was manifested, took place at Runcorn Parish Church at noon on Wednesday. The contracting part ies were Miss Amy Bankes, second daughter of the late Mr. J. J. B. Bankes, of Lewster Gardens and Mr. Wesley Spearing,confectioner,Ch urch-s, reet, third son of Mr. D. Spearing of Stockton Fording Bridge ; both of whom are widely known in the town, and there was a large congregation to witness the cere- mony. The bri,le who was given away by her uncle Mr. James Collier,of Weston,was attired in a travelling dress of shot sague cloth and wore a velvet picture hat to match, while she carried a beautiful shower bouquet of choice, white flowers. She was at tended by five brides- maids:—Mlss Bankes and Miss Eva Bankes (sisters) and Miss Spearing (sister of the bridegroom) and her two young nieces Miss Collier and Miss Lily Collier. The three first named wore" new shrimp brown" dresses and pink and white picture hats, and carried shower bouquets,the gift of the bridegroom. The Misses Collier were attired in heliotrope coloured costumes, and white picture hats, and carried baskets of flowers, and presented a very pretty appearance. Mr. Berkeley Spearing, brother of the bridegroom officiated as best man, and Mr. Edgar Spearing also acted as groomsman. The ceremony was performed by the Rev. Joseph Hold,m, and at the conclu- sion the happy couple were the recipients of numberless congratulations and good wishes. Subsequently break- fast.' was served at the residence of the bride's mother Mr. Bankes, Lewster Gardens. The brides cake which was from the establishment of the bridegroom, was in three tiers, surmounted by a spray of orange blossoms and jessamine, and was a splendid specimen of the con- fectionery art. The weight of the cake was nearly 50 lbs. Besides the bridal party the following guests were present :—Mr. and Mrs. William Bankes, Mr. Walter Spearing. Mr. Horace W. Handley, Mr. Clement Handley Mr. Gilbert Pritchard, Mr. Master, and Miss Shone, Mr. and Mrs. Grounds, Dr, MacKenzie, Mrs. Kirkham, Miss Kirkham, Mrs. Dobson, Miss Heaton, Mr.H. Rhodes, Mr. J. Bate, Mr. H. Annett, Mr. J. Collier, (Helshy). Miss Bowyer (Helsby) Miss Findlow (Preston Brook) Miss Kewley, and Miss Fardoe. In course of the afternoon Mr. and Mrs. Spearing departed amid a shower of rice and old slippers for London where the honeymoon will be spent. In the evening a dance was held. The fol- lowing is a list of the presents : Mrs. Banked (mother of the bride) American organ, lver teapot and timepiece; Miss Bankes, breakfaSt castor and sugar sifter ; Miss Eva Bankes, silver toast rack, brush, -rid comb bag, and nightdress case; Miss Spearing, 4.0 clock tea table ; Mr. Edgar Spearing, hand- some pedestal lamp ; Mr. and Mrs. James Collier (Wes. tn), cheque ; The Misses Collier, tea service ; Mr. J. T. Collier (Helsby) fire screen ; Miss Heaton, trinket set and mats to mateh ; Master and Miss Shone' case of carvers ; Mr. G. Smethurst Orme, dozen dessert knives ; Mr. and Mrs. Mason (Ithyl), toastrack and butter dish (combined) and jainj ; Mr. Horace W. Handley, silver hot water jug ; Mr. Clement Handley, breakfast cruet ; Mrs. Hutton and sons, pair of pictures ; Miss Bertha Hill, plush cushion ; Mr, T. Kirkham senr., handsome tea cosy ; Mr. and Mrs. Grounds, antimacassers, d'oyleys and butter cooler ; Mr. and Mrs. Bankes (High-st.), Ewbaull carpet sweeper ; Mrs. Speechley, dinner gong; Mr. G. Pritchard, timepiece; Mr. H. Rhodes, timepiece; Miss Rathbone, cheese dish ; Miss Findlow, ornamental card basket ; Mr. Peter Booth, jam dish; Miss Ida Kirkham, handsome cushion; Miss Bowyer (Helsby), glove and handkerchief satchet ; Two friends, hand satchel; Miss Armstrong (Weston), , brush and comb tray ; Mr. Caunt (Aberdare), pair of carvers ; Miss Cowap, wool antimacasser ; Mrs. Dobson, wool anti macasser ; Mr. Walter Spearing, silver biscuit barrell and butter cooler ; Mrs. Cornes, Wedgewood bis- cuit barrell ; Miss Simkin, cushion ; Miss Hamb'ett, ink- stand ; Mr. W. Spearing, counterpane; Mrs. Spearing, fire brasses and dinner service ; Miss Floxworth, afternoon tea cloth ; Mies Dutton, pickle jars ; Mr. Kirkham, tea cosy ; Mr. Fred K. Welsh, set of jugs ; Mrs. Mayers, tea pot • Mr. T. Spearing, cutlery ; Mrs. Potter, set of jugs; Mrs. Kewley, flower vase ; Dr. McKenzie, travelling rug antimacassar ; Mr. C. W. Collier (Stoke), trinket set ; Mr. P. Hockey (New Brigh- ton), trinket set ; Mr. F. Arthur Dunkley, dinner and tea serviettes.</p>
        <p>LAND NATIONALISATION SOCIETY.—We have received from the Land Nationalisation Society a Summarised re- port of their fourth van campaign. This shows that their van lecturers travelled during the past summer through seven counties in the north of England and in the south of Scotland. They visited all the chief tr,cvns and large villages, and report that 175 meetings were bold altogether, at all of which a resolution was carried, to the effect that the nationalisatien of the land and its control by representative local authorities are essential to the prosperity of the country and the just distribution of the national earnings. The Executive Committee ex- Feet to add a third van for their next year's work.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Haweis is a little man, with wonder- fully bushy whiskers of an inky black and a crop of hair that is remarkable. This deliverer of eccentric but mar- vellously fine sermons is passionately addicted to music and paintinz. Art in any form he worships, and his house is filled with artistic treasures. He is,says "Home Sweet Home," a great traveller, and when not at church work may be looked for in some delightful retreat on the Confluent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LATEST NE WS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NE WS.</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL CHARG'ES.</p>
        <p>We understand that the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany will next session promote a Bill in Parliament the chief object of which will be to fix the rates and charges on the canal, the president of the inquiry into canal tolls having decided that the company 'cannot be ex- empted from the regulations of the Act of 1888 (which imposes on the Board of Trade the duty of enforcing the schedules) unless Parliamentary sanction is specially obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MATABELE WAIL THE DESTRUCTION OF BULUWAYO.</head>
        <p>THE MATABELE WAIL THE DESTRUCTION OF BULUWAYO.</p>
        <p>The Cape Town correspondent of the " Daily Tele graph" gives the following description of the destruc-- tion of Buluwago :—The united columns practically surrounded the kraal and opened fire upon it with their seven pounders. The cluster of rudely formed struc tures which have served as the King'spalace, courtroom- and fortress were soon a burning mass of ruins. Skir- mishing parties were thrown out, and advancing under cover of the guns,fired into the ranks of Loben- gula's warriors. The main body of the troops shortly afterwards advanced :on three sides to follow up the work done by the machine guns and skirmishers, They assailed the Mata.bele :with great vigour, but the dusky warriors made a desperate defence. The com- bat was short, sharp,•and decisive. The Maxim guns, partially concealed from the enemy by thick scrub played on them with terrible effect. With splendid courage Lobengula's men rushed forward under the withering fire of the Maxims, seven pounders, and rifles, but they were unable for any length of time to withstand the storm. Cut down in scores they wavered and were soon in full retreat. Upwards of 2,000 men, the flower of Lobengula's army, perished, and the wounded number fully as many more. Among the dead are several in- dunas. Altheugh many of the King's best fighting men were armed with guns they failed to use them with any effect, but whether was from want of ammunition or lack of skill in handling their weapons it is impossible to say. One oh the most striking incidents in the storming of Bulu- wayo was the explosion of the powder magazine. It was a little clay hut with nothing to distinguish it in outward form from the other buildings in the vicinity of the King's house. A loud report was heard and at the same instant the air was filled some sparks, and fragments of the shattered structure and scores of human beings. After the collapse of their military storehouse the Matabele lost all heart. Nearly 1,000 of them were assembled round the building, and of these about one half lost their lives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>COAL CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>HEAVY LOSSES THROUGH THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at a meeting of the Nelson Town Coun- cil, the Mayor (Alderman Hartley) reported that by the purchase of coal sufficient for the manufacture of gas up to the end of the present month the Corporation had sustained a loss of £2,500 equal to the rate of 7d in the pound. The estimated profits from the gas undertaking for this year had more than disappeared. The Council decided that the Gas Committee should consider the advisability of advancing the price of gas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>REFUSAL OF NON-UNIONISTS TO PAY STRIKE LEVY.</head>
        <p>REFUSAL OF NON-UNIONISTS TO PAY STRIKE LEVY.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the miners, numbering 500, employed at the Astley pit, Dukinfield, came out on strike because the employers refused to compel, under a penalty of dismissal, eighteen non-union men to pay the special strike levy demanded from members by the Miners' Federation. The men have since passed a resolution not to resume work unless an advance in wages of thirty per cent, on the present rate is granted. This point however, may be waived if the employers will supply the men witl coal for home use at the prices which ruled 94 lion the strike commenced. Deputations have been appointed to wait on the men at Dunkirk, Hyde-lane, Broomstur, and Limehurst Collieries with a view of get- ting them to adopt a similar resolution which will be brought before a meeting of the Ashton lodge to-day (Saturday) for ratificatien.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>PENDLEBURY AND PENDLETON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>PENDLEBURY AND PENDLETON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>Mr.GreenalLagent,who addressed meetings at Whiston and Matto Heath yesterday, said the whole of the mlners in the Aahton and flaydock districts, which in- cludea Pendlebury and Pendleton, are, if anything, more determined than when the dispute commenced not to accept any reduction. It is one of the most important districts in the Federation, haying 35,0C3 members. The decision of Lancashire at the Conference on Monday, he was sure, would be a rejection of the employers' pro- posals. RESTARTING OF LEIGH PITS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Isherwood, J.P., miners' agent for the Leigh district, has received the gratifying intelligence- that arrangements have been made to restart the West Leigh Colliery Company's pits and the Brynn Hall pits. near Garswood, on Monday next, at the old rate of wages. Fifteen hundred men are employed at the pits. The news will cause intense satisfaction in Leigh and district, where at the present time there are 11,000 miners out of work. It is anticipated that the other pits in the Leigh neighbourhood will speedily follow suit.</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF M. lIAILWOOD OF RUNCORN•roc 4 The mortal remains of Mr. William Hailwood, of Runcorn, and Widnes, whose death was recordea% this journal on Saturday, were committed to the graleor Runcorn Cemetery, on Monday. The deceased %hone 9. menced business in Runcorn in 1854,had a most succ°, ful career. He was a Liberal in politics and inreligile Catholic. A kind hearted charitable man, the rto found in him a generous friend, and his benefactions St. Edward's Church, in which he took a deep were' to the were many. His death will he a severe 1083 Catholic cause in Runcorn. For some time Pas Hailwood had resided at Clare Terrace, Overhury.stre.ea e Edge Hill, Liverpool, and the funeral cortege en lealithe that place, proceeded to St. Anne's Church, Wh%eerhlds Requiem Mass was celebrated by the Revs. Father Davey, Almond, and Darby, in the presence of a congregation. At the close of the service the A' I Father Turner played the Dead March. On arriving 313 Runcorn Cemetery the body was met by the Rev. oeeettPo Schwarz S.J. who performed the burial ceremonS in rs r absence of Dr. Gastaldi who is confined to his ove with a severe cold. There were present Messrs. Cref'drj, Hailwood F. Hailwood, E. Imson, J. Fishwick ith Broadhurst. The coffin which was of polished oak, the. brass mountings, was covered with beautiful wte'tder The deceased gentleman was 65 years of age. We 115„,01 stand that Mr. Hailwood has left legacies to se—or Roman Catholic institutions in Liverpool and neighl)° hood.</p>
        <p>SALE OE WOFIK AT HALTON. joi With the object of raising funds towards the el-jac church proposed to be erected in Halton-road, ja of the present mission room, a sale of work wag:on- s menced in tha girls school Halton on Thursday after," ars Good work has been accomplished under existing stances but more ample accommodation is needo', a sum of £lOO has already been promised to wardlas project. A much larger sum is needed. There sv good attendance at the opening cermony which '3.sshi?lec formed by Lady Brooke. Her ladyship said tho of the sale of work was to raise funds for a as° larger Mission Room in Halton-road, the oorest "n P ;n t most remote part of the parish—remote that WO ' Th sense of its distance from the church and school' present Mission Room was established some four is ago, and had flourished in 2 most wonderful waytogiker to the unselfish and devoted efforts of the church in this parish, and to those of the Vicar and Mrs. r at their head. Such an object needs no words tore mend it, and it had already the sympathy of Ina,rifec those present, who have evinced their sympathY " operating in various very practical ways—some teilb log their time and work for many months past, 0„c/ltnei helping either in kind or with money, and to our most hearty thanks were due. Tnere was an or re, verb familiar to them all, " Well begun, well o,c'el'ci t That good beginning had been made,and she their generosity that afternoon with the utmost ence, for the means to enable the uudertaking , brought to a happy and successful conclusion. brought M. Baguley, proposed a vote of thanks to Lady PA arV for so kindly opening the sale which was seconde',oi carried by acclamation. The sale of work WO " tinued last evening.</p>
