      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. ALLEGED " PERSECUTION " AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. ALLEGED " PERSECUTION " AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY MAGISTERIAL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court, yesterday, before Messrs. Wm. Tyrer (chairman), and B. A. Drom- goole, another batch of prosecutions for non- attendance or irregular attendance of children at school was heard. In a cose against Edward Tay- lor, 127 Crossley-road, the defendant said that he was before the Bench a month age, when the magistrates dismissed the case, as he proved that the girl was over 13 years of age. Superintendent Dutton said that in the first instance proceedings were taken under the byelaws, as they believed the girl was not 13 years of age. but they were now proceeding under the Education Act with the view of getting an attendance order granted. The girl had only passed the third standard. Mr. Droomgoole J.P., said the case was dismissed on the last occasion because the girl was over the school age, and it was tins fair to come before them again in that persecuting fashion. Superintendent Dutton: We are bound to carry out the law. Mr. Dromgoole Das the case been before the attendance committee. Superintendent Dut- ton : Yes. She has left school, and she has only passed the third standard. Mr.Dromgoole : That is an unfortu- nate thing for the girl,but it is no reason why the parent should be persecuted. Superintendent Dutton: We don't call it persecution. Mr. Dromgoole : But I do. Supt. Dutton :We dcn't. It is a misnomer. Mr. Dromgeole : It is an impertinent remark for you to make. Supt. Duttnn : I don't think so. It is carrying out the law. Mr. Dromgoole : I think you are setting officialism above the law. Superintendent Dutton : I don't think so. Mr. Drorngoole : I think you have a great deal of impudence here. Superintendent Dutton : You may think so ; but lam only asking for the law to be carried out. Mr. Drerng,oole : I think that this matter has not been put' fairly before the committee,or they would not have sent it here again. Superintendent Dutton: I say that it has. I have their written authority here (holding up a paper). The defendant ssaid the case was dis- missed exactly a month age. Superintendent Dut- ton : I have already explained that the proceedings were taken then under the wrong form. _ Mr.</p>
        <p>Dromgoole : You mean that you made a blunder. You admit it. Superintendent Dutton: Yes. Mr. Dromgoole: Then for anything we know you are making a mistake now. Superintendent Dutton: It is for your worshipa to say whether we are or not. The Chairman said he thought it.was for the bench toinquire whether a mistake had been made. Superintendent Dutton said he had the authority of the committee to take the proceedings. The defendant said Mr. Dutton's daughter held a position as teacher at the school attended by his (defendant's) daughter, and he thought the officer was doing nothing but trying to harass him in making the girl attend school. Mr. Dromgoole (to Mr. Dutton): Is it true that your daughter has taken a position at this school? Superintendent Dutton: That is so. Mr. Dromgoole: If that is true we may also take it for granted that there is a little spite in the matter. The Chairman: We ought not to have remarks from the bench like that. Superintendent Dutton : Where should the spite come in ? Mr. Dromgoole : From his statement, which is admittedly true, we might assume . so. Superintendent Dutton: Rut I think your worship would not endorse a thing like that. Mr. Dromgoole : But if people come here and give a straightforward story to this court we are bound to pay some respect to them. Superintendent Dutton: And I hope you will also pay some respect to those who have to take proceedings.—Mr. Dromgoole With all due respect. I don't think it is a proper thing to press the case. Superintendent Dutton: If you say it is not the law, we will take your dictum. We are bound to do so. The defendant, in reply to the bench, said that he kept the girl at home to assist in minding the shop. Ile had an elder daughter who assisted at another shop, and there were three younzer children attending school. Ile had two shops. The Chairman said he thought that people who had two shops could afford to educate their children properly. The defendent : We intend the girl to go to a private school. The Bench ultimately adjourned the case for a month, and the Chairman said he hoped the defendant would send the girl to school in the meantime. Mr. Dromgoole Send her to some school : we don't as this school.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Hill, the senior partner in the well-known firm of organ builders (the oldest of existing English firms of the kind), has just died. He represented the sixth generation of Hills in the business since its founda- tion in 1755 by John &amp;loftier, whose traditions have been worthily carried on.</p>
